      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>CYCLING AND CYCLISTS.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND CYCLISTS.</p>
        <p>No. I.—CYCLING ABROAD. BY ROBERT L JEFFERSON, F,R.G.S., Author of "To Constantinople on a Bicycle," "Awheel to Moscow and back." "Across Siberia on a Bicycle," Sze.</p>
        <p>The mere title "Cycling Abroad" suggests a world of possibilities, since it has been proved again and again that the bicycle is ridden from end to end of the world, all round it, to divers remote parts, even from China to Peru. The cycling abroad, however, which is likely to be most interesting to the average Englishman is that which deals mainly with those</p>
        <p>countries which lie adjacent to our -shores, lay claim to some degree of civilisation, and are credited with possessing a full share of those creature comforts so dear to man. Therefore, while I might find more latitude for my pen in the cycling possibilities of, say, the Balkan peninsula, the Central Russian plain, the Steppes and Mountains of Siberia or the southern shores of Asia Minor, it is clear to me that the aforesaid average Englishman would vastly prefer the magnificent chausgies of Republican France, the valleys of the Switzer, the dusty roads of Italy, the homely countryside of Prussia, or even the some- what squalid towns and villages of middle Spain. It is not unnatural that •</p>
        <p>THE TOURING CYCLIST BENT ON WHIBILING ABROAD turns his first thoughts towards France. Well, that country is near, and a goodly proportion of English- men have a smattering of the French tongue;</p>
        <p>moreover, more people -tour in France than -any-</p>
        <p>where else— unless I except Switzerland and reports of its condition, its people, its customs, and its traditions are rife amongst us. To the cyclist France offers much: Good roads, excellent feeding, comfortable hotels, affable and hospitable people, low tariffs, and beautiful scenery! What snore can the wheelman want? To enter France is almost as easy as it is to pass from England to Ireland. The customs officers at all the points of debarkation on the northern coast have come to recognise the touring cyclist from the island beyond the Pas de Calais, and place as few obstacles in his way as possible. If the cyclist is a member of a recognised cycling club his machine passes through duty free, and the usual customs ceremony is more ceremony than anything else. Personally I, with the single exception of Germany, have found no country where I have had less trouble with officials than in France</p>
        <p>The passa4,-,,e of the Channel having been aecom-</p>
        <p>plished, we find ourselves at Calais, Boulogne, Dieppe, Havre, or St. Malo. At Calais we have the</p>
        <p>somewhat flat and uninteresting country to the south before us, until the French Ardennes are reached, and we are rewarded with some examples of woodland beauty; at Dieppe we have better choice, for the valley of the Seine is near, and through Rouen may make our way to Paris and the champagne country beyond; at Havre we have even</p>
        <p>greater scope, for from this port we find the whole wealth of Normandy nith its breezy uplands, its picturesque valleys and its charming peasants to delight us; and at St. Malo ancient Brittany appeals for our exploration. Whither to go must be the asclividual's choice, but, whichever way he goes, let me put it in plain print that he will find broad, hard roads, excellently engineered as far as the hills are concerned, splendidly marked with kilorn6tre storws, and at every bifurcation and in every town and village will be guide posts and distance indication 3. Where in the few remote places the Government has failed in its duty in these respects that most energetic body, the fouring Club de France, has supplied the deficiency I would advise every cyclist intending to tour in France to become a member of the Touring Club of that country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>ITS BADGE IS A PASSPORT</head>
        <p>ITS BADGE IS A PASSPORT</p>
        <p>to many privileges. The club issues a hand-book giving details of hotels and lists of repairers. By aid of this hand-book the tourist, even if he does not know the language, need never fear extortion. In France, too, the cyclist seems to have a better standing than he does in England, and his wants are more carefully attended to. It may be interpsting to know that the tourist can travel in the country and live well on less than 10s. a day, although in the largest towns this figure is slightly increased.</p>
        <p>The -roads of France are divided into two categories, Routes Nationale and Routes Depart- mentale. The former correspond to our terms "highway" or "turnpike," and the latter to by- roads. The tourist must stick to the National roads, which are under Government control. The depart- mental roads are not always good and are much afflicted with pane. One of the best roads I have ever been on is the Route Nationale No. 9, leading from Paris to Geneva, although the scenery of this road is rather tame. Another grand road is that from Paris to Belfort, passing through the magnificent champagne country rid Chateau Thierry, Epernay, Chalons and Neuchateau. Then again there is the historic road from Paris to Bordeaux through Orleans, Tours, and Poitiers, said to be the finest chayssie in the world. Further south the road from Lyons to Marseilles, where, the cyclist is corn- •'pensated for hill climbing' by grand scenery, the coast road through the Riviera by Toulon, Flyeres, Cannes, Nice, and Monaco is open to him. Ido not recommend this latter, however, in summer, for then the climate of Southern France is insufferably hot.</p>
        <p>I have dwelt somewhat lengthily upon France, believing, as I do, that it is the most popular of touring grounds abroad. Next, perhaps, comes Switzerland, the land of cantons and many tourists. Here we have</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>COSMOPOLITAN LITTLE COUNTRY'</head>
        <p>A COSMOPOLITAN LITTLE COUNTRY'</p>
        <p>to deal with: French on its west, German on its east, and Italian on its south. Living is much the same as in France, and, if anything, cheaper, except in those palatial hotels which the enormous American and English traffic has brought about. English is spoken almost everywhere, and one soon begins to regret ever having troubled about learning even a smattering of foreign tongues. As to roads, they are not so good as in France, and in summer are painfully dusty. Contrary to general ideas, however, they are not hilly; the roads as a rule run in the valleys and generally by the side of rivers and streams; the hill roads are the passes, and —well, they are hills, with this compensating advantage, that a ten miles climb means a ten miles coast. Of the scenery, what can I say that has not been said a thousand times before? On the other side of the glistening peaks of the Pennine and Lepontine Alps lies the land of the swarthy and black-eyed Italian. Here let the unsophisticated tourist beware. I am prone to admire the Italian, for never have I seen man beg so urbanely as he, nor have I seen</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>ANYONE CHEAT AND EXTORT</head>
        <p>ANYONE CHEAT AND EXTORT</p>
        <p>with such gracious manner as the Italian innkeeper. Why it is I don't know, but the average Italian looks upon every Englishman as a branch of the Croesus family, and too valuable to be parted with lightly; but they are an interestingly lazy people, ever smiling, ever picturesque, in spite of their dirt. In village, town, and country life seems to roll easily along. Under the shadow of the piazzas one may easily waste his hours in sipping his iced drink and feel none the worse for it.</p>
        <p>Italy is rather dear, and, except in the big towns, living is poor. The roads are fairly good and the military highways vie with those of France. In midsummer, however, they get horribly loose and lusty. The best road is probably the Austrian-made highway from Milan to Venice through Lombardy ; the road to Rome over the Appennincs through Florence is also fine, but the by-roads are quite unsuitable for pleasurable cycling. It must be remembered, too, that the tourist must pay duty on his bicycle on entering the country; the sum, however, is returned on leaving within six months. 'Hieing north again, we come to my own favourite touring ground, Germany. Somehow Germany is not popular with the average tourist, unless I except those portions of it adjacent to the Rhine and the Black Forest. I have done Germany as tbnimucally on nIY wheel as I have France. and</p>
        <p>SEL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>F—RAISING FLOU R. mums TB BEST PASTRY</head>
        <p>F—RAISING FLOU R. mums TB BEST PASTRY</p>
        <p>much as I fl2e the pleasant ma or uaui I lure Germany better. Its roads are but little inferior to those of France; its hotels are scrupulously clean, roomy, ana comfortable, and tne Englishman is welcome everywhere. The single word "Englander" is sufficient introduction to the affections of the most stolid Teuton, There is perhaps</p>
        <p>RATHER MORE SAUSAGE about Germany than we English like, but one soon gets used to that. I remember on my first tour through Germany I become quite resigned to sausage before I left the country. At the very first town I came to, the hotel waiter recommended me to have a beefsteak. Nothing better, thought I. He brought me sausage At the next town I would not have beefsteak, and suggested a chop. Certainly, I could have a chop. They brought me sausage. Beefsteaks and chops being now impossible, I next tried a different dish; a painstaking waiter strongly recbrn- mended me to have a cutlet. I had the cutlet, it was sausage. And then the beer! One becomes Germanised very soon, and quite placidly will sit the evening through in some shady beer-garden, listening to that incomparable German military music, and periodically, and most mechanically, lift and lower that ponderous mug containing the universal brew.</p>
        <p>The tourist will not find much interesting scenery in the great plain of Northern Germany embracing Hanover, Brunswick, Magdeburg, and Berlin. He may feel repaid, however, for his pedalling by ex- ploring those quaint old towns en route which stand to-day substantially as they were centuries back: rife with historical interest, and containing still their relics of the days before the North German Confederation made a nation of many scattered States. For scenery one must go south; into the Black Forest right down to the Swiss frontier, or penetrate into Bavaria. Here the roads are magnificent, although the hilly nature of the country is somewhat trying. One of the most delightful tours I ever undertook was in the Moselle district, following tha course of the river from Metz to Coblenz. This district is not at all tourist-haunted, and deserves far greater patronage at the hands, or wheels, of the cyclist than it does. It is a district which will well repay a fortnight's, or even a month's, exploration. The number of cycling tourists who find their way into Bohemian Austria or the Tyrol are comparatively small. Smaller still, the number who penetrate beyond Vienna into Hungary. And perhaps they are wise, for Hungarian roads are like Russian roads, too bad for even condemnation. But in the Tyrol and Bohemia there is a line field, equalling Switzer- land in the grandeur of its mountain scenery, and possessing peoples of strangely diversified character. The Slav, the Czech, and the German meet; each one intensely patriotic, even to the smallest item of detail, and to he who is observant here is an opportunity for philosophy and wonder. It is in these somewhat out of the way regions that the tourist gets to fully appreciate what cycling abroad really is, for with national costumes and national idiosyncrasies ever before one, the change from our northern civilisation is marked, and lives in the memory.</p>
        <p>The first cycling journey I ever undertook abroad was into Southern Spain. The tour was made under adverse conditions, for the weather was tropically hot and the roads simply vile. The main chaussis of Southern Spain receive so little attention at the hands of the Government that they are almost unrideable in places. The country teems with in- terest, as one may well imagine, and the people are by no means the swashbucklers, which popular legend leads us to believe. On the contrary, they are cheerful and hospitable; Italian in manner, but without the Italian's cupidity. A dignified, proud, and patriotic race, down to the humblest muleteer, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>SAFE TO 00 AMONGST</head>
        <p>SAFE TO 00 AMONGST</p>
        <p>in spite of those hoary tales about brigandage which have given themes to many a hack•author. The posadas in the villages are very poor and com- fortless, but in the large towns the latest adjuncts of civilisation are to be found. In connection with Spain the intending tourist would do well to provide himself with a passport, which may be necessary in order to obtain letters from the post. There is a fine road from the northern frontier town of Irun over the Guadarrama Mountains and through Burgos and Valladolid to Madrid. This s reckoned the best road in the whole of the peninsula, and it is interesting to record that more than twenty years ago Mr. A. M. Bolton, a pioneer English tourist, wheeled over this route on an ordinary bicycle.</p>
        <p>Nearer home we have two delightful countries in which to tour—Holland and Belgium. The Lowlands simply over-run with interest, and though the scenery may consist mainly of canals, dykes, wind- mills, and brown-sail barges, with a melancholy stork here and there to relieve the monotony, yet every village is quaintly picturesque, and mynheer, lumbering and phlegmatic, and hi a frau, plump and red-cheeked, are charming personages. But living in Holland is dear and is rather heavy. The roads generally run on the tops of the dykes, are narrow and paved with small bricks. These bricks, when well laid, form excellent going for the pneumatic tyre, but when they are bad, well—they are very bad. There is in Holland an institution possessing the portentous title of the Algemeen Nederlandsch Wielrijdersbond,which is the C.T.C. of the country, and looks after the interests of touring cyclists. The "Bond" hotels are to be recommended.</p>
        <p>Belgium possesses better roads, where they are not paved with those huge cobbles which make cycling life a misery. In the south the lovely Ardennes are well worth exploring, and in this respect I would heartily recommend a perusal of Percy Lindley's "Walks in the Ardennes."</p>
        <p>THEN THERE IS NORWAY,</p>
        <p>the land of fjords, mountains and rain. Here the roads are lovely, particularly that stretch between Christiania and Bergen. The tourist must, how- ever, be prepared for much hill climbing, and his recompense must be the majestic views which seem everywhere obtainable. I fear that with such a wide area to deal with I have not been able, in the foregoing short article to do the justice I should like to the various countries commented upon. Certainly, much as I am tempted, I dare not picture the "delights" of cycling in Russia, or Siberia, or, Turkey. Having, however, cycled in every European country with the single exception of Portugal, I should like to have the opportunity of expressing my belief that the average Englishman, before referred to, need have no fear of entrusting himself into the hands of the "hated foreigner" in those countries which I have enumerated. On the whole, too, he must enjoy himself far better than he would in England, where the daily paper with its bi-daily war scare serves to spoil a holiday, and where telegrams and letters for ever remind one that eis a worker. In touring abroad there are one or two main rules which should be observed. Firstly, the necessity of a groundwork in geographical detail and the possession of really good maps. Secondly, a philosophical temperament which, when the possessor is "cornered" is ten thousand times mpre efficacious than all the bullying and snarling in the world. Thirdly, a disposition to eat and drink of the commodities of the country, and, fourthly, to remember that, although we are all patriotic Englishmen, the Continental is just as patriotic. "only more sp." Let us leave Our politics and our national idiosyncrasies at home, thzrefore—and then we shall enjoy oursolves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>LADTFIS' LETTER</head>
        <p>LADTFIS' LETTER</p>
        <p>MT nun Mamie,</p>
        <p>' There are in evidence many low-crowned boat- shaped hats of black straw with long black ostrich feathers sweeping round the crown from a jet or steel ornament in front; but they can scarcely be considered really fashionable, although boat-shapes in grey or white felt with black or green trim- mings are often worn by lady cyclists who aim at smartness.</p>
        <p>A hat of fancy open work black straw looks very becoming edged and covered with accordion-pleated frills of black chiffon. In front are large black ostrich tips, drooping on to the hair on either side, and in the front there is a large rosette bow of flame-coloured ribbon, held in place by a dull silver hat-pin, which is studded with paste. A little later on there will probably be some startling innovations in colour mixtures in the trimming of hats. Pink and purple is one that is said to be in store for us, and one of the most up-to-date Parisian models certainly exhibits a wonderful arrangement of folds and frills of pink and heliotrope tulle wreathed round a crown formed of velvet roses shaded from palest pink to purple, a bandeau of bright pink corded silk raising the hat at the left side.</p>
        <p>A suitable spring walking costume can be made of twill of any colour and looks particularly well braided with mohair braid to match. The vest might be ef a contrasting colour. Very smart and effective toilettes can be arranged from a combination of different materials, such as velvet and cashmere, silk and fine costume cloth, aor glace silk and lace. Take, for instance, a walk- ing costume of hyacinth-blue cashmere, trimmed with chestnut-brown velvet, and worn with a little Eton coat of velvet to match. The skirt is cub with a gored front in one length but a full, deep flounce goes round the back and sides, and is headed by rows of velvet, finishing in scrolls, which lie on the seam of the front width.</p>
        <p>Blouses are still very fashionable, and are now made with fewer seams than the tight bodices of a year or two ago. The backs of all smart bodices are now flat and tight, but they are mostly in blouse form, although clearly defining the side outline, and in this fashion admit of a very tight belt, which is still kept quite narrow. Circular, or baysdere, lines of tucks, braid, ribbon, or other trimming are general on these bodices, but not the slightest fulness is permitted at the back, and many are made with fitted fronts also, the loose collar and cascade arrangements taking off the plain effect. Most effective is a silk bodice made with fitted back and pouched front, the silk laid in grouped tucks, and the top of the sleeve finished in the same fashion. A very pretty blouse is of glace s ilk, which is arranged in tucks on either side, a:3d a vest and jabot of chiffon. The edges of the front drap-ric4 are scalloped out, and edged with a very tiny ruching of chiffon, which also appears on the turn-down collar and cuffs. A very useful little blouse suitable either for tennis, cycling, or ordinary morning wear, can be made of flannel or serge and can be worn with a habit front and high or turn-down collar, as de. sired. There is a small V opening in front, which has a very chic appearance with a high collar and small bow tie. This is most simple in make, and cut in three parts, and the sleeves have only the one seam. When putting this into its neat flab band, the sides and back can be kept short and close, and the front allowed to pouch, or it can be i full all round, as shown in the diagram. This s an unlined blouse—usually termed a shirt—and can be worn under an open-fronted jacket. The new capes are infinitesimal in length, but enorrnoes as to width, and rendered even more voluminous by the frills and ruchings so liberally employed. Black capes now show appliquiis of lace, principally in floral designs, or a pretty fashion is a border with two frills of grenadine or gauze, headed by handsome cream insertion lace about three or four inches wide, placing narrow black ribbon ruching at either side of the lace. Lengthway stripes of jet, laid over cream-coloured satin, are also effective, the frills of black grenadine alternating with frills of creamy lace. But applique over-capes of cloth in pale tints are mounted on shot silk, and finished with many pleatings of silk. Capes for matronly wearers have shawl-pointed backs, and the fronts arranged with long stole ends, or with narrow separate fronts running down to form points. Your loving JENNIE.,</p>
