      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>EVERY WOMAN</head>
        <p>EVERY WOMAN</p>
        <p>Bhould send two otamps for our NEW BOOK containing valuable information to Ladies' bow all Irregularities may be Atirely Avoided or Removed by Simple Means Recom ended by eminent Physicians as the only Safe, Sure, and .;enuine Remedy. " Never Falls, Thousands of Testi- monials.—alr. W. E. BLANCHARD. Claremont Ho oreat-road, Da.laton London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>EXAMINEEZ M AY, 6 1898 (Copyright.) THE WHEEL OF FATE.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEEZ M AY, 6 1898 (Copyright.) THE WHEEL OF FATE.</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>Princess Victoria of Schleswig Holstein,yrho on Friday last celebrated the anniversary of her birthday, has now quite recovered her usual good health, and is once more enjoying the active outdoor life she delights in. The bonny Princess is an accomplished hersewoman, and when at Windsor always makes a point of sharing her father's long rides. Princess Victoria's own apartment at Cumberland Lodge is a spacious one on the first floor. The walls are covered with water colors, photos, brackets covered with curios, and flowers are everywhere. The grand piano stands here, and the large writing table is usually heaped with papers and correspondence. Tne Princees has done much useful work in this room, for she helps her mother energetically in all her good works. Little Pr.nce Edward of York has dispensed with pet- ticoats, and now enjoys the dignity of sailor suits—the pockets being, as is usual on many occasions, an im- mense attraction. His pretty curls have disappeared with his petticoats, and in his latest photograph he appears to have quite left babyhood behind him. Al- though His Royal Highness will only complete his fourth year on the 23th of next month,the photegraphs already taken cf him, are simply legion. And unlike the majority of small folks, he enjoys the operation exceed- ingly. Both the Princess of Wales and Princess Victoria are very artistic photographers, and for some years past have been in the habit of taking a Kodak about with tbem on all possible occasions. The Princess of Wales has a tea service of 60 pieces, each pieee being decorated with a different view which she took herself in Scotland. Her Royal Highness too is very fond of photographing aeimals, and at the Prince's horse sale which takes place each summer at Wolferton, the Priecess may frequently be seen on the sale grounds, taking snap shots of the I animals as they pass in review. Madame Patti must be following events in Cuban lwaters with no little ieterest, for it was in Cuba that the Diva sang for the first time in publio. Her real debut though was in New York, at the age of sixteen. Those who had heard her as a child predicted for her a great future. but even their expectations fell short of the mark, and when she arrived in these Isles two years niter her debut, her fame had preceded her. Since those far off happy days, when the beautiful half Italian, half Spanish girl won the heart f the public as the Som- nambula, in the Opera House, Covent Garden, Madame Adelina Patti has remained the pre-eminent favourite of the whole nausic•looing world.</p>
        <p>Among the housewives of Britain, Madame Patti may take high rank, for she estimates above all domestic vir- tues that of punctuality. She is also never a moment late for any engagement, however trivial, and this spirit of regularity is probably one of the secrets of her health. Madame herself is the despair of her clever chef and his numerous attendants, but the great man bas full scope in preparing most exquisite and elaborate menus for guests at the Castle. At a birthday festival last çear, when a large house party was entertained, the elaborate making and ornamentation of the traditional cake with musical instruments of every kind, in sugar and mar- tizspan, was considered an artistic triumph among pro- fessional cooks.</p>
        <p>_ One happy feature in connection with the Court gowns en evidence this month is the large number of British silks employed. Another feature is the soft frilled sashes of chiffon. These are not new, we have seen them more or less for the last year, but for Court frocks, they are something of a novelty. They have a delightful effect on slim figures, and counterbalance agreeably the weight and fulness of the train. Though stately and imposing, Court trains are not al- ways becoming, Many women seem quite overweighterl by their train, especially when it is in velvet. But this is a small matter in comparison with the importance of kreping up the great traditions of our Queen's Drawing Rooms. If any kind of dress were permitted, what a scene it would be. Even es it is, with a hard and fast rule laid down, some extraordinary effects are arrived at by whose who seek by their dress to occasion that attention which would not be bestowed upon them, on grounds of position or appearance. Her Majesty's attachment to all accustomed forms is well known, her dislike to innovations in dress being abundantly proved by her adherence to the same styles as were worn when she first became a widow. The Queen is very quick to notice the smallest divergence trom the usual form of Court dress, and has once or I twice expressed annoyance with women who have en- deavoured to initiate changes that may have seemed to them very desirable, but of which the Soverign took different view. In one well-known instance the wife of a baronet who had cut her hair short, and wore her feathers in a perfectly original fashion to stilt her short coiffure, was received with such a regal frown, that strong minded woman as she was, she nearly col- lapsed.</p>
        <p>The new capes are all very short, but what they lack in length they make up in width, being rendered even more voluminous by the frills and ruchings lihrrally employed. Mantles for middle-aged women (if any re- main) have shawl pointed backs, and the fronts ar- ranged with long stole ends. Hoods in lace or velvet adorn many of the latest specimens, and these are lined with some bright colour, which should either match, or blend with, the gown or toque,with which the mantle is worn.</p>
        <p>Nowadays the older a woman crows, the more youth- ful she becomes. Dentists,hairdressers, and complexion specialists have brought their arts to such perfection that that the middle aged woman is ancient history. With regard to hair, rot one women in ten wears her own', Tourpres are a recovnised institution. But little can h said against this particular modeos under the shelter n a tourpee, it is possible to give our own hair its mtph needed care and rest. What with the absence of oily dres4ings,and the constant crimping,curlinv,and waving operations, few can boast such heads of hair as were the rule when our mothers and grandmothers were young..</p>
        <p>, The laws of England with regard to artificial aids to beauty, are, happily for a considerable number of women' not so striet as they were in the reign of Charles 11. In the year 1670 this curious Act of Parliament was nassed. " That all women, of whatever age, rank,pro• fession, or degree, whether virgins, maids, or widows, that shall, from after the passing of this Act,impose up- on and betray into matrimony, any of his Majesty's male subjects, by scents, paints, cosmetics, washes, artificial teeth, false hair,Spanish wool,iron stays,hoops, high-heeled shoes,or bolstered hips,shall incur the penalty of the laws now in force against witchcraft, sorcery, and such like misdemeanours, and that the marriages upon conviction, shall stand null and void." At the present time,rouge and powder are undoubtedly less seen than formerly, and we probably owe this change to the healthy outdoor life we now indulge in Cycling, golf, tennis, rowing, etc., paint the roses in for us, and our clear healthy complexions no longer need ' the powder so necessary in covering up the muddy specimens of bygone days. Ever since the Princesa of Wales underwent a course of instruction in spinning, before starting the weaving industry at Sandringham,there ha a been a considerable demand for tuition in this picturesque occupation. And to meet this a school has been opened in the West End of London, under distinguished patronege, at which several women well known in society have not only themselves bec-me proficient manipulators of the new " wheel." but in many cases have made wider and more practical use of their knowledge by founding schools for weaving and spinning on their own estates, Siviag thereby a lucrative employment to the local population. A good instance of what can be accomplished with a little energy,ia afforded by the case of a woman in the Orkneys. After thoroughly mastering all tbe details of the art herself, she taught them to the cottagers on h-r large estate, and with such good effect that now the sheep are shorn,the wool dyed,carded, spun, and woven into tweeds, and other fabrics, on the island. Sylmar) POTATOES.—Cut cold boiled potatoes into dice, quarter of an inch thick, and drop into boiling milk thickened with flour, and seasoned delicately with butter, pepper, and salt. When the sauco just boils, serve at once, with a dust of chopped parsley over. IT the remains of any cold boiled fish are available, add them to the potato. _ _</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>KEYSTONE OF HAPPINESS.</head>
        <p>THE KEYSTONE OF HAPPINESS.</p>
        <p>HEALTH has been happily described as a treasure only properly valued when it is lost. When all one's energies are required in the struggle of life, good health becomes one of the most valuable of our assets. Many business men, to whom time is precious, are incapacitated occa- sionally by severe, though temporary, attaeks of those minor ailments from which most of us suffer at some time or cther, and which, if allowed to develop, may assume a chronic and dangerous form. What is obviously required In such eases is a remedy which shall be pleasant in form and effectual in action, without entailing any interference with business pursuits Such a remedy is offered by " California Syrup of Figs." In all cases of habitual constipation, biliousness, indigestion, depression,and loss of appetite, "California Syrup nf Figs" is confidently recommended. To maintain a healthy habit of body, a pure, natural laxatiyei medicine is required, ''California Syrup of Figs" admirably fulfils the purpose without inconveni- ence or discomfort, and in the shortest tinge possible. "California Syrup of Figs," Nature's pleasant laxative, is P delicious extract of the finest ripe figs, with an ex- cellent combination of planta whose properties are medi- cinally laxative,and act most beneficially. Whilst being most pleasant to the palate, its action is mild and gentle, but prompt and unfailing. It acts in harmony with Nature, cleansing the system and aasist- ing the natural functions of liver, kidneys, and bowels, without any of the uncomfortable griping and weaken- ing effects so well known to those in he habit of taking ordinary pills and purgatives. Its good effects are per- manent. Of all chemists in bottles of two sizes.ls. iji. and 2s. 91. Simply asking for " Syrup of Figs "won't do ; von must ask for " California Syrup of Figs," and see that you get it. Every package of the genuipe bears the name and trade mark of the California Fig Syrup Co., 32 Snow Hill, London, E.C.</p>
