      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>By MRS. BAGOT HARTE. Author of "Love's Revenge," "Bianca," "The Silver Lining, &amp;c., &amp;c. CHAPTER XXXVIL</head>
        <p>By MRS. BAGOT HARTE. Author of "Love's Revenge," "Bianca," "The Silver Lining, &amp;c., &amp;c. CHAPTER XXXVIL</p>
        <p>The verdict was no sooner given than Mr. Tre- herne, with joy reflected on his worn, haggard face, rose and walked towards the dock, as if to prove his right to claim his child. Eric followed close behind, his bright face sparkling with anima- tion, looking "smilingly triumphant, as Gwendo- line afterwards said. Meanwhile Lawton had been too much engrossed with the dencntement of the trial to continue watch- ing Richard Musgrove's varying expression. Now, however, it suddenly entered his head In do so, and he glanced round in the direction where he had last seen him—but Richard Musgrove had disappeared. Dennis Vane remained seated at the solicitors' bench, purposely keeping himself in the back- ground. His sense of satisfaction at having indirectly saved Gwendoline from a conviction was greater than he dared to realise • but he knew that his part in the drama was played out, and it was his duty, as a man of honour, to withdraw himself from all contact with her, and centre his thoughts on the woman to whom he was legally bound. "Vane, Mr. Treherne wants to speak to you," said Eric, hurriedly. "Come home and lunch with us." "No, not now, thanks," was the decisive reply. Hume looked disappointed, but there was no time for argument. Outside the court a large crowd had assembled to witness Gwendoline's departure, the news of the unexpected manner in which the trial had terminated having spread like wildfire. As would be expected under the circumstances, exaggerated accounts of Musgrove's perjury were afloat. Some people were saying it had been proved that he had forged the cheque himself; others, that it was he who had uttered it, knowing it to be forged. But incorrect as these statements then appeared to be, they were successful in producing a reverse of public opinion and creating a deep feeling of com- miseration for Gwendoline. Everyone fully believed that she had been the victim of a cruel conspiracy. The moment she appeared outside the court a good, lusty British cheer burst from the assembled crowd. A carmine tint overspread her pale cheeks. She was not prepared for this proof of kind feel- ing; but it struck the keynote of her gladsome, happy nature. "Gwen, give them a smile," whispered Eric in brotherly fashion. The very suggestion had the desired effect. Gwendoline's smile was a pleasing smile—one that called forth instant confidence and admiration; so immediately another cheer, warmer than the last, rang through the air. Mr. Treherne glanced round appreciatively; those cheers were very welcome to his ears, assuring him that his child was already righted in the eyes of many of her countrymen. "Home I" Mr. Treherne said the word with a feeling of unutterable relief; and the footman's normally serious expression relaxed, as he sprang on the box. To poor Gwendoline how good it was to re-enter her home unhaunted by fears for the future; to know that many peaceful days might yet be hers, and that she might still live to lea f 3 'footsteps in the sands of time." Mrs. Treherne had already heard the glad news, and literally fell upon her daughter's neck as she entered. "My darling girl " she exclaimed. "So it has all ended Lar,,ily—as I always said it would. I am so relieved, bo delighted at the satisfactory termination of affairs. Of course, my dear," she added, turning to her husband, "you gave that dreadful person, Richard Musgrove, in charge before you left the court." "No; it never occurred to me to do so." "What an extraordinary omission! You remem- ber how I implored of you weeks ago to prosecute him for perjury, which you must see now was a very wise suggestion." "He is sure to be arrested in the course of the day, if he has not been already," observed Eric. "But where is Mr. Vane ? " she asked, looking round. "Surely you invited that sensible, clever young man to return with you for lunch? Without his shrewdness matters might have terminated so very differently. I am longing to assure him of my heartfelt gratitude." "I asked him to come back with us, but un- fortunately he could not do so," answered Eric. "We must at once shew our appreciation of his admirable conduct in some practical form. What pity it is that he is a married man ! " Gwendoline flushed up and ardently wished her mother's thoughts did not lie so near the surface. Mr. Treherne, who realised that his wife's pro- pensity for making unfortunate remarks was rapidly asserting itself with her returning happiness, pro- duced a welcome diversion by suggesting it was luncheon-time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>CHAPTER XXXVIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXVIII.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hughes was contemplating the Gloire de Dijon rose tree, that adorned the south wall of the Vicarage, with unfeigned admiration. "There must be at least fifty in full bloom," said he meditatively. "I think I will count them," he added, speaking like a man who, having nothing particular to do, is suddenly seized with a desire to find some occupation. "Yes, do," agreed his wife from the drawing- room. "It will fill up your time until Mr. Dawson arrives." There was nothing irritated her more than seeing her husband doing nothing. Mr. Hughes raised his stick, and proceeded to count in measured tones pointing at each flower as he did so. "Twenty-one, twenty-two, twenty- three," he continued with growing interest, which did not abate until he reached thirty-eight. Then, as if somewhat tired of his self-imposed task, he let his eyes wander towards the fields. A moment later he was hard at work again. "Thirty-nine, forty," he said out loud. As he uttered the last number his wife put her head out of the window and remarked:</p>
        <p>---- ----- • "You have counted that rose twice already, William."</p>
        <p>"How can you possibly know, my dear; con- sidering you have been indoors all the time? Ido wish you would not interrupt me. You have made me lose my place, and I shall have to begin at the beginning again--Oh! here's Dawson," he added more cheerfully ; "and his wife has come too! I see her red parasol behind the hedge." "She has come to see the baby! " exclaimed Mrs. Hughes, in a tone of satisfaction. "I was telling her yesterday what a beautiful child she is." Mr. Hughes hurried down the drive and opened the Vicarage gate, and his wife hastened upstairs to warn Grace of Mr. Dawson's approach; also to give directions that baby Phyllis should be adorned in splendour suitable to the occasion. "She could not have come on a better day," observed Mrs. Hughes. The "she " referred to being, of course, Mrs. Dawson. "You are look- ing so much better to-day, Grace, and I wanted her to see you looking more like your old self. How well that dress suits you! But everything you wear seems to suit you. I am not surprised Dennis was captivated by you. But I must not flatter. Will you come downstairs now? You'll End the Dawsons very chatty people." Mrs. Hughes spoke correctly in describing the Dawsons as chatty. As Grace entered she dis- covered conversation going at full swing. It was of a serious nature, agricultural prospects occupy- ing the.foremost rank. Knowing that Grace could not take part if, it, Mrs. Hughes introduced "baby" talk for her especial edification. But Mrs. Dawson had an inquisitive type of mind, and she had come determined to gain, if possible, information regarding Mrs. Dennis Vane's family. "I once met a Miss Tewser at a garden party. I wonder if she was a sister of yours,, she observed addressing Grace. "Have you a sister ? " she added pointedly. "Yes." "Is she fair, like you? " "No; my sister is tall and dark—artistic- looking, some people say." Grace had scarcely uttered these words before she flushed scarlet.</p>
        <p>"She must be very - different in appearance to you," said the visitor, pleased a having made a promising beginning. But Grace's rapidly - increasing nervousness warned Mrs. Hughes to change the topic of con- liersation. _ _</p>
        <p>"Do tell me the latest news about the Treherne case," she began, knowing that imparting news was as pleasing to Mrs. Dawson as ferretting out family secrets. "I am so anxious to know what it is." "Mr. Hitchcock told us all about it," was the readilv-aooken reply. "He oame home by the 4.5</p>
        <p>Itrain. He was at his publisher's in the Old Bailey this morning. and he 'heard the elleee;re Treherne left the coort. It appears that your r husband, Mrs. Vane, discovered the date of a patent, or something like that, which upset the whole of Richard Musgrove's evidence. It is said that either he or a confederate of his must have both forged and uttered the cheque, and that he must have invented all his evidence against Miss Treherne." . "Really! " ejaculated Mrs. Hughes in surprise. "Yes : Mr. Hitchcock says his evidence was a . fabrication, from beginning to end," observed Mr. Dawson, joining in for the first time. "You have read an account of the trial, of course, Mrs. Vane, as your husband is so deeply connected with it ? " "My niece has been too ill to do so," interposed Mrs. Hughes. "What has become of Musgrove? Has he been arrested ? " asked Grace. She was deadly pale now. "Mr. Hitchcock did not say. But of course he will be soon. It would be a gross miscarriage of justice to let him go free," said Mrs. Dawson. "Yes, indeed it would be," concurred Mr. Hughes. At this moment the door opened, and nurse appeared with baby Phyllis in her arms. Instantly all conversation relative to the trial was scattered o the winds. 3Dicl you ever see a more beautiful child, Mrs. Dawson ? " asked Mrs. Hughes, as she took baby from the nurse. The lady addressed rose at once with that critical interest depicted on her face, peculiar to mothers when called upon to express an opinion on other people's babies. She regarded Phyllis carefully for a few minutes, then said, as if pronouncing a verdict : "Yes; she is certainly a very fine child." A discussion on infants generally immediately followed, but it was entirely supported by the two elder ladies. Grace leaned back listening mechanic- ally, with a piteous look of fear in her face. The gentlemen took the opportunity of drawing their chairs near the window and letting their conversa- tion drift back to the Treherne case. "We saw a good deal of the family during our stay in Switzerland," Grace overheard Mr. Hughes saying. "I thought that the girls were kindly disposed and sensible. My wife did not approve of their style of dress; I consider that a minor detail." "Quite so," agreed Mr. Dawson. "The manner in which your nephew has distinguished himself by making this timely discovery respecting the yatent must be a source of satisfaction to you. Of course, it felled Musgrove's evidence to the ground. Hitchcock says it will do Vane's position at the Bar a great deal of good." "I am glad to hear that. But to return to the Leial. The evidence regarding the dress still remains to be disproved. Ido not see how Miss Treherne will be quite free from suspicion until all the evidence is thoroughly refuted. If it can be proved that another dress exactly similar to hers exists, then everything will be simplified at once." "This is what I imagine will happen," said Mr. Dawson, placing the tips of his fingers together, and adopting an oratorical style. "Musgrove, finding himself cornered, will divulge the name of the person who endeavoured to get the cheque cashed at those two shops. then we shall see the mystery as to the dress cleared up." "Then you really believe he knew it was forged, and was a party to trying to get it cashed ? " "Certainly I do." ' "It is sad to think how ungodly some men are." As Mr. Hughes said this, his eyes happened to rest on Grace for a moment. He was startled to see how pale she had turned. "My dear, do you feel ill ?" he asked kindly. "No—oh ! no, thank you—l feel quite well. But the room is very warm—that is all." She spoke in 1 a disjointed manner, then rising, walked slowly towards the door. "Violet eyes, just like her dear father's," she heard Mrs. Hughes, who was still occupied noting the characteristics of the baby, saying to Mrs. Dawson, "and her hair grows longer every day! " "She is certainly a very pretty child, and will grow into a handsome woman, observed Mrs. Dawson. Grace started, then turned round and looked with longing eyes at her child. After a moment's hesitation she retraced her steps to the sofa, where the two ladies were sitting with baby between them. "I will take baby up to the nursery now," she said. "But she is too heavy for you to carry," expostulated Mrs. Hughes. Grace shook her head. "It does not matter if she is, aunt. I should like to carry her upstairs." Mrs. Hughes lifted the child up and placed her in her mother's arms. Once outside the drawing-room Grace leaned down and rapturously kissed the wee, doll-like face nestling on her arms. "Oh, my baby! " she murmured; "my darling little baby! Will they ever tell you ? Must you know? " She had traversed the hall and was just on the point of ascending the stairs, when the hall door was quietly opened and Richard Mu-grove entered. "Hush! " he whispered. "No oue must know lam here. Where can I go? I do not want to be seen by anyone ! lam wanted for perjury." "Oh ! but I cannot hide you in this house—it is mine! " said Grace in a distracted voice. "But you mast ! " He spoke almost defiantly. She looked at him hopelessly. "There is the boot-room leading into the garden. You must wait there—if—if it is necessary. I will come down in a few minutes and speak to you." . Musgrove followed the direction of her eyes, and entered a small room, the door of which stood half-open, then closed the door quietly behind him. He was not many minutes alone—Grace soon joined him. "Why have you come here? " she asked in a I troubled voice. "Because the Treherne trial fell through this morning, entirely through your husband's inter- ference. Now they are rounding on me. But I managed to dodge them, and gut safely to the station and came straight on here." "I can do nothing for you," said Graze, despair- ingly. "Except lend me a little money to take a passage to America with. Bella promised she would give me fifty pounds if I kept her name out of the case. But she turned funky about herself this morning, and left England this afternoon. Can you lend me thirty pounds—at the least ? " "No," she answered, clasping her hands con- vulsively together. "I have been ill lately, and have been considered too weak to he troubled with the care of much money. Here is a five-pound note and three sovereigns; that is all I have got." "Can you not borrow some more? " "I will try to do so; but Mr. and Mrs. Hughes do not consider it safe to keep much money in the house." "You will do your best, won't you? I must fret clear out of England within a few hours. I don't want to be caught." He spoke nervously. "If I had only told may husband everything months ago, all this trouble would have been avoided." "Yes ; hut it's no use crying over spilt milk. The thing is done, and can't be undone. I feel as if I had been playing the scoundrel rather heavily just lately, and it wouldn't be pleasant work being caught, and it won't be easy work getting sway either, for everybody's hand is against me." "Oh ! that I had confessed all to Dennis! she cried piteously. "Sometimes I feel as if all this anr-iiisli and sorrow were killing me." "You look awfully ill. I'm sorry to be bother- ing you now." "I should be glad to die—my heart is broken! " Once clasped her hands tightly over her face as :he said this. She had barely finished speaking when the door opened and Mrs. Hughes walked in. She glanced from Richard Musgrove, dusty and flushed from excitement, to her niece cowering in the corner. Then drew herself up and coughed, a short, ominous cough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CHAPTER XXXIX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXIX.</p>
        <p>A. dead silence followed Mrs. Hughes's entry. Musgrove turned red, then white, but made no -ffcrt to speak. '7l the stillness the Dawson's carriage could be neard driving away. "This man is in trouble, aunt," Grace began, speaking in a low, pained voice, "and he has applied to me for pecuniary assistance. Can you lend me some money ? " "Indeed! " observed Mrs. Ifughes, loftily. "I am always sorry for people in trouble. But at the same time, everyone should shew discretion in the matter of selecting people to whom they apply for help." Then, addressing Musgrove, she continued: "Mrs. Vane is not strong, and ought not to be troubled with such matters. Kindly apply to her husband." _ _ _</p>
        <p>AA silo uttered the last words Mrs. Huzbes _ .</p>
        <p>looicea towards the door, clearly indicating that she expected Musgrove to depart. "I should like, aunt, to—to help this man, lt stammered Grace.</p>
        <p>"It is clearly your duty to do no such thing, without your husband's sanction." Musgrove fidgeted about and bit his lips, but said nothing. . _</p>
        <p>"Oh, aun-t, could you not help me? Perhaps you could lend me ten pounds ? " "Ten pounds!" exclaimed Mrs. Hughes, aghast. "I never yet slept with ten pounds in the house! We should have all the thieves in the neighbour' hood breaking into the Vicarage if *hey thought we kept as much as ten pounds by us As she ceased speaking she glanced from Grace to Musgrove, evidently anxious that one of then should enter into further particulars. But neither spoke. "I think I can spare you seven-and-sixpence,4 she observed magnanimously, producing her purse and carefully counting out the coins, which she laid on a tabs in front of Musgrove. He lifted his eyebrows and shook his head. She looked at him with surprise, wondering what would now be her best course of action.</p>
        <p>"Oh, aunt, if you could kindly get some more money I should be so much obliged," whispered Grace imploringly. "I cannot, Grace," was the definitely-spoken 4 reply. "This young man looks like an—an evil doer. I cannot encourage you in taking his part.° As Mrs. Hughes said this a recollection of her nephew's hasty marriage and his reticence con- cerning his wife's antecedents came surging into her mind. Was it possible that Dennis had deceived her, taken advantage of her love for him to introduce to her as a good, pure girl—one with a past? The next moment she was hastening towards her husband's study. "William !" she exclaimed, entering precipit- ately, "I want you! Come quickly! " "What is the matter? " he asked placidly. But instead of answering she hurried back to the boot-room, Mr. Hughes following with growing surprise, but in no wise suspecting that anything of a distressing nature had happened. One glance, however, at his wife's excited face and poor Grace's crouching form, with Musgrove standing in the background, sullen and nervous, told him that trouble had arisen in their midst. "William," his wife began abruptly, "Grace has asked me to lend her ten pounds to give to this man! "</p>
        <p>"Yes ?" he replied, looking mystified. "May I ask for what the amount is due? " he added, turning to Musgrove. "It is not due for anything. interposed Mrs. Hughes. "Grace wants to give it to him." "Wants to give it to him ? " repeated Mr. Hughes, as if he were thinking out a problem. "Why do you want to give it him, my dear? " he added, addressing Grace. "Because—because—oh! I cannot tell! " She shook her head hopelessly and began sobbing. Mrs. Hughes flushed slightly. The sight of her 'niece's distress affected her in no small measure; but she stood well to her ground and tried to- conceal it. Her husband looked interrogatively at Musgrove. "If you please, sir," the latter began respectfully —for Mr. Hughes was one of those men who command immediate respect—"l have no wish to trouble you or Mrs. Vane; but I am in great need of some money, and having known Mrs. Vane for some years, I ventured to apply to her now. If you could kindly lend me, through her, about ten pounds, I should be greatly obliged, and would leave the house at once."</p>
        <p>There was no immediate reply. Mr. Hughes stood looking critically at him; then he asked: "What is your name ? " "Musgrove," was the answer, thoughtlessly given. "Not Richard Musgrove? " demanded Mrs. Hughes, quickly. "Yes—Richard Musgrove!" was the defiant answer.</p>
        <p>"Oh, William I she exclaimed, turning to her husband, "to think that our house should be a rendezvous of perjurers and forgers! But stand before the window quickly, and I'll lock the door at the outside and wait there while Martha rings the alarm-bell and rouses the neighbourhood." Musgrove turned ashy white and started forward. Grace sprang to Mr. Hughes's side, and seizing his arm, looked imploringly into his face. She was speechless from fear. "Maria! Do nothing! " cried Mr. Hughes in a voice of strict authority. "Is it seemly that you and I should take upon ourselves the denouncing of criminals? Leave such work to those to whore it properly belongs. Assistance to escape this erring fellow-creature shall not receive at my hands. But I will not say a word that shall lead to his capture. Go !" he said, turning to Mus- grove and speaking slowly and distinctly. "Leave this house—and may God forgive you your sins.</p>
        <p>(To be concluded.)</p>
        <p>.el</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WESLEYAN PREACHER AND WIFE.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN PREACHER AND WIFE.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. T. SALMON, a well-known builder and contrac- tor of Brierley, near Barnsley, is also widely respected in the district as a Wesleyan local preacher, and the • Barnsley Chronicle reports a remarkable interview with him. Mrs. Salmon, the wile of the worthy preacher, lo likewise well-known amongst all the people oi the town ship as an invalid. But quite recently her friends and neighbours noticed a !change. From the weakly, delicate woman, so prostrate as to be in danger of -dew h, Ehe almost suddenly became cheerful, hearty, and full of vigour. Many were the congratulations she received.. and her friends were even more than surprised wL they learnt hew the improvement was effected. Th, reporter requested Mrs. Salmon to relate the facts. Tho following is her statement . " I was in very delicate health for years, suffering from bronchitis, antemia,.and palpitation. I was at- tended by a medical man for a long time, but seemed tp get worse. I was thin and bloodless ;my breathing was very bad, and anything like quick movement,especially going upstairs, left me breathless and exhausted. MY lungs were affected; I lost flesh and perspired profusely at night. My friends thought I was dying of consumr tion. All kinds of remedies were tried, and I paid a visit for change of air and treatment to Southport." "Did you receive any benefit from it ?" "Yes, a little ;.but on returning I became as bad as ever. Everyone seemed hopeless. But my husband read in the local paper about a consumptive schoolmiS- tress, who had been suffering similarly to myself, and had been cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. My husband then got me a box, and I obtained -It relief."</p>
        <p>,be The</p>
        <p>instam. _ "And you have not recently taken any other medi- cine ?" the reporter asked. Mrs. Salmon replied, " No, I felt conceived if I fol.' lowed up with these Pills, seeing that I had obtained so much relief from the first dose, I should eventually be. quite strong. And such is the fact. I attribute my eure' solely to the Pills, and so decided am lin my opinion that L have recommended the Pills not only to my imme-- diate friends, but all poor sufferers whom I know to be afflicted. When I tell you that I have a good appetite.. and can eat anything, and can perform my household, duties, whereas before I could eat nothing and was too' weak and ill to do anything, you will see that I have every reason and proof to back up my opinion of their efficacy." These facts, fully confirmed by the excellent preacher accord with the experience of other persons who haVe employed the same remedy. Thin, poor blood gives dis- ease its opportunity. Consumption waits like a tree' &amp;emus foe,to seize on the weak spot : and, especiallY if there has been bronchitis or a cold, attacks lunge which are too weak and bloodless to resist it. Dr. Wit' Sams' Pink Pills give richness and health to the blood, and the blood carries strength—strength to bear fatigue strength to resist disease—all over the body. .Thua have</p>
        <p>itmile."</p>
        <p>been cured many thousands of cases of arsnmia,rheurns- tistn, chronic erysipelas, consumption, bronchitis, anti unhealthy pallor. The Pills are also a splendid nerle And spinal tonic,, and have thus cured many eaFei of paralysis, lotorriotor ataxy, neuralgia, St. Vitus' dance; and nervons headache. They are now obtainable of all chemists,and from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company,4B, Holborn-viaduct, London, at 2s. aci. a box, or six for 235. 91, but are genuine only with fun name. Dr. Wil* hams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Toe latter point is its' portant ts be observed ; there ara many worthless jolt' tations sold as " pink pills," without the name Dr. Wit' hams', which distinguishes the only realty curative , pills.</p>
