        <p>John Gorst has sadly offended the voluntary °Ol party. fie stated in a recent debate that b""ae facts and history of the Christian faith are tLf.Ater taught'to children in Board schools than in ie Voluntary schools," and, according to - the 8 atioaal Society, there is"not a tittle of evidence to „4PPort this suggestion." AOMOVer. it is made .a Ztallziplaint against the Vice President Of the Couneil t, at he should throw the weight of. hie official,posin 4 into an insinuation that Voluntary schools We i,abind Board schools even in those subjects which is the special duty of Voluntary schools to teach. the National Society in its anger overlooks ob- elcUs facts. The reports of inspectors show clearly that, apart from dogma, the knowledge of ti,e Board school children of London in regard to "e facts and history of the Bible is very good in- teed; and on the question of the comparative ex- ia"ence of the two sets of schools, people will more ace.v., the verdict of the Vice President ian that ~)f a part:zaa organisation such as the ational Society.</p>
