        <p>--- , There has been much speculation in all political e4clea as to the great naval preparations in France. "ley have been begun Inarriedly,but they are being altrried on with startling vigour. French sympathy, tlicl the interests of Frence financiers and of French °Mere of Spanish bonds, are on the side of Spain,.. If Cuba and the Pnilippines are lopped off from c e Snanish monarchy the security ot the French breditors of Spain is so much less. It has always Iteen prophesied in Continental newspapers that :79rope would have something to say in connection Zr,lth the cabala settlement, and a Continental corn- Motion could easily send a fleet into American !raters which would overwhelm the fleet of the ttates. This might be one object of the French preparations. Another theory is that a demonstra- !ton cn the West Coast of Atrica is intended as en ,3,leewer to our military preparations in the interior. conflicting rumours regarding the negotiations "tween France and this -country are suggestive of Cart; . mg trouble in that quarter. A few days more /41111 disclose in what direction French energy, is cut to expend itself.</p>
        <p>j 4 !Meat addition might be made to the degree of celigence with which strangers follow toe pro- \vednags of the House of Commons if attendants authorised on occasion to explain the inner • ih rung ot the Parliamentary joke. Here is a,case in,P°int. Mr. Giti;•on Bowles rose on Tuesday even- elZ to put a question. Eefore he had had time to toe his lips grave and elderly senators sadnenly titaPsed into fits of laught,r. Others, whose met- Was only less boisterous, broke into loud cheers. There was nothing in the appear- Mr. Bowles to account for the explosion. drite era craned Whir necks and scrutinised hie se- esit„tind natty figure with intense but futile curi- olo,;Y• They, of course,could not know that the de- ledrtration was intended as a hilarious tickn3mr- bai2'l,lllut of Mr. Bowles's victory over the united der" of her Majesty's Government. Ousted some ivPc .7 a . o from his corner seat above the gangway, re2,;°ll. member had been fighting ever since:to Possession. Once orlwice the struggle at- into a trial of physical strength ""etni himself and the official representative, as Bowles believes the usurper to hare been of,rm I"2lgrateful Government. Finally he resorted to the iliat extremity of Parliamentary protest by return- whip" sent to him by-Sir William Walrond. +net was on momday. On Tuesday Mr. Bowles Came down d witnessed his triumph in the .elo- fluent vacancyan of the accustomed the reception of a con seat. He hadqueror.</p>
        <p>Probslaly very few people have any idea of the Itnniense Wealth of the Philippine Islands, which! tj"11.../12' now probably fallen into the hands of the „."Ked States. Perhaps the best proof of their greatj uatortit en_ resources is the fact that, in spite of the; nitions of government that have prevailed under' c„"113, the Islazds have prospered greatly. A very ii"eln_ Potent authority recently stated that he be-: tCed the Philippines to be the 'richest islands in h World. What other country-could have stood, 6 ask to the three steady drains on her resources tt.,„ "101 l the Philippines have been subjected? The loo' of these is the to the official .olass. There the 1141111 e of" to the victor the spoils" is exempli- rie In its most extreme form. From the Governor honeral to the smallest employe in be Custom.: eb,t4se every official is changed when there is a , hasn.Re of Government in Madrid, and every office' to vista price—a price which is paithby the holder ohtn",e person through whose influence it has been .t 51d. The resillt is corruption, open and un tlied—squeezing the commerce and industry ri- ot, lands to the limits of safety; and, as their hold litlti'meBe lucrative posts is so precarious, the aim raLinktlbieet of every good Spaniard -is to save as ~as he can while the good time ,lasts. With 'to a Object Spanish officials reduce theirerpenditure bolllininium and send every dollar they can save 1V11,5;.,' 0 Spain, to be invested against the day tee tney must return there to make way for their Teti -sors. The sum of ,money sent to Spain each r b„ , t_eocia,emcials is very large, but it is doubtless ex- oY sums annually sent to China .by the ;rulers, who have absorbed the entire retail ;he islands. Some of these Chinamen are 1Y rich, vet they will live on a tew•dollars and remit the balk of their savings to Last and not least, there is the heavy drain by the Church, which here is as wealthy )werful.; and lain assured by persons who ,osition to know that the rebellion in the Les is much more a revolt against ecthesias- ttion than against the civil government— ' that undoubtedly is.</p>
