      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WARRINGTON RUGBY' TOOTBALL CLIJI3. A SEASON'S FEATS AND FAILURES.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RUGBY' TOOT- BALL CLIJI3. A SEASON'S FEATS AND FAILURES.</p>
        <p>Tim curtain has been rung down on another season of Rugby football, and for four months quietoes i reign supreme on'the enclosures wherein mach exiting ' battles have been fought during-the past eight munths. At Wildeispool, one would think football a totally for- gotten pastime to judge by the pastoral appearance of tbings,for instead of being trampled by the heavy boots of excited footbellers,the turf is now the happy bunting ground of a drove of sheep. Bat though this may be a peaceful prologue to a series of hard facts—some of them rather unpalatable—it is the facts that are, I imagine, most degred. Well,to commence with, a general com- parison between this season's doings and those of last -season is not productive of the satisfaction it should he, Instead of progre4s being made, the movement of 1897-Bg-has been rather one of retrogression. Less matches have been won, more lost, and altogether the doings of the first team have been more mediocre than is liked. Last season a rather poor progress through the League fixtures, was compensated for by brilliant cup- tie achievements, but this season all we can see is an in- ferior League record and a regular" knock-out "in the cuptim, slightly atoned for ley some creditable perform- ances jest before the call of time. To sum up, the season has not been actuaily a bad one, but yet tar below the staedard hi herto maintained by Warrington in Rugby circles. Achievements and failures have been medium throughout. One of the things most manifest in all first class matches has beenthe lack of scoring power, and the necessity foe an infusion of a little new bloodin one or two places has not only become apparent to the mem- berg but to the more eelightened section of tin !committee. Warrineton and Runcern now stand alone as teams of local talent, and the latter's escutcheon is the more spotless of the two. Similarly both have received some pucommenly haraknocks, both have finished up worse than in the previous year, and both ought to be con- winced of the neeesaity for keeping level with the times. Warrington are not without excuse for their reverses, as Barber's enforced retirement from the game for half the season, means ' far more than the absence of one man usually does. Minus him, and Warrington is minus half it scoriae pewer. Minus Bate and the team would be miring prectic illy all aeressiveness. Very little change is apparent in the names of the players cornposing the Premier team now, from those of twelve months ago. Boseew has superseded Heesom,end Duckworth replaced Berber, butthe four most notable additions to the ranks are R. Bate at half back, and Mason, Morrison, and Naylor in the forwards. Young " Bob" has made such a good partner for" Smack 'that the poiitione behind the scrimmage Seem filled fors year or two. The other three players have turned out I) De the best of the sfor- wards, their speed, dash, and cleverness earning them much distinction. What the cotnposition of the fifteen will kbeit fwoiullranit loenateht coshnetnacien, no one knows, but I hould thin these four. The latest re- cruit of all is J. W. Wood, who has shaped exceedingly well during his shert career with the team. Statistics show us that in the Lancashire Senior Com- petition, Warrinston have played 28 matches, won 10 lost 14. drawn 2. total 22 points, and scored 13 g0a15,34 against 26 goals, 42 tries (178 points). Ttrhieesn, arim7 inf2B poorints), consi,tency which was held by Widnes last seasen, now certainly belongs to Warrington, whose career all along has been most in and out. They com- menced the season with a loss of 19 points to nil, and followed it up with a win the following Saturday, by 19 points to 8. Then the results arrived in somewhat simi- lar style until just before Christmas, when Barber was hurt, anri a period of depression ensued and lasted until the last month of the season, which was the brightest of the whole eight. The two most brilliant achievements of the season have been far apart, thefirst, Swinton's de- feat at Wilderspool being on Octobet9, and the triumph ever Widnes, the last league match of the lot, on April 28. The latter was such a refreshing surprise that one' is almost tempted to exclaim "All's well that ends wale'', IA record of the fixtures and scores for 1897-8 is as follows : POINTS.</p>
        <p>Sept. 4--Tyldesley a .... 0 19 11—Rochdale H., h ........... ' IS—Wigan, a —........„ .. ........... 0 3 25—vtockport, h ... .... . Oct. 2—Broughton R.,h ... ...... 0 2 9—Swinton. h lO . 5 1 '6—Morecambe, a.. . l3 0 23—St He!ens, a ~. 8 3 1 30—Leigh. h 'Nov. 6 —St. Helens Ree5,h.............. 13—Runcorn. a 3-- 5 20 —Salford, ft A 27-Ilochdale H., a Dec. 4—Tyldesley, h 11—Oldham a 18—W1gan, h ... 8...... 0 25—Widnes, h</p>
        <p>...,- --. 27—St Helens, h— 8..... 0 Jan. I—Leigh, a . .......... ............. 0.....k1 B—Morecambe, h 10...... 2 15-01dham h . 22—Salford,, ..- ..... *- 29—Stockport, a Feb. s—qwinton, a .. 12—Hull Kingston Rovers h.— 19—Wigan. h 26—Cup tie (Wakefield Trintty a) .. Mar. s—St Helens.. ... 12—Runcorn, h ..</p>
        <p>19 —Manninghara. a .... 2... 8 26—Manningham, h April. 2—Broughton R.. 0...... 9—Birkenhead Wanderers,' k... 24...... 0 11—Lancaster. h 23—Birkenhead Wrmderers; is 23—Widnes. a __ . .. . . .</p>
        <p>-,—. _ _ It will be seen that a number of friendly matches have been played in addition to Competition games, and with these the full record for the season is : played 3E, won 17, lost 17, drawn two, and 23 goals 55 tries,2ll points, against 33 goals, 52 tries,`222 points. The highest score Warrington have made has beetl24 points to nil against Birkenhead Wanderers, and the highest score against them has been 21 points to nil, which has been twice scored,the teams being Leigh and Hull Kingston R‘scers. Warrington have been beaten by three or less points on seven different occasions. The individual scoring record is not a bad one,Moores especially having done well with 12 tries to his credit. Last semen the top score was only five tries,but five players reached this poor maxi- mum. Out of 40 players who have at times done duty, the following are the searers.:— .. tries goals volute Moores l2 0 36 Mason Barber Duckworth '4 4 20 Isherwood Deakin '4 0 12 Naylor 'Wood 3 0 9 Taylor Scott ,Clroas .Hallam 1 1 5 :Mill 'l 1 5 Houghton, Lawless, Fairhurst, Radford, J. T. Bate, R. Bate, and Morrison have each scored a try, and Mos- cow and Heesom have each kickeid a goal. ,During the season, Warrington have beaten St. Helens three times. They have beaten Merecansbe, 'Birkenhead Wanderers, and Wigan, twice: and the following once each :—Tyldesley, Rochdale Hornets, Stockport, Swinton, St. Helens Recs., Manningham, Lancaster, and Widnes. They have drawn once each with Leigh and Salford. They have lost once against Tyldesley, Wigan, Salford, Roehdale Hornets, Leigh, Stockport, Swinton, Hull Kingston Rovers, Wakefield Trinity, Manningham, and Widnes, and have been beaten twice by Broughton Rangers, Runcorn and Old- ham. They have won four matches away from home, viz. Morecambe, St. Platens, Birkenhead Wanderers, and Widnes, and have been beaten en their own ground five times, the teams to do this being Broughton Rangers, Runcorn, Oldham, Widnee, and Hell Kingston Rovers The two drawn games—Leigh and Malford—were both played at Warrington. Deakin has the honour of taking part in most matches, as out of 36 he has played in 35, the only occasion upon which be rested being the friendly with St. Helens Recs. This is a fine record. Closelrfollowing him are Moores and Fairhurst with 34 games, and Isherwood and Taylor with 33 Barber only todk .part in 12 games and yet in those he scored four tries and kicked 6 goals, so that his loss to the team can easily be seen. The .** A " team has done better than the Senior Club, with 13 victories out of 29 matches, and 11 defeate. They made rather a bad start or would have stood a much better chance for the "A" team championship, which has again gone to Widnes. Renewed interest has been given to the fag-end of the season by the Works' Competition' in which 20 clubs have been actively engaged. The final was played on Saturday and the medals were wan by ?Eamon and Knowles, who just defeated Crosfield's by a try to nil. Large crowds have assembled to watch the struggles each evening, and in consequence a sum exceeding £6O hasibeen banded over'to the local charities. It is pleasing find the Warrington Football Club so ready to devote a portion ot their handsome profits to deserving otejects outside their control.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>NEW LEMONADE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW LEMONADE.</p>
        <p>2 GALLONS FOR 441), Many people suffer from extreme thirst during the hot weather. Messrs. Foster Clark and Co. have supplied the want that has BO long been felt by making a eoncentrated lemonade, which is far more economical than the ordinary lemonade. It is made from the finest lemons, and the great advantage Is that it is partly manu- factured in Italy, in the midst of the lemon orchards. The lemons are taken direct from the trees tothe factory to commence their transfor- mation into the Eiffel Tower Lemonade. You can get thirty•two tumblers (or two gallons) for fourpence halfpenny. If you cannot get it from your grocer, send sixpence to</p>
        <p>G. FOSTER CLARK AND Co</p>
        <p>O. 594-EIF-FiL TOWER FACTORY. MAIDSTONE. Sole mannfeetureraot " EIFFEL POWER LEW/UDE</p>
        <p>A NEW ZEALAND TRADE COMM. SIGNER OW THE ADVANCING COLONY.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Holmes, the New Zealand Government Commissioner visited Warrington on Friday last week with the object of placing before Warrington manufactured; the advantages of his colony so aptly set forth by the MOD. W. P. Reeves the agent gen- eral, in his pamphlet " rhe Fortunate (Wee" and also to interest them in the country's imports and ex- ports. Mr. Holmes is especially desirous of calling the attention of rope and cordage makers to the merits of Nevnealand hemp as being suitable for binder twine,.clothes lines, ploughing reins,halters, and similar goods. He is also interesting the vari- ous varnish makers throughout the kingdom in the advantages of \Read gum which is admitted to be an excellent basis for varnish making. This gum is obtained from the Northern parts of the island where it is found throughout a district estimated roughly at some 1,800,000 acres in extent. Already Mr. Holmes has made great progress in advancing ' the interests of the New Zealand colony., mention of his name being constantly made in the principal papers of the lingdons• He visited amongst other places of business in Warrington the gas stove works of Messrs. Richmond and Co. Ltd., and expressed the liveliest interest in their very extensive industrial operations and also his admiration of the magni- tude of the company's workshops and offices ex- tending, as they do, over the greater portion of both sides of Academy-street. The spacious showrooms of the company, forming part of this range of build- ings, were also inspected and the large quantity and variety and the artistic quality of the goods displayed were matters of which the Commissioner spoke in highpraise. Mr. Holmes was especially in- terested in inspecting the large number and variety of stoves packed for delivery to the various Gas Companies who have adopted the penny-in-the- slot gas meter—a departure which he thought would be watched keenly by the Gas Companies of his colony. Mr. Holmes had previously made a abort visit to the soap works in the district and had explained to the various manufacturers the nature and extent of the tallow export trade from New Zealand. He is also giving to our home mann- facturers, shippers and exporters, all and every in- formation he can afford in regard to the imports into his colony which last year represented over £8,000,000, 75 per cent, of which, we are pleased to learn,is to be placed to the credit of British manu- facturers leaving only 25 per cent, to be divided amongst the commerce ot the remainder of the world. We are pleased to learn that Mr. Holmes' mission has already borne good fruit. Since his ar- rival in this country several largl orders at im- proved prices for regular shipments of hemp have ' been sent to the colony. This must be very gratify- ing to him and especially to the colony he so suc- cessfully serves. This excellent object les- son may be commended to all British manufacturers who desire an extension of 'their trade with the outer world. The steady increase and development of New Zealand may be judged by a few facts taken from the Budget speech by the Right 'Hon. Dr. R. J. Seddon.delivered on the 12th October, 1897. It appear; that the wool exported has increased from 102,522,185 lbs in 1890 to 128,309,673 lbs in 1896; while the quantity Purchased by local mills has increased from 2,979,293 lbs. in 1890, to 3,989,934 lbs in 1896. the total in- crease aggregating 26,798,129 Pm The number of sheep increased from 16,116,113 in 1890 to 19,138,439 in .1896. representing a total increase of over 3.000,000 in six years. Turning to the frozen meat industry the tables show that in 1890, the export was 100,934,756 lbs., while in 1896, it was 123,576,544 lbs, an increase of 22,641,788 lbs. Equally satisfactory has been the progress ot the dairying industry In In% the combined hatter and cheese exports were valued at £207,687. In 1896 they were valued at £411,882. With refer- I ence to Kauri gum the annual output varies from 7,000 to 8,000 tons. The total export of gum from this Colony from 1854, to the end of 1896, is quoted at 183,529 i tons, having a value of £8,114,852. It is this apparent that the gum industry of New Zealand is one of no mean importance. The estimated number of people, male and female, more or less directly dependent on gum for a livelihood is roughly stated at from 5,000 to 6,000. If ever zeal and industry along with an intelligent grasp of all the facts affecting trade will help New Zealand to secure a leading position in the group of our Australasian colonies then the Government of the country is to be congratulated on securing the services of so able a commissioner as Mr. Holmes to whom we wish every success and a safe return to his colony.</p>
        <p>-- CADBURYS Cocoa is absolutely pure. and Is therefore the best Cocoa.lt Is a refreshing,stimulating drink, and a nutriti.- one food, containing no forelgh substances, inch as kola, malt, hope, ie. The facts cannot he t^o strongly impressed that Cocoa must be unadulterated to ensure Its tulle t beneficial effects. Always Insist on having CADBURY'S-5.1d only in Packets and Tins—as other Cocoas are often tub. 'diluted for the sake of extra prott,...Advt. (3).</p>
