      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>THE Ex" ,MINER, FRIDAY, MAY 6,1898.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>-A meeting of this committee was held on Friday inthe Board Room at the Workhouse. Mr. J. C. Sutton occu- pied the chair pro tent. There were present : Messrs. G. Pilling, William Hickman, A. Jolley, A. J. inlleY, J. Billings, J. Clarke, William Hnueiton, J.P.,J. Johnson, S. Davies. and T. Flalton, with Mr. S. Sutton (assistant clerk), Mr. Tunstall (assistant overseer), and Mr. Owen (valuer). APPOINTMENT OF CHAIRMAN. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLInt-1:1-01:1C1H--T-0-1; said t-h-e--fiWt---5--u-siness of the meeting was the election of a chairman for the ensuing year. He was going to propose a name which he was sure would be unanimeusly received. There were special features in connection with the year they were entering upon with regard to the assessments, and there were several important matters in a state of transition, It would therefore be to the advantage of the committee to have the former Chairman re-elected, and he had there- fore great pleasure in proposing that Mr. George Pilling be Chairmen for the ensuing year. Mr A. J. JOLLEY seconded, and Mr. A. JOLLEY supported. Mr. BILLINGE also supported the resolution and said , that a section of the ratepayers seemed to be misled by certain statements which had been made in the public press. He wished as a member of the committee to say that the committee had not been by any means a hole and corner meeting. Any person who felt aggrieved at any particular assessment in the Union could have an easy, legal way of making known his particular grievance to that committee, and be had always found the commit- tee were willing to entertain any suggestions or to fully investigate these grievances. The meetings were open to the public press, and full accommodation was made for them. _</p>
        <p>Dayses : Would it not be better if you would save your remarks for any particular case which may come forward. You are telling your secrets to the enemy, I think. Mr. HICICIUN : Are you supporting the motion or are you giving us a set half hour's speech ? (Laughter.) Mr. Huainan lam saying this because several rate- payers have spoken to me about it. I wish to say that we shall be pleased to meet them at any time. The motion on being put to the meeting was unani- mously passed. Mr. PILLING on taking the chair said that he should not have taken it had it not been through the action taken against him. He would otherwise have made way for Mr. A. J. Jolley whom ha hoped at no distant date would take the position. THE VICE-CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Deviss preposed that Mr. A. J. Jolley be vice- Chairman. Mr. A. JOLLEY. as one of the oldest members of the Committee,seconded. - The motion was carried, and Mr. A. J. Jolley, in reply said that he hoped he would deserve the confidence of the committee. THE DATE OP MEETING.</p>
        <p>After a short discussion it was decided that the meet- ings of the committee should be held as usual on the first Friday in each month at the usual time.</p>
        <p>THE RATING OF BISHOP'S WHARF.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. L. FLEMING appeared in support of an appeal against the assessment f the Bishop's Wharf and Shipping Company. He said the case had been before the Committee on a previous occasion, and it was with reference to the excessive rating of the wharf. It was adjourned because matters had not been settled with the Warrington Corporation. He was glad to say that this had now been done,and the rent had been settled at £36 per annum. He now produced the receipt. He also produced a receipt note from Messrs. Bishop and Com- pany. Limited, showing that the rent of the warehouses was £lOO. In 1897, the rent of the warehouses was only £B7, but the accommodation had since been in- creased. The company now asked the committee to allow them to pay on an assessment less one-sixth of the rent instead of on the assessment in existence, viz. : £170. By Mr. Hickman : Is there any ground rent on this wharf ?—lf there is the Corporation will pay it.—ls it let on an annual tenancy or on a lease ?—We have a lease of seven years from the commencement.—By Mr. Owen : The rent of £lOO is only for the warehouse fronting the wharf and not on the new portions which have been added behind ?--It is for tbe whole block.— It is a rent the company charge themselves P—Messrs. Bishop and Corapany,and the Shipping Company are en- tirely different firms.—This rent of £36 to the Corpora- tion is quite different from the published facts P—Mr. Armitage and myself went before a committee of the Corporation, and that was the price agreed upon.—By Mr. Billinge : We have it that the gross estimated rental is £2OB may I ask how much is paid to the Corporation as rental?-IThe answer was tkat £36 is paid to the Cor- poration, and £lOO to Bishop and Company —I suppose the directors of Bishop and Company and the Bishop's Wharf are the same P—No, there are different directors for the Shipping Company and for the Borax Works. I don't think I ought to disclose their names.—The Clerk said it wasa question on which the committee must rely on the experience of its valuer. The whole thing led to this that with the new light now thrown upon the case it was advisable for the valuer to go through the proper- ties and advise the committee as to the valuation to be made.—Mr. Hickman moved that the valuer be in- structed to g into the matter and report to the commit- tee.—Mr. A. J. Jolley seconded.—ln answer to Mr. Houghton, Mr. Fleming said his reference to less 1-6 re- ferred to the difference between gross and rateable.— The resolution was then carried unanimously. THE ASSESSMENT OF THE LONGFORD HOTEL. The CLERK said the question of the assessment of the Longford Hotel was practically an adjournment of the case. At the last sitting of the committee no alteration was made in the assessment of £l5O gross and £127 lOs rateable. Thereupon Mr. Arthur Browne served him with a notice of appeal against the decision, but they agreed between them that in view of the fact that it was a new house, incomplete in several respects,to defer the subject until that meeting with a view to seeing what the position of the house would be. Mr. &amp;unarm BROWNS said they did not want anything unreasonable or anfair. This publichouse was only built last year, and was occupied on July 10th for the first time, but the builders only finished the house the pre- vious week. With reference to the bowling green which it was intended to lay down, he might say that only five yards had been set and it would be twelve or eighteen months before it was ready for use. The hotel was intended to meet the wants of an increasing neigh- bourhood, and in view of the building of the Asylum. There had been no hands employed at the Asylum until within the last few months. There were now a few hundred hands engaged but they had only commenced within the last week. He suggested that as it was a new house a temporary assessment should be made for a few years. He would suggest that it be reduced from £l5O to £lOO. £l5O was too much,and was almost equal to the Lion Hotel. There were only three rooms down- stairs and four bedroom!, and bathroom. He under- stood that the Valuer had not assessed the hotel himself but that the Overseers had fixed it. They had had some difficulty in ascertaining who assessed it. The takings , were not sufficient to justify even £lOO as an assess- ment. The takings averaged £l6 10s. per week. The hotel was not built to meet present requirements but future requirements, and those would not be for a few years. He therefore suggested a temporary assessment of £lOO per annum until the Asylum was further ad- vanced,and the neighbourhood had increased in popu- lation. Mr. BILLINGS Do you say you have had some diffi- cults:, in ascertaining who assessed it? Mr. BROWNE : Yes we have. Mr. BILLING&amp; : I am sorry th- Overseers hadn't the moral courage to tell you. Mr. WiLLIAM HOUGHTON : They h perhaps taken it from the Inland Revenue authorities. Mr. BROWNE : No, the Inland Revenue authorities say that they have taken it from the Overseers. Mr. HICKMAN : What is the actual rent ? Mr. Swamis : ESC). We went before the Inland , IRevenue Commissioners and they have reduced the In- come tax of the tenant below £l6O. He produced his books and that satisfied the Commissioners. The Committee then considered their decision, Mr. Browne having retired. Mr. A. JOLLEY said he knew the house very well, and proposed that the assessment be reduced to £lOO gross. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said he would like to know what was the assessment of the Horse and Jockey, which was a house not very far away acid might be a partial guide in arriving at the value. The CLERK said whatever was done in this case it should bee temporary arrangement. The CHAIRMAN said he remembered that the com- mittee reduced the assessment of the Imperial hotel until the bowling green was made. Mr. TIINSTALL (assistant overseer) said the assessment of the Horse and Jockey was £125 gross. After some further discussion it was decided to reduce the assessment to £lOO gross and £ll5 net, and that this arrangement should last until March next. THE ASSESSMENT OF PUBLIC PARKS. The CLERK said at the last meeting a letter was read from the Town Clerk stating that there was a recent decision of the House of Lords that public parks were not rateable under law. He (Mr. Sutton) wrote its reply to the Town Clerk that he would have to appear in due course before the committee. The Deputy Town Clerk (Mr. Wilson) waited upon him the previous day,and ex- plained that the Town Clerk was away and would not be able to attend that morning, and asked that it be adjourned until the next meeting. He (Mr. Suttou) noticed that other townships in the Union were interes- ted and proposed to follow the decision when arrived at. The Deputy Town Clerk showed him the case upon which they relied, and he pointed out to them what in his (Mr. Sutton's) judgement made the cases not , parallel. Be did not thins they were, and he said that subject to any opposite view being expressed. It would be quite right to hear the Town Clerk upon it at the neat meeting.—lt was decided to adjourn the considera- tion of the matter until the next meeting. THE VALUER'S REPORT. -</p>
        <p>Mr. Ow= in his report said he had made the following valuationa and inspections in accordance with instruc- tions and begged to report: In the township of Warring- ton :—Mr,John Clarke,shop and house,Orford-lane,addi.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MAZAWATTEE TEAS AHEAD OF ALL OTHERS 1</head>
        <p>MAZAWATTEE TEAS AHEAD OF ALL OTHERS 1</p>
        <p>MAZAWATTEE TEAS AHEAD OF ALL OTHERS I</p>
        <p>REFRESHMENT HOUSE KEEPERS AND THEIR SELLING HOURS.</p>
        <p>APPEAL TO THE WATCH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>- - --- At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Mon- day morning, before the Mayor (Alderman Wright), Mr. T. Birchall,Councillor J. Shaw Green, Alderman Blackhurst, Mr.J.W.Hayward, and Councillor Sme- thurst, three refreshment house keepers were sum- moned for keeping open their houses during pro- hibited hours, namely, after eleven o'clock. Mr. I 'Browne appeared for the defendants. The first case was against James Danks, of 17, Bewsev-street.—P.C. Beecharc said he was on duty at 11-30 p.m. on April 20th, with another constable when they saw five men taking refreshments in the house.—By Mr. Browns: The defendant only sold chipped p itatoes, peas, and fried fish. They didn't open on Sunday. James Mayhall, 131, Sankey-street, was charged with similar offence on the 23rd April.—Detective Meacock said that at 11-45 on the night in aquestion he was in Sankey-street with Detective Forster, when he saw the defendant's door open. There was a table across the doorway, and the defendant served four customers in the street with chipped potatoes.—By Mr. Browne :The euatomers did not enter the shop but stood in the street. —Mr. Browne : Is it a fact that the police during the last fortnight have instructed the whole of the refresh- ment house keepers to close at eleven o'clock P—Datec- 'tive Meacock : Further back than that. Alfred Smith, 86, Orford-lane, was similarly charged. —lnspector Spinks said these people had been cautioned in November last in consequence of complaints having been received at the Police Station of persons having been guilty of disorderly behaviour at this class of shop. —CouncilloiShaw Green: Have they been cautioned recently ?—The deputy Clerk: In November a written notice was sent out. Mr. Browne said this was a class of offence which bad caused a great deal of trouble in different towns. These people were entitled to keep open during the same hours as a licensed victualler—until eleven o'clock week nights and from 12.30 to 2.30 on Sunday and six to ten. They did n open their houses on Sunday, however. On the week nights they did not open their shops until six o'clock. They had a refreshment house licence, which cost a guinea a year each, and unless they took out that licence they were liable to a penalty. What rid keep anything to be owoansouttnhoei foanct tnPo—ipfrotmheigyesdidthnooyt anloluo open all night ; but inasmuch as they sold a paltry glass of gingerbeer, or a pennyworth of fried fish or chipped potatoeg, and allowed them to be consumed on the pre- mises they must close at eleven o'clock. It struck him that this was one of those laws that ought to be honoured more in tne breach than in the observance. It was the height of absurdity. What moral offence bad been committed by selling potatoes and fish after eleven o'clock? No harm was done to anyone, and nothing of an intoxicating nature was sold, nor was any gambling allowed. In Manchester—Oxford-street—a compromise had been effected between the Watch Committee and the refreshment house keepers, who wished to keep open after eleven o'clock. because most of their business was done when people came out of the thratres. They under- took not to open on a Sunday, end he (Mr. Browne) bad a proposition to make with regard to his clients, If the police and Watch Committee would undertake not to issue any more summonses the defendants would not open on a Sunday. The case could be adjourned so that he could appear before the Watch Committee and dis- cuss the question with the members. The mayor We don't want to stand in the way of these men making a living. I prefer the adjourn- ment. Mr. Birchall : But the law save the shoos shall not be kept open after eleven o'clock. It may be a frivolous law, but etill it is the law. The Mayor : The feeling of the Bench is that we must fine the defendants only on this occasion in the costs. If Mr. Browne likes he can appear before the Watch Committee to arrange a compromise, Up to the present it is branch of the law to keep open after 11 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Browne : We will appear before theeommittee on Thursday. The committee have power to order the Chief Constable not to take p!oceedings. (To the Bench :) Will you. say the case is dismissed on pay- ment of costs ? The Mayor We see no objection to that.</p>
