      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>grosi £5, making ,otal assessment gross £4O, rate- able £34. Mr. Thomas Locker, works, Ellesinere-acreet Inasmuch a:4 curtain land had been given up arid new buildings had been erected and other land aloining acquired, ho had revalued the whole premises for assess- ment at gross £79, rateable £64 10s. The present assessment ot gross £47 10s., rateable £4O sh.uld be •truck nuZ. Messrs. Richmond Aryl Co., Limited, plrlitinns east and wee' sides of Azaderny-strcv, grass £176 104., rateable £146. Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons, Limited, Dallam Brewery, additions, gross £45, rateable £36. Messrs. Platt, Wilkinson and Co.. Limited, cutting shop, Norman-street, gross £231, rateable £lB6 103. This is the value he put upon this building if wholly occupied. At present only one floor was occupied, and be understood that this would be so for souse little time. He should therefore recommend that a trmporary assensment of one-third the amount be fixed for the present which would be gross £77, rateable £62. Me.srs. H. Bennett and Son, Derby House,Levely Lane bakehous.s, stores and stables, gross £2O, ratable £l7. The following properties which had been inspected were not yet sufficiently completed for valuation. War- rington Corporation wharf, Ho imlAy ; Messrs. Forshaw and Son, additions to works, Napier-street ; Messrs. Bishop's Wharf Co., exrension of wharf; Messrs, Monks, Hall and Co., Limited, tube works and offices,Atherton's Quay, The report was adop'ed. THE ASSESSMENT OF GAS AND WATER MAINS.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM QOM:MON thought the extension of the mains of the Warrington Corporation ought to receive the attention of the committee. He thought it was time there was a revision of the assessment of these mains. There had been several material extensions of the water mains to Penketh, Great Sankey, Poulton-with-Fearn- head and other places. Mr. HtcxmAx : That means it wants a revision of the whole thing. I After considerable discussion it was decided that steps be taken for the reassessment of the gas and water un- dertakings 4if the Corporation. THE REVISION OF THE ASSESSMENTS OF THE UNION.</p>
        <p>After some discussion, it was decided that the Clerk should take strps for a revision of the several railways in the Union's district. It transpired in the course of the discussion that there was no systematic revision of the assessments of the Uni3n.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley said he was c,rtainly under the im- pression that a systematic revision of the assessments of the Union was made at certain periods. 'The CLERK said that was not so. It was the case in London.</p>
        <p>Mr. A.. 1. JOLLEY thought there ought to be a syste- matic revision of the a.ssessments. There might be pro- perties which had become mere valuable, and lost sight of by the Committee, and which should be brought under the view of the Committ(e, It seemed to him that it ought to be done.</p>
        <p>The Call% sai-d-the—Committee was constantly ap- proving of supplemental lists. The overseers were sup- posed to revise the assessments in their own townlhips and take notice of any alteration that took place in the rental of property. He knew Mr. Tunstall did. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said admitting that the overseers did their duty he thought thia committee stood in a position of responsibility. Mr. HICKM&amp;N Do you suggest that there should be a re-assessment of the whole union.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said he did not Profess to have any technical knowledge of the work of the committee but it seemed to him that a systematic revision of the assess- ments ought to take place. The CHAIRMAN: The committee has revised the assessments of licensed houses and shop property. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY : When. Mr. Owns (valuer) : Public-houses were re-assessed in 1894, and shop property in 1891. Mr. BILLINGS said the valuation lists of Newton, Earlestown, and Haydock were quite up to date. In a large district like Warrington.. it might be possible that some properties escaped revision. The CLERK thought the subject had been very properly introduced by Mr. A. J. Jolley and he thought the mem- bers of the committee might quietly consider before the next meeting whether there was room for a more strict revision of the present assessments. With regard to the commotion that had been created with reference to the assessment of public-houses be was quite sure that the committee had done justice to licensed premises and that the principle upon which they had assessed was the maximum principle. Of course in all classes of property there was some material change in the assessments ne- cessarily due to growth of populatioa or trom other causes,and taking them all round the principle adopted by the committee was the maximum. It had been stated that in one or two places there had been a professional re-valuation of the assessments of licensed houses where- by many had been increased. He believed that had been Ithe case at Accrington. He would, if the committee liked,go over and inspect and report upon them. The Cain/maw said if the committee thought any particular class of property required revising they had a full month to think it over and devise what was the best course to adopt. Mr. BILLINGE said personally he was not alarmed at 'what had been going through the press. He should allow it to lie, and let the assessments ne revised in the usual and ordinary manner. The subject then dropped,and the meeting terminated.</p>
