      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>FIRE AT MESSRS RESKETH AND DUTTON'S Wonxs. On Saturday afternoon it was discovered that a fire had broken out on the premises of Messrs. Iles- keth and Dutton, cabinet makers, &amp;c., Bewsey- street. The workmen on the premises extinguished the flames, the services of the Fire Brigade not being required.—A false alarm of fire was given by someone ringing the bell on Sunday morning at an early hour. Some firemen turned out, but they soon ascertained that the alarm was a false one. THE FIRE BRIGADE AT THE WORKHOUSE. The members of the Fire Brigade under the direc- tion of the superintendent (Mr. Turner), on Satur- day afternoon attended at the Workhouse to practice, and to test the fire appliances at that insti- tution. The practice which was successfully gone through, wa very useful. There was a good supply s of water, sufficient for all purposes.</p>
        <p>MAY-DAY PROCESSION AT WARRINGTON. The usual May-day processions this year took place on Monday, in consequence of the let May falling on Sunday. The weather which was good most part of the day broke slightly later on in the day,though therein did not continue. Both public and private companies dressed their horses with artifi- cial flowers and gaily coloured ribbons for the occasion. One or two well known firms in the town had independent processions through the streets displaying their ,200d5 and manufactures on the carts and lurries. Altogether greater interest seems to have been shown in the day than on pre- vious OCCasions. The London and North-Western Railway Company made a special effort, and formed a procession of 25 horses and lurries. Not only were the horses splendidly decorated, but their fine physique attracted general admiration. A fine black horse known as "Sammy" was a perfect pic- ture of symmetry. The carters had all made great efforts to turn their horses out to the b-st ad vantage and under the direction of Mr. R. Dawson, they suc- ceeded. The Warrington Slate Company paraded nine horses and vehicles, all being dressed and ornamented to perfection. A large number of people journeyed to Knutsford by road and rail to May-day procession there. The uisford make the let of May quite inhabitants nt s e oufs usual a gala day. People visit linutsford from all parts of the country round about. It is a pity the aavent of summer is not signalized in a more co-operative manner in the towns, at the occasion is worthy of celebration,</p>
      </div>
