        <p>*</p>
        <p>DEA.TEC OF A. CH/LD FROM BITES&amp; On Tuesday evening the coroner, Mr. Brighouse, held an inquest at the Police Station, Ham-street. Itouching tne death of Ethel Bramhall, 95, Chorley- street, aged 2 years and two months, the daughter of Eliza and John Bramb all. The death took place at the Infirmary on Tuesday morning from the re- sult of burns. The mother said in answer to the coroner's inquiries, that on Saturday the 23rd April, she was in the house and heard a scream, and then a terrible scream, andn rushing to the entry close by, found her child in flames. She was removed to the Infirmary. Some half burnt paper and matches were found, the day after, in the entry. Mr. Brain.. hail did not know bow the child had got hold of the matches. She bad not got them out of the house. Witness was in the Infirmary ward when. the child died at 12.15 on Tuesday morning. The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death."</p>
        <p>SHOP ASSISTANTS. A public meeting of the Warrington Shop Assis- tants was held in the Druid's Hall on Tuesday night. There was a fair attendance. Mr. Ir. R. Roberts took the chair in the absence of Mr. T. Pugh through illness. The object of the meeting was to make the advantages to be derived from joining the National 'Onion of Shop Assistants more generally known. Mr. F. R. Roberts Mr. Seddon, and Mr. C. Morris spoke on behalf of the Union. The shop assistants of Warrington are not united. It is the desire of the Union to form a branch in ' this town. Some of the benefits the assistants would receive from the society are sick pay and out of I work payments, It is also the object of the society to shorten the hours of labour, and to improve the condition of shop life generally. NNW RAPID CYCLES.—GARLAND, 51, Brid,ze.btreA, Waraington. VISIT OF THE REV, F. C. ll.A.rmo. The Sunday School Anniversary services of the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, were held on Sun- day. The Rev. F. C..Haitne.a former minister of the circuit, preached twice, In the n,orning to a good congregation, he spoke upon the eternal love of God, from the words "It was a river I could not pass over, for the waters were risen, waters to swim in, a river that could that could not be passed over." He wished them to consider this river, to look at its water, its nature, and its flow, lie pointed how in its nature it was sacred, saving and divine, and in its flow it issued from the throne of God. In the afternoon there was a musical service. The Rev. G. Harbottle presided, and explained that owing te the continued indisposition of Mr. Dodds be was unable to take part in the service. Mrs. D. S. Stens was beard te advantage in the solo " The Sweet Story," and solos were also given with good taste by Mr. A. E. Baker and Mr. Gough. The anthems, " Weary of Earth," and" Lead, kindly Light," were rendered by the choir, the scholars contributing several hymns In the evening to a crowded chapel the Rev. F C. Hahne preached from the words "The Lord God hath given me the tongue of the learned, that I should know how to speak a word in season to him that is weary." He dwelt upon the thon,ht that Christ took upon Himself our &amp;lib, and for 30 years he was receiving a train- ing that He might know how to speak saving words to those who were weary, and that he might be a sympathetic helper to them. Great praise is due to Mr. Baker for the excellent singing of the scholam. Mr. B. Wadsworth accompanied on the organ. The collections for the day amounted t 0.013. CYCLING and TOURING ROAD MAPS.--omr S. 'GREEN. Stationer, kis.. 31, Bridse-screet.Warrington. STRIXE OP BRICKLAYERS' LLBOURERS. About 100 bricklayers labourers' came out on strike at Warrington on Monday. They are asking an increase of their wages from 61 to 6fi. per hour. The masters offered them 611 per hour, but the men declined it. At present the building trade is very brisk, and the joiners and bricklayers have already received an advance, and the painters are negotia ting for a rise in their wages. This strike has affected the bricklayers, as they cannot work with- out the hod-carriers. A meeting of the hod-carriers was held on Monday night, but no settlemont was arrived at. Several firMF, have intimated their in- tention to concede the advance. PREMIRR CYCLES, special terms.-44.11LAND, Agent Warrington.</p>
        <p>P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.C.A. Last Sunday a very good meeting was held. Mr. E. Osborne presidsd and gave a short and interest- ing address. Mr. Frank Hickman played a piano- forte solo. The speaker was Mr. C. Roylance. His address on 'Search the Scriptures" was thoroughly enjoyed. Messrs. Hurst Taker, G. B. Holford, and. Gregson, also took parr in the meeting. STYLISH SPRING MILLINERY, DRESS FABRICS, TRIM- MINGS, BLOUSES, AC.. AC. "DRESSMAKING A SPECIAL- ITY." Charges moderate.—W. T. WILLIAMS. Saw. ey- street. LeTh-street, Warrin ,ton. SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION. This association held their quarterly meting in Holy Trinity Schools on Monday evening. There was a large attendance. The Rey E.C.E.Carleton presided. Mrs. enubb, of Liverpool, gave a brief address, and Mr. Powell. of Liverpool, followed with a " Model Lesson." Many of the questions were answered with readiness by the boys. A discussion was taken up by the clergy and teachers. and a rots of thanks, on the proposition of Mr. A .J. Jolley, seconded by Mrs. Stansfield, was passed to Mr. Powell for his services.</p>
        <p>EXCELSIOR It n d NEW HOWE CYCLE3.—GARLAND Ironmonger, Warrington. SPIRITUALISM. Miss Smith gave an address on Sunday at the weekly meeting of spiritualists, upon the ancient idea of the seven souls of man,tracing its origin. in the evening she dealt with the principal items in I the philosophy of Spiritualism in relation to this; life and the life to come." At both meetings she gave clairvoyance in her usual incisive manner. Monday evening was devoted entirely to clairvoy- ance and physchometry, in the latter of which she gave some striking tests. There were good audi-• ences at all the meetings.</p>
        <p>One of the most interesting of tho reports yet issued by the historical Manuscripts Commission' appeared on Wednesday in the form of a first instalment of collations from manuscripts in the Welsh language. There are forty-two such manu- scripts at Mostyn Hall, among the most noteworthy- being a kind of autobiography by one Ellis Griffith, a native of Llacasaph in Flintshire and a " soldier of Calais" in the reign of Henry the Eighth. Many historic events of the period are described by the author in vivid detail. His account of the coronation of Anne Boleyn and of the coming of Anne of Cleves to Calais. and thence to England, with Henry running to have a look at her by the way, clad in a coat of grey velvet, with hat of the same material, on which there was a gold chain for a band, presents living pictures full of those charm- ing personal touches which make much of the his- according to the editor, Professor J. G. Evans, the most readable prose in the Welsh language after Ifabinogwn. We do nqt merely see the pageantry and hear the huzzas, but we seem to mix with the crowd and lePrn their mind. •</p>
      </div>
