        <p>WARRINGTON, GRAPPENHALL, AND LATCHFORD, VALUABLE FREEHOLD AND LEASEHOLD PllO- - TO BE SOLD BY LUCTION BY MESSRS. 111031 AS SUTTON AND SON, at the BLUE BELL INN, WARRINGTON, on WEDNESDAY, the 18th Day of MAY, 1898, at 6 o'clock in the evening* punctually, subject to conditions of sale to be then read and which can be seen at the Offices of the Vendors' Solicitors for seven days prior to the sale on the following or such other Ices as may be then a etermined. LOT I.—SIX MIISSUAGES or DWELLING-HOUSES situate on the easterly side of and being Nos. 95 to 105 inclusive in Dallam-lane, Warrington, now in the occupation of Messrs. Ryder, Yeomans, Hough, Brown, Barnes and Barnes at weekly gross rentals amounting to £BO 12s. per annum. Contents 679 square yards or thereabouts. Leasehold for 1000 years from 11th March 1857 subject to a yearly ground rent of £5 Ws. 2el</p>
        <p>LOT 2.—Firstly, FOUR MESSUAGES or DWELLING- HOUSES Nos. 18, 20, 22 and 24 Bewsey-road, Warrington, with the site thereof containing 592 f square yards or there- abouts now in the occupation of Messrs. Crookes, Morrell, Shaw, and Davies, at weekly gross rentals amounting to £72 16s. per annum. Secondly, a receivable chief rent of £6 17s. Bd., issuing ow of an adjoining plot of laud contain- ing 413 square yards or thereabouts and the 3 messuages erected thereon being Nos. 1,2, and 3in Walker-street, reserved on an underltase for 990 years from Ist May 1873, together with the reversion of such underlease. Leasehold for 999 years from Ist May 1869, subject to a yearly ground rent of £l2 lie. 4d.. which by receipt of the £6 171. Bd. will be reduced to £5 13s. Bd. _ _</p>
        <p>Lom 3.—The MESSUIGE or DWELLING HOUSE, No. 6 bewsey-road, Warrington, now in the occupation of Mr. F. Massey, with the lawn in front thereof. Contents in- cluding halves of adjoining streets 517 1-3 square yards. Leasehold for 993 years from 7th September 1845 subject to an apportioned ground rent of £3. LOT 4.—Firstly. FIVE MESSUAGEI9 or DWELLING HOUSES Nos. 7,8, 9.10, and 10A, Mersey Mount, Mersey- street, Warrington, in the occupation of Messrs. Simpkin, Isherw od, Kermode. Pemberton. and Church. at weekly gross rentals amounting to £7O 17s, per annum, Contents 825 square yards or thereabouts. Secondly, the receivable ground rents amounting to £lOl.. per annum payable in res- pect of Lot 6, as hereinafter mentioned. Leasehold for 999 years from 24th March 1840 and will be sold subject to an apportioned yearly ground rent of £l6 which by the receipt of the £lO la will be reduced to £5 19s.</p>
        <p>LOT S,—LARGE Woil-KS— in --ir—OSE AND CROWN STREET, Warrington in the occupation cf Messrs. Lawless. Jam Manufacturers, under a lease for the residue of a term of 10 years from the let September 1894 at the yearly rent of £2O together with the large chimney adjoining, the flue of which is used by Mr. Thomas Hmchinsuu, for which me he pays 19s. per annum. Contents 468 square yards or thereabouts, An acknowledgement of Is. per annum is paid to Mr. Hutch- inson fur the use of a wall belonging to him. Leasehold for 999 years from 24th Mar.) 1840 and will be sold subject to an apportioned yearly ground rent of £4lO. Lop 6.—WHEELWRIGIIT'S SHOP, No. 50. Mersey-street. Warrington, in the occupation of Messrs. Heywood Bros., at the yearly rental of £lO, and 6 cottages or dwelling-houses NOS. 1,2, 3, 4.5. &amp; 6, Mersey-terrace, Mersey-street, War- rington. in the occupation of Messrs. Slevin, Howard, Mealor, butch, Brown, and Baker, at weekly gross rentals amounting to £55 ss. per annum. Contents. 554 square yards or there- abouts. This lot is held under unde -leases as to 573 yards part thereof for 890 years. from 2nd December. 1857, at the yearly rental of £9 lie. ; and as to 21 -yards the remainder thereof for 890 years from the sarne date at the yearly rent of 10s., making together £lO ls. per annum. LOT 7. —All that SMALL FREEHOLD ESTATE, compris- ing a messuage or dwelling-house known as "Vine Cottage," situate in Grappenhall. near Warrington, with the gardens, I stables, vinery, greenhouses and land, mounted therewith e-mtaining together 2 acres, 0 roods. S 5 perches statute measure or thereabouts, now occupied by Mr. Charles Houghton as yearly tenant at the rent of £45 per annum. This Lot Is situate on the northerly side of the Chester road and comprises a considerable quantity of land suitable for the erection of a large number of good dwelling-houses the land having a frontage of 157 yards or thereabouts to the highway adjoining the same. I LOTS 8.9, 10, 11.--EIGHT MBSSUAGES or DWELLING I HOUSES situate In Knutsford-road. Latchford, being Nos. 327 to 341 will be offered in Lots of 2 Houses each. Rents Is, per week for each house. LOT 12. A PLOT of BUILDING LAND, adjoining No. I 327, sufficient for the erection of two houses, Lots 8 to 12 are Lemeholl for 995 years, from 24th February. 1877, and each I lot will be sold subject to an apportioDed ground rent of £4 Bs. per year.</p>
        <p>LOTS 13 TO 21 —EIGHTEEN MESSUAGES or DWELL- INCI HOUSES situate in Welington-street, Warrington, being NOR. 33 to 71, (exclusive of Nos. 4L and 43). will be offered in Lots of two houses each. Rents 4s. per week for each House. Lots 13 to 21 are Laasehold for 985 years from 25th March, 89 Each of these Lots will be sold subject to an apportioned g:c., :id rent, viz :-5 of them to £1 16s. each, per year. and 4 0 them to £1 18s. 4d. each, per year. All the above mentioned Leasehold Lots will be sold subject to the covenants conditions and exceptions contained in the respective Leases and Underleases comprising them, and to all such rLshts as to party walls and easements if any as may respectively effect the same. Two-thirds of the purchase money of any lot (or such other sum as may be arranged) may be allowed to remain on snort gage if the purchaser shall so desire. For further particulars apply as to the first 7 lots to Mr. FREDEttICIC MASSEY, 16, Bewsey-mad, Warrington, and as to all the lots to the Auctioneers, Leigh-street, Warring- ton, or to the Vendors' Solicitor MESSES. JEANS AND SON, 7, Brazennose-street. Manchester. and 25, Winwick-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>pITTS'S, LONDON ROAD, MA NCHESTE R. Just past Station. through Railw ty Arch . THE LARGEST AND MOST CONVENIENT GENERAL FURNISHING ESTABLISHMENT IN THE KINGDOM, Electric Light Throughout, Passenger Lifts to all Floors. " In the spring time a young man's faney lightly turns to thoughts of love," and having tot nudely obtained his financee's commit to marriage, the important question, 'Where shall we Furnish ?" presents itself. Both the young People have been very forcibly impressed by the magnificent display in Pitts'a colossal showrooms, together with the genuine ring in Pitta's adveitisements and the uniform good reputation Pitta's bear, but before definitely deciding they very wisely conclude to refer the matter to their parents' superior judgment and experience. Upon broaching the subj-ct the unhesitating reply is " Certainly, Furnish at Pitts's, like we did nearly thirty years ago. You see the goods are little worse for wear after all these years. Pitts's occupy the uncommon position oi having often furnished three consecutive generations, parents, children, and grandcaildren, and you cannot possibly d better than place the order with that old-established fl in. Result—another home made bright and comfortable at Ittle cost by Pitts's, to the mutual satisfaction of all concerned. PITTS'S COMPLETE HOUSE OF FURNITURE for 151- guineas, worth £35; includes a splendid serviceable suite in leather, dining table. i ender and brasses complete, capital Brussels carpet, with hearthrug to match, window fittings, to. Kitchen includes strong Windsor chairs, strong table with heavy white top, nine yards of seasoned oilcloth. hearthrug, fender and Irons complete. Bedroom has all requisite articles ' • massive bedsteads, mattresses, bed, bolster, and two p!IlOws (guaranteed new and clean), carp, t, toilet ware, &amp;a., all comnlete and full size. Other houses of furniture at 221, 28i, 38i. 50, 100, up to 1.000 guineas Hach honestly worth d ble the price asked. Full particulate, :testimonials, and press opinions on applioatoin. Cash prices quoted. Deferred dayment by arrangement. DITTS'S—ENTIRI: CONTENTS OF SEVEN-ROOMED JL HOUSE. origina lv cost £lB3, for 75 gulusaa. The dining.room Is in morocco leather, the drawing-room is in rosewood, a spare room upstairs fitted as a smokeroom Is in saddlebags. Furniture complete for kitchen, hall, stairs. landing, cellars, to. The first bedroom Is In American Walnut, including wardrobe, dressing table, washstand, I chairs, &amp;c, bedsteads, bedding, carpet, rug, and toilet ser- vice. The second bedroom is similarly furnished, but in ash, and the third bedroom Is in mahogany. Uadoubtedly the greatest bargain ever offered.</p>
        <p>F- ITTS'S SPECIAL BAROAINS.—DININO—ROOM SUITE handsomest in Manchester, upholstered throughout with curled hair (guaranteed). timall Chairs, padded backs ; Magnificent Sideboard,fitted with bevelled glasses throughout; Brackets, Shelves, Cupboards, CellareLtes, ; Extending Table, with patent screw ; Overmantel, Carpet, Brass fender, and Presses; originally cost £l3O ; Pitt's price 62 guineas. Drawing-room Suite. Chippendale style, solid rosewood, beautifully inlaid with marqueterie work, upholstered throughout with curled hair (guaranteed), and covered in rich Genoa velvet ; Magnificent Cabinet and Overrnantel, Occasional. Wine, and Card Tables, all inlaid to match ; Car- pet, Rug, Brass Fender and Brasses. Clock and Ornaments, com olete; cost £175; Pitts's price 60 guineas. Bedroom Suite solid throughout, consists of a very large Wardrobe, with bevelled glass doors, drawers, cupboards. .itc.; Pair of large Toilet Tables, fitted, with every convenience ; Pedestal Towel Rail, and Chairs complete ; a superb Suite ; originally cost £B5; eitts's price 35 guineas. Other bargains on view, , equally goad value. PITTS British make. Largest, best, and cheapest stock. As. tounding value. Grandest display ever st_.en. Nothing similar elsewhere. 13IITS'S Piano End of Season Sale.—Entire Stock by Collard, Brard, Kirkman. 8:0- ; must be cleared out totally, irrespective of what they may fetch, now is your chance to save thirty to forty per cent. ;cash or deferred. IDITTS'S have scoured the entire Cycle Market; in order to obtain time Best Machines for the Least Money, and they are now showing them In their windows ; splendid selec tion, dozen different makes ; light, attractive. easy-running- up-to-date ; year's guarantee ; exceptionally low prices, cash or deferred.</p>
        <p>13ITTS'S BASSINETTES, Lamps, China, Glass, Rarthen- ware.—Magnlficent Selection in Show Windows. Plessure to see it.</p>
        <p>PI1To"8 FOR OFFICE AND LIBRARY FURNITURE Every description of Bookcases. Secretaries, Desks, Stools, Chairs, Safes, Copying Presses at about Ina city prices.</p>
        <p>SAMUEL PITTS. Established 1845. LONDOII-ItOAD (just past Station), Manchester, Ardwick. Longslaht, and Belle Vue Cars pass the door.</p>
