      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>ELIATINER,</head>
        <p>THE ELIATINER,</p>
        <p>MAY 6th, 1898.</p>
        <p>THE war between the United a indh a AWf taerr. States and Spain has taken a more interesting turn this week. There has been some real downright fighting between the opposing forces, but up to the time of writing the exact results of the conflict are not known. Spain has two principal colonial possessions, both of which she misgoverns in the good old Spanish manner. One of them is Cuba, and her misman- agement of that island has led to the pr esent hos- tilities ; the other is composed of the Phillipin e Islands named after one of the monarchs who ruled over the Spanish Empire at the time of its greatest power. Just as Cuba is, naturally, one of the fairest portions of the globe, fully deserving its title of the " Pearl of the Antilles," so the Phillipine Islands are amongst the choicest parts of the Asiatic Continent. Both of these veritable Edens have suffered severely from the blight of Spanish rule and a portion of the inhabitants of each of these distant possessions have been forced into that final protest of an oppressed people, an armed insurrection. Curiously enough, while the main, and indeed the sole, object of the United States is to liberate Cuba, the, first really serious blow that has been struck at Spain by the American fleet has been given so far away as Manilla the capital of the Phillipines. The exact nature of that blow is, as we have said, not yet known, but there seems to be little doubt that the Spaniards have suffered a very serious reverse. Their Asiatic fleet appears to have been destroyed, and it may be that the government of the islands has been entirely taken out of their hands. What the ultimate consequences of the latest development of the strife may be it would be useless to specu- late. They may possibly be of far-reaching im- portance. The best thing that could happen at the present juncture would be the growth of a conviction in the minds of those responsible for Spanish policy that they are engaged in a hopeless contest. That might at all events lead to a speedier conclusion of the war than, ha s been</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>generally expected. On the other hand, howeve the American capture of the Phillipine Islands may produce still further complications. There may, for instance, be a revolution in Spain. The news of the reverse has already caused rioting in Spanish towns, and the Spanish throne has on more than one occasion during the present century . i been tumbled over on very short notice. An I attempt to eirpel the Queen-Regent and her son, the youthful King of Spain, who are closely allied 1 with the royal house of Austria, might lead to European intervention in Spankh affairs, the- end of which no man could foresee. If the Queen- Regent were deposed Nom power 'no doubt an effort would be made to establish a Republic. It has, however, been made pretty clear by some recent diplomatic incidents that there are on the Continent powerful influences-at work against the' extension of political freed omr and it is not at all unlikely that these influences might be brought- to bear against the Spanish people if, owing to 1 ,1 popular discontent, they were to display any , strong desire to change their form, of government. , Outs!de interference of that kind is always bitterly resented, and in this way what is until now a rather uneventful war between Spain and the United States may. begin to affect European politics in a way that would not tenth towards a reduction of the area of strife. In another way the conquest of the Phillipines might have an adverse effect upon the peace of the world. It is almost certain that the Americans Tr:11 have no desire to retain these islands as a colonial posses- sion. Their own continent is quite large enough to allow of full scope to their expansive energies for many years to come yet,and the Phillipines would form an awkwardly distant dependency. But if the Americans eject the Spanish, and do not wish . to stay themselves to whom, are they going , to hand over the islands ? The inhabitants are, it is said, altogether too divided in race sentiment amongst themselves to form a stable government of their own. There will, however, be no lack of aspirants for the honour and proit of owning these very productive islands. The danger indeed lies in the other direction. All the leading nations of Europe are eagir to acquire desirable colonial possessions, and, the Phillipine Islands might easily become a bone of contention amongst them.</p>
        <p>LORD SALISBURY did not The Evolution of Nations. give a very encouraging de- scription of the state of Euro- pean politics in his speech to members of the</p>
        <p>- - Primrose League on Wednesday. He divided the nations of the world into two classes, the living</p>
        <p>and the dying. The living are the great powers and the dying are the small powers. He believes the tendency is for the</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>great nations to become stronger, and the weak nations weaker and more insignificant. The large nations are every year growing in power, growing in wealth growing in dominion, growing in the perfection of their organisation. The result is an increasing temptation for the strong to encroach upon the weak. This process does not, in the opinion of Lord SALISBURY, con- duce to the peace of the world, and he draws a rather grimpicture of the kind of warfare that will result from a conflict between two really first- class military nations. He points out that rail- ways now give the power of concentrating troops upon the frontiers in numbers which were never dreamt of in former days. And then again science has placed in the hands of those armies weapons whichin their efficacy of destruction add enormously to the slaying powers of modern soldiers. Very naturally, therefore, Lord Sltrs- BurtY takes a gloomy view of the future and ap- peals to the "tenacity and Imperial instincts " °f the British people, a sentiment which, it is need- less to say, was received with enthusiastic ap- plause by the Primrose dames.</p>
        <p>It is rumoured every now and then that perhaps some of Spain's unlocated war-vessels may be con- templating an attack on New York or any other big city on the American sea board,as a reprisal for the disasters suffered at Manila and Matanzas. Were - such a thing accomplished it would certainly be aI master.stroke on the part of the wily Spaniard, but ; we imagine the possibility of such an attack is not , one that need very seriously alarm American i citizens. Events which have happened this week• point more to the probability of an early extinction and capture of the new Armada, aZportion of which ' has already ceased to be. But allowing that an odd 0 battleship or two might arrive off New York, unless ~J) they .get there 'very shortly, their position will most certainly be one not to he envied, what- • ever glory may be in futuro. There is now nearly completea a record great gun for the defence of New York harbour. Da Vinci's terrible conceptions are as nothing to it. In fact t is stated that a larger or more destructive war en- gine never was designed. The new gun is 49ft. 21n. in length. It weights I2G tons. The weight of the shell or projectile is 2,350 pounds, while the weight of the power which will send this monster cannon ball whizzing through the air is 1,500 pounds. Its calibre is IGin. The projectile will fly through the air for sixteen miles, at the rate of 2,000 ft. per second. At this terrific velocity it is estimated the shell:could, at a distance of five or tett miles,pierce and batter down the heaviest armoured man-of-war afloat. It would put such a vessel as - the " Vizcaya "out of action in short order, so that it may be presumed, should the Spaniards attack - New York they will more likely meet with a sacri• • Ice than a conquest.</p>
        <p>SMITH'S TASTE.I.IIISS DANDELION ANTIBLLIOUS PILLS. la Lid. and 2s. 94:1. per box (by post for 15 or 33 staml/o,' are a proved substrage for calomel. Are suited tor either eaS b" and may be taken at any time. Thoy clear the liver. stolrlo ' t) head and kidneys; free the skin m of humours, purify tb. blood, regulate also bowels wto)s comfort and safety, sites excess in eatira or drinking,. and are the most valuab,le a . change ot life, et periodical ob&amp;truction. No Blue Pill or Ma' (meals, benes no fear of ectd. Made only by Mr. SMITH, M.P.S., 2CII, Walweirt.road. London. PILOPUIETOR Dr FOWL'S (111LLIBRATEN CO HO' BUNIO', AND CHILBLAIN, PLASTER, is. lid., I box, by post for 15 stamps ; a known sure, however bad 1 MO • SMITH'S NVALUABLE PILE POWDER, AND PECTORAL FRUIT COUGH LOZENGES, BAMB PRIOBB. Bold by sil Potent Medicine Venders.</p>
