      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>RUN (.201IN ANP WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUN (.201IN ANP WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A BOAT SUNK. On Tuesday morning as the vessel "Eliza "laden with Manure, and belonging to Mr. George Hooton of Liver- was being towed up the river between Halehead and the Dungeon,it commenced to fill very rapidly and sank in a few minutes. The two men who were aboard managed to escape in a small boat. WRECK OF A RUNCORN BOUND SHIP. News reached Runcorn on Saturday that the schooner Letitia, of Bosharn, Poole, bound for Runcorn had gone Mho a'; the mouth of the Darmouth harbour, and Would probably become a wreck. The crew was saved ,1?3, the coastguard life lines. Captain F. Towes, of Boa. &amp;. was found dead in the cabin. DRUNKENNESS AT RUNCORN. At the Runcorn Police Court, before Mr. F. J. Norman ell Friday, Elizabeth Shaw was fined ss. and ss. 4d. Coats for being drunk and disorderly in Granville-street eally that morning. P.C. Wylie proved the case.—On waturday, James Evans, of Wellington-street, employed as a" lightman " on the Ship Canal, was fined ss. in- eluding costs for being drunk in Wellington-street, the Previous day.</p>
        <p>CONCERT AT RUNCORN. On Wednesday evening a grand concert was given in the Public flail, Runcorn, by the band of the training eniliP "Indefatigable" assisted by Mr. R. Jardine, negro linmorist of Liverpool, under the auspices of the Run- nortn,Seamens' Mission. There was a poor attendance Vihen the Rev. W. M. Tod, M.A., chaplin to the Train- ing Ship, took the chair. The parts taken by the boys Were well carried out and almost all of them were en- Mrs. Kenyon Davies was the accompanist to Mr. Jardine. Mr.J.P. Hiney,bandneaster,was in charge Of the lads. At the interval the Rev.W.M.Tod addressed the Company. Forme LYING BY THE FEET Of HIS HORSE. On Monday morning, a man named James Price, of 'Weston, employed as a carter by Mr. George Whitby, nOntractor, was found in an unconscious state in the toad by his horse's feet. A youth who was passing at the time seeing the man in such precarious condition Obtained assistance, and Price was removed to a stable and subsequently to his home. The man regained con- Women at periods, and when questioned he said that his horse commenced to plunge and be was in the &amp;et of jumping off the lorry when he was thrown to the Mind. No bones appeared to have been broken, but Th., man was evidently suffering from concussion of the brain. When he arrived home medical aid was knight. RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE. Mr. E. Kay, jun., presided over the usual fortniehtly Meeting of the above society held on Saturday evening i There were also present n, the Greenw ay-road schools. Messrs. D. BhhroWflE. Key, T. Whvte, J. Johnson, and J. Holt. Reference was suede to the loss the society _mid sustained by the death of Mr. William Stubbs. He had been an old and loyal member of the Order, and Various speakers bore testimony to the fidelity and con- stancy of his life. He was unobtrusive but earnest in everything he took in hand. A vote of sympathy was Passed with the bier, ved e'etives. A short address was Win given on the "Order of the S, 11/ of Temperance" by Mr. D. Bisbrown. Mr. 'thomas Whyte also spoke. PRIMITIVE METHODIST SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY. AS will be seen from an advertisement in another Column, three sermons will he preached moraine, after- noon, and evening, in the Primitive Methodist "Chapel, Greeuway-road, Runcorn, on Sunday, by the Revs. J. !!ifairi, of Oldham, and an ex-president of the Confer- ence, and S. Wright of Runcorn, also an ex-president of e a nTdh e rev. their be United Methodist Free Church Cponfaecrehnecres: gentlemen are known as powerful re Presence in the pulpit in the Greenway-read chapel, Should draw large congregations. On Monday evening a service of song entitled d Tim's Secrifiee " will be ren- dered by the scholars and choir. There are 243 scholars on the roll book, with an average attendance of 176. The income of the Sunday school during, the year has bean 35 7s 4i ,and the expenditure £37 18s showhich shows an adverse balance of £2 16s. Oid. Collections 'Will be made at each service in nid of the school fund. Other particulars will be found in another column. RUNCORN leS A. SOCIETY, AND TEE WAR. The weekly meeting of the Runcorn Bethesda P.S.A. Society, was held on Sunday afternoon in the Bethesda Chapel, there being a large attendance. Mr. George Bellamy occupied the chair. The R or, J. Osborne,B.A., Was the speaker. In the eewse of his le marks he re- ferred to the Hisnannearneriean war, and said that for two nations who not only ,e-re civilised but who were Professedly Christian natioue to be at war with each Other, was deplorable in the extreme. In no society Should this feeling be more stronger than in the P.S.A. Society. How did they leok upon war, from the point of the P.S.A. brotherhood ? The ruling idea of their society was that all men were bound together by the common tie of humanity, all to help one another with- out distinction el net innality,custom,colour, or religion, and consequently the idea of war was repugnant to them The rev, gentleman indicated some of the causes of war Which should be guarded against. For instance, love of excitement, passion for power, and false patroitism. They should try and show that the honour of a country Consisted in equil la ws,free institutions, and the develop nsent of intell,rc,. sal end moral power, and the diffusion of knowledge, jusnce, peace, and goodwill. This would Prepare tho way for the time when arbitration would take the place, of war. The address was listened to with ,eareful attention by the audience. A solo was rendered ...Ely Mr. William Gowanlock, and a recitation by Mr. sitoore. The membership of the society dow stands at BEGGING NUISANCE --EXCITING POLICE CHASE AT RUNCORN. Before Mr. F. J. Norman and Mr. E. Speakman at the nalincorn Petty Sessions on Monday, John Mullen and 'uomris Johnson were charged with begging in Bridge- Street on Satuidav evening. They were also charged 11:tith hawking wiihout a license at the same time and e Bovayer proved the charges. Prisoners were teatarnitted to Knutsford for seven days on the first saeuce and firied 5s and costs or in default an additional ten days.—Cha rim Parsonage and Henry Smith were Charged with soliciting alms at Weston Point on Sunday. ene--C. Bancroft stated that he watched the men the pre- zetens day go from door to dorirlie arrested them and was tinging them to the Polies Station when Parsonage got aWay. Witness handed the other prisoner to another nean,and he ran after the escaped prisoner for a quarter af a mile across some fields. He caught him in a house !ear Sandy-lane, where he had run in and locked the idoor after him. Witness followed him and found him trng quietly en a sofa. He terrified the occupants of e house. Witness then putnthe handcuffe on him. They trent along together for a while, and prisoner loosed himself from the cuffs. On arriving at Runcorn,Parson- ;B43 again slipped the handcuffs, but did not manage to awaY• Where searched 2s. Bid. all in copper with the exception of a threepenny piece was found on Par- nnnage, and J. in copper was found on Smith. Parson- age said he did rot try to g•et himself loosed from the ...handcuffs. His hand was very thin and it slipped off :en' easily. The officer broke his stick on his (Parson- age's) head in a thousand piezes.—Parsonage was sent gaol for 14 days and Smith was committed for seven "'an with hard labour.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB. FIXTURES AND SCORES. POINTS. FOR ARST. . Eipt. 4 -Ttechdale H. h ' 11-Tyldesley, la 18-01dham.a 20-Stockport a . . 25 -Leigh, a ' Oct. 2 . -Widnes h 2 0 • 9 -Hor-cunbe, li 2l 2 16-Altrincham, a , 23-St. Helens liens, h 30 St. H'qens, h • NOV. 6-Altrincharn, h l7 0 13-Warrington, h 5 3 . 20- , 27-Broughton 12, h 2 5 ° Dec. 4-Salrord, h 8 21 11-Tyldesley. a 5 6 * 18-Swinton, a 25-St. Helens, a 27-Stockport, h Jan. 1-Rochdale H, a 0 3 3-Broughton 11, a 0 16 8-Leigh. h 15-Salford, a 5 20 22- 29-Morecambe, a 0 0 /.135. 5-Wigan, h 12-Swinton. h ......................... .. 8 8 19-Widnes a 2 8 26-27'yers ton, a Ilar• 5-Cup Tie Brighouse Rangers a 3 3 9-Replayed lie Brighouse Rgrs hll 12-Warrington, a 19-Cup Tie-Oldham a ........... 26-4 t. Helens a 0 ...... 11 kw. 2 -013harn, 11 6 12 fl-Birkenhead W, h 16-Wigan, a 23-Widnes. a 0 2 -Birken W, a</p>
        <p>PENK—ETIT. 9rtßaT SANKEY Boast ScHooL.—The monthly meeting Lne Great Sankey School Board was held on Tuesday oon,when there were present—Messrs, T. Halton tl,le chair), J. Wright, T. Perks, W. D. Jeans, jun. t).G. Clarke (schoolmaster) and B. Shearer (attend. ° officer). The treasurer's account showed a balance nand of £167 124. 5.1. The following is the school- Riter's report :—April 23rd, on books 327, present avetage 2E9.9; 'lnd fees, 14q. 2d. April 30th, on preqpnt 315, average 292.0, and fees 145.10 d. 40-1, linftpr</p>
        <p>14°TrIERE, DO YOU REALIZE how your little ones when their tender skins are literally on fire with tlg and turning ecz.mas and other itching, scaly, blotchy eltin and scalp humours? To know that a bath with CIITICORA. SOAP, a single anointing of 4:ECA, purest of emollient skin cures, will, in the InaitY of eas's, afford instant relief, permit rest -.11 glee beta° P, and point to a permanent and economical 11," 90 speedy) cure, and not to use them without a lea delay, as to fail in your duty. No greater an be bestowed upon alchild than a skin without - and a body nourished with pure blood.</p>
