        <p>Rrevi</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED RECREATION GROUNDS FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>THE COUNCIL ACCEPT THE DUTIE3 OF CONSERVATORS. A special meeting of the Urban District Council was held on Monday morning for the purpose of affixing the seal to the undertaking of the Council, to accept the duties of e,onservators under the regulation of the Com- mon, and also to form of consent to the Provisional Order. The vice-chairman: Mr. J. W. Lightburn, presided in the absence of Mr. S. Taylor. There were alse rresent ; Messrs. J. H. Kirkham. E. Galley. W. Handley, J. Wilson, D. Bisbrown, F. Wiswall, Fl A. Lake (law clerk), E. Marshall (secretary), and J. Wilding (surveyor). Mr. Handley moved that the seal of the Council be fixed to the documents referred to. Mr. Biihrown considered that the Council ought to have some information as to the cost of the road. This road was part of the undertaking, and it seemed that they were in the dark as to the cost of it. He strongly ',retested against the manner in which they were pro- ceeding with the matter. Mr. Lake said the road was not provided for by these documents. It was dealt with ins separate agreement with the Marquis of Cholmondeley. Mr. Wilding said the cost of making a macadam raad would be £3OO. Mr. Bisbrown said he did not object in any cantan- kerous spirit. He thought it would be an advantage to the town to have a recreation 'ground, but what iae did ebject to was the cost. They could not afford to spend the money at the present time. The cost of the road was put down at £3OO and the cost of the Provisional Order at £lOO, making a total of £4OO. They could not spread the amount over a certain number of years, and the town would be saddled with the expense. For that reason he could not give a silent vote on the qnestion. Mr. Handley said there was one view of it which be thought Mr. Bisbrown bad net looked at fairly. That was this—they would lose money if they did not go on with the scheme. They would lose £lOO, because they would not escape the costs of obtaining the Provisional Order and the Marquis of Cholmondeley's costs. The advantages to be gained by acquiring the common rights were very important indeed to his mind. They would have what they had been looking forward to for many years in Runcorn,viz.,a recreation ground for the public of Runcorn. There was very little land to be got in Runcorn, because it was nearly all laid out, or was in- tended to be laid out, for building purposes,and it could only be obtained at a very prohibitive price. There was no doubt that as Runcorn became larger the necessity of haying some open space for recreative purposes would become very much greater. Under this Provisional Order they got a recreation ground as well as an alter- native road to Frodsham. That would be a very great advantage indeed to his mind. They all knew the difficulty there was of mounting Halton Hill and taking that road to Frodsham. It would be a very much shorter cut across the Common, and it would be a continuation of the policy that was initiated over 20 years ago, when Norman road was made. They knew at the present time that at the foot of Rock- savage slope every vehicle was stopped, and had to pay a sixpenny toll. As soon as this road was madewand the District Council took it over, this toll on the turnpike would be abolished for ever. They would then have a mote direct and better road than the: one round by Halton. He thought that was a very great advantage, and would far outweigh the cost which they were asked to contribute. If they could get the money spread over 30 years he thought the whole cost would be something under £2O a year upon the rates of Runcorn. That would be some- thing less than half a farthing in the £ per year. It was very much like the tobacco tax, they could scarcely estimate or divide it. The Chairman seconded the motion and it was car- ried, Mr. Bisbrown being the only member who voted against it.</p>
        <p>THE LALE MR. GEORGE MOSS, OF RUNCORN. THE FUNERAL. _ _</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon the funeral of the late Mr. Geo. Moss,of Greenway-road, Runcorn,took place at the Run- corn Cemetery. A procession was formed at the deceased gentleman's late residence. The coffin was met at the door of St. Michael's Church, by the Rev. Canon Wood,and the Rev. H. C. Moyle,who recited the opening ' sentences of the burial service. When all had been ' seated the 3911 Psalm was read. The lesson was taken , by the Rev. H. C. Moyle. Led by the clergy,the proces- sion moved to the Cemetery, where the Vicar ' concluded the service at the graveside. Those ' present included :—Messrs J W Ligh' burn, T Lea, T Simister, A Jordan, W Hough, T Bowyer. C Pownall, S Johnson, R Morris, W Handley, G Howard, B Crosby, D Bisbrown, J. Pritchard, T Fl Art- nett, W Walton, P Riles. W Nickson, F Ellison, G MC- lington, R Woodland,J Horsefield, 11 Morris, F J Norman Joseph Littler, J Brown, W F Massey, John Littler, Frank Rutter, J E Meredith, J H Weston, W Heap, W Fisher, Dobson, E C Hicks, R Abel, A Abel, T Hill, W Hankinson. H Branch, Joseph Tudor, Jones, J F A Lake,Captain Davies, Messrs. W Abram,W R. Was, J Cooper, W Kirkham, R R and F W Clarke, M Barker, William Mellor, Arthur Salkeld, Joseph Salkeld, E New- ton, James Collier, Dr Robinson, U Summerfield, Peter Davies, Charles Phillips, T 9 Burgess, F Hatton, J Percival, E Ashton, David H Dunning, Captain Henry Hill. The mourners were—Messrs G E Moss (son), P Moss. VV Harrison, Atther Harrison, Charles Walker, W Mellor, Dunning, L Rosenberg, gen., L Rosenberg, jun., W Sutton, Goodwin, C Ditchfield, and F J Moulton. A number of beautiful wreaths wore contri- buted by Mr and Mrs Hough, Mr and Mrs T C Price, Miss M D Robb and staff of Mill-brow School, Mr and Mrs Mellor, L and A. Rosenberg, Mr Charles Ditchfield, the teachers and scholars of Victoria-road Board Schools, Mr W Sutton, the teachers and scholars of the Bruns- wick and Granville-s reet Schools. REFERENCES TO THE DEATH.</p>
        <p>In the course of his sermon at Runcorn Parish Church on Sunday morning the Rev. H. C. Moyle referred to the death of Mr. Moss, and a letter has been received from Mr. E. M. Sneyd Rynnersley, her Majesty's Inspector of I Schools, expressing his sympathy with the family, and stating that Mr. Moss had always done his work well at Runcorn,and he had always found him both straight- forward and manly. At the monthly meeting of the Runcorn School Board, on Tuesday evening, at the Town Hall, reference was I made to the death of the late clerk to the Board (Mr. George Moss). Mr. J. Pritchard was in the chair, and there were also present the Rev C inon Wood, Messrs. F. I J. Norman, J.P., and E. Imison, C.C. The Chairman said it was his duty to refer to the great loss the Board I I had sustained by the death of their late clerk,Mr.George I Mose,who had occupied the position for the last eleven years. It was hie (the chairman's) pleasure to know him ever since he came to Runcorn. Mr. Moss was selected out of a number of candidates to fill the position of clerk to that Board. In his dealings with him he had always found Mr. Moss courteous and willing to assisc in advancing the cause of elementary education in the town. Since hi 3 appoint- ment in 1887 the duties of clerk had been greatly in- creased, and they were all well aware of the chse atten- tion he gave to his business, and of the faithful way he discharged the duties appertaining to the office.—The Chairman moved the following resolution;—" That the Board recognizing the great loss that it has sustained by the death of Mr. G. Moss, late clerk to the Board, desire to exprese their sincere :sympathy with the family, and rev est the Chairman to convey the same to Mrs. MOSS." —Mr. Imison seconded the resolution whinh was carried. In another column Mrs. MOSS and Family tender thanks for the sympathy shown to them in their sad bereavement.</p>
      </div>
