      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>1;`00'1,';,</head>
        <p>1;`00'1,';,</p>
        <p>DEATH AND YUNERAL OF MR. T, W. NICHOLLS, OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>This week it is again our sad duty to record a death of a well-known local resident, Mr. Thomas Wm. Nicholls, of Wellington-street, who died on Saturday after a long illness. Dmeased was extremely well-known a d re- spected in the town. He had occupied the position of sidesmen at the Parish Church for a number of years, and was also prominently connected with the Lily of the Valley Odd fellow Society,and had held the office of trus- tee for 30 years. He was also a past officer of the lodge and district, and had represented them at the annual movable conferences of the Order. In 1893 he was pre- sented by the members and officers of the lodge with a splendid photograph of himself in recognition of his valuable sorvicee. The funeral took place on Tuesday afternoon at the Runcorn Cemetery. A e.rocessiou was formed at the deceased gentleman's late residence and proceeded to the Parish Church. where they were met at the gates by the Rev. Canon Wood, Revs. W. Scholee and H. C. Moyle and a stir. pliced choir. As they entered the church the opening fientences of the burial service were recited. The choir ' sang the 39th Psalm and the lesson was read by the Rev. W. Scholes. The choir and congregation then joined in singing" Fight the good Fight." The proces- sion VVI9 then re-formed and marched up Wellington-st., sand Greenway-rd.to the Cemetery,in the following order —Members of the Lily of the Valley Lodge, including Messrs J Robinson,J Busheli.R Jones, R Williams,W Hol- ford,J Curzon, W R Wan, J Brown,T Strineer.ll Brown, JRily, T Carruthers, T Parry, W Ford, S RoYlance, W Reece, M Edwards, T Wilson, J Harlingen, R Evans, A Derbyshire, J Cooper, II O'Neild, W Littler, T Stringer, S Littler, W Ward, P Drown; church officials and choir ; carriage containing the Rev Canon Wood (chaplain to the Lodge) ; hearse and bearers viz., Messrs S Harrison, P Thompson, S Welker, J Sherlock, S Sherlock, and T Sherlock ; the mourners, including Messrs James Nicholls (brother), W Worthington, Davies (Widnes), D O'Neil, 11 Jones, J Hole, W Hodgetts, J Siddeley, J Peters, S Boyers, 3 Veil, J Withington, and Honghton (Daresbury.) On arrival at the Cemetery the burial ser- vice was concluded by the Rev. Canon Wood, and the choir sang "On the Resurrection Morning. Wreaths were sent by Mrs. Wass, the officers and,members of the Lodge, and Messrs Sherlock Bros.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 6 1•48.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 6 1•48.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF MR. KNIVETON OF</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The mortal remains of the late Mr. Kniveton were in- terred in the Runcorn Cemetery amid a large concourse of sorrowful and sympathising friends on Friday after- noon. A procession was formed at the deceased gentle- man's !ate residence,beaded by the Revs.C.W.L.Christien (superintendent of the Runcorn Wesleyan Circuit), J. A. B. Malvern, and it proceeded down Greenway-road and High-street, to St. Paul's WesLevan Chapel where a ser- vice was held. The church was draped in black out of respect to the deceased. The Rov.C.W.L. Christie° read the 39h Psalm, and the lesson was taken by nig Rev . J.A.B.Malvern. After the singing of a hymn the Rev. C.W.L. Christien ascended the pulpit and delivered an address. At the close of the service the organist (Mr. W. Cooke) Played the "Desd March." The procession was re-formed and moved on to the cemetery. Among those present were—Messrs. W Handley,A. R Norman, Thomas Boots, Barley, J F Moulton, J Lunt, J Nickson, E Marshall, F J Norman, D H Jones,C Littler,W Simpkin, 1 3 Shaw, W Morris,(representing the Co-operative Society),Green- way Millington, James Leat D. Bisbrown, r Simister, R Rushton, S Coventry, J Unsworth, A Unsworth, G Florsefield. R Rawlinson, J Ball, T Lea, T Cooke, J D Jones, R Dyer, J Higgins, S Walton, G Bellamy, T Wat- son, J Leadstone, W Rushton, J Marsh, Joseph Smith (Liberal agent for the Knutsford Division), J Corf, Cap- tain Andrew Millinger, Messrs. J Bowyer, H. Linthert (Frodsham), J Gregory, W Kirkham,W Mellor, J Dobson R Morris, W Pendlebury, H Ackerley, Jonathan Horse- field, W Stsnway, and E Clarke (Barton). The mourners included : Messrs. C and E Kniveton, E Pearson (son- in-law), the deceased's two trethers, Messrs. W A Mil- linger, B Crosby, J Corns, J Bell, W Cooke,W H Starkey (I,verpool), and Ralph Peters (ryldesley). Oe arrival at the grave the burial service vPs read by the Rev. C W L Christien,and those present joined in singing' Rock of Ages," A number of choice wreaths was contributed by the family and friends. The deceased was 56 years of age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>MONTHLY MEETING. APPOINTMENT OF A TEMPORARY CLERK.</p>
        <p>- - The monthly meeting of the above Board was held on Tuesday evening, in the Board-room at the Town Hall, Mr. J. Pritchard. presiding. There were also present, the Rev. Canon Wood, Messrs. F. J. Norman, J.P., E. frnison, C.C., and Mr. G. E. Moss, (acting clerk pro tern) MR. G E .1%1055, APPOINTED CLERK:</p>
        <p>, The Rev. Canon Wood, sold it was necessary some- thing should be done to provide for the carrying on of the work of the Board in consequence of the lamented death of him whom the chairman and Mr. Imison had so kindly sp en of. He therefore proposed that Mr. George Ernest Moss, be appointed clerk to the Board from MaylOth to August 3rd, and that he be paid £25 for h s services during that period. The duties should be those as stated in the schedule passed at a special meeting held on 23rd March IBB7,and that the engagement should terminate without notice from either side on August 3rd. In moving that resolution Canon Wood said he did not know whether it was necessary to say very much, but he considered it necessary to say something. First of all he called atten- tion to the fact that what the Board were likely to do ,in this matter was unquestionably due to the apprecia- tion of the services of the late Mr. George Moss. They believed that he was emphatically a good servant of that Board, and the public for the eleven years he occupied the position. It was absolutely essential that someone should carry on the work, and as Mr. G. E. Moss was perhaps the only one who was really intimate with the work at the present time except the members of the Board,it was natural enough and in fact they were corn- ',Plied to go to him to fill the gap in his father's illness,and therefore finding he was able to fill that gap up it was only right that he should be appointed for a certain time. It should be noticed that he had been connected with the office work of the Board long before his father's illness, and he believed he was correct in saying that he had really kept the books for the last two or three years, and so he was not to be regarded as an untried man, The Board had taken care in that resolution that it should be distinctly a temporary appointment. In his opinion, he considered that if Mr, G. E. Moss was as capable as his father had been he would have the first claim on them, but if he should turn out otherwise they would have no hesitation in throwing open the appoint- ment for others who might perhaps prove themselves , more confident. In so acting he was certain that they ,would have the public atltheir back. Many persons hadex- pressed their feelings to him that such a course under such sad circumstances was a very desirable one to be taken. He also found that the teachers who were perhaps the chief persons interested in the matter had made up their minds to do all they could to help Mr. Moss clinker, the three months. The public would fully understand' that nothing had been executed in the nature of a job because not a single member had any normal interest in the appointment at all, and he felt that they were doing not only what was best for the family, hut what they hoped migh'. be the very best for the welfare of the Board and its work. The Chairman seconded the resolution. He thought the Canon had properly stated their case. It was neces- sary to make the appointment of Clerk to the Board without delay, and it was very desirable that Mr. Moss, junior, should be appointed to the position. As he had been assisting his father for some time he had no doubt gained considerable knowledge of the office work and business of the Board. The resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The :Scho'olTtQLVC-e—C7m-m-i-trt-e.e—r-e-n-o-rted that 66 parents had been summoned before the committee. Sixty appeared and proceedings were ordered to be taken against 11. The visitors' report showed that the num- ber of scholars on registers was 3,340 as against 3,388 in the corresponding month of last year, decrease 48. The average attendance was 2,842 against 2,693 in the cor- responding month of last year showing an increase rf 149. The percentage of attendance was 85, showing an increase of 55. This improvement in the average at- tendance was satisfactory. The minutes were approved of on the motion of Mr. Norman, seconded by Mr. Imison. The Chairman said that with regard to Victdria schools they had rec-ivod a communievion froqi ti- Public Works Loan Commissioners, Ile p:opoad tba•.; the seal of the Board be affixed to the request asking the Commissioners to pay the balance of the loan of £733 to the Board's treasurer,Mr. J.C. Haddock. They had pr?.- viously received £3,009 as their first instalment. Mr. Enigma seconded, and it was agreed to. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>RUNCORN CRICKET CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CRICKET CLUB.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Cricket Club open their season at home on Saturday, when they meet Sankey Wire Mills. The officiald of the Club anticipate that the season will be a successful one from every point of view. Practice games have been very well attended and it is expected that the batting averages at the close of the year will surpass those of previous years. At the head of affairs are seven- teen gentlemen who are doing all in their power to maintain the fair position that the club has obtained. The following are the fixtures of the season : FIRST TEAM Date Club Ground April 30 Walkers Brewery May 7 Sankey Wire Mills horns 14 Widnes White Rose away " 21 Walkers Brewery " 28 Sutton June 4 Prescot home " 11 Preseot . _ away</p>
        <p>" 18 Grappenhall St Wilfreds' away " 25 Weston home July 2 " 9 Granpenball St. Wllfreds' • . home " 18 Northwieh " 23 Sankey Wire Mille away " 3) Suttox ... _ __ _ . . ... . home</p>
        <p>Avg. -1 Old Tettenhallians' • ' 6 " 13 Weston 29 Northwich away .. 27 Whines White Rose SECOND TEAM Date Club Ground May 7 Pa dgate away .. 14 Widnes White Rose 21 Helsby 28 Hale Bank home June 4 Briggs 0.0 away .. 11 Halton " 18 Liverpool T.M.C.A. " 25 Halebauk July 2 Moore home .• 9 Liyerpool Y.M.C.A. " 16 " 23 Padgate hom.. " 30 Halton Aug. 1 " 6 Moore</p>
        <p>"13 Jiolby ------------ " borne 20 ------</p>
        <p>27 Widnes White Rose away The teams chosen for Saturday are as follows :—let team at Runcorn V. Sankey Wire Mills, Btturday, May 7, 1998: S Cross (captain), J. Henn, R. B. Jeacock, S. Abrams, Rev. Reiser Gore, W. E. Houah, W. Johnson, W. Hc ward, Tomlin, El. Heath,'o. Houg,hton: Second Team v. Padgate, at Padgate, Saturday, May 7th, 1898 : —F. Burrows, (captain), C. Borrows, A. Gana, B. Wright, .1. Price, W. Leathwood, P. Owen, J. Hayes, C. Pyo. G. Forrester, and T. Forshaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Y.M.C.A.—The above Association held their usual open meeting on Sunday afternoon in the Old Mission Room. The chair was taken by Mr. E. England, and the Secretary read the lessons. The soloist was Miss Woodward, and an address was given by Mr. Hickman, president of the P.S.A. in connection with the T.M.C.A. Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>RU.r CORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RU.r CORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Council was held on Thursday morning, in the Council Chamber, at the Town Hall, Runcorn, Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., presiding, There were also present :—Messrs. P. Speakman, J. p.. C.A., E. Imison, C.C.. H. J. Grine, G. H. Kirkham, E. Gatley, J. Wilson, J. Pritchard, J. W. Lightburn, Horsefield, F. Wis wall, W. Collier. D. Bisbrown. M. J.: Sweetman. W. Handley, F. A. Lake (law clerk), E. Mar- shall (secretary), and J. VVilding (surveyor). HEALTH OF THE TOWN.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer reported that the numher of deaths registered during the previous month was 33. One was an inquest case, and of the remaining 32, 3 were due to diptheria, 4 to inflamation of the kidneys, 3 to influenza, 4 to senile decay, 4 to bronchitis, and one each to chronic rheumatism, mesenteric disease,inflammation of the living membrane of the brain, convulsions, hermerr- bage, premature birth, exhausti ,n of the brain, pneu- monia, whooping cough, abscess on lung, jaundice, and diarrhoea. The 33 deaths showed an average death-rate of 22 6 tor the twelve months, and exclusive of the in- quest case of 22 per thousand of the estimated popula- tion. For the corresponding month of last year, 28 deaths and 48 births were registered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN'S WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN'S WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>It was recommended by the Water Committee that an analysis of the water be obtained quarterly,and that the sum of £l2B 33. 2d. for empties, etc., in respect of the water rate for the quarter fod,d 24th March, 1898, be written off. The committee also submitted the water engineer's annual report and supplementary reparc. The supplementary report stated that Runcorn reservoir had been cleansed. The water was run off on the 13th inst, the bulk of it being passed by means of surface channels into a manhole in Highland-road some 200 yards away from the outlet the outlet being situated in a quarry about 80 yards above Mr. Ford's barn. The water thus passed into the sewers flushed the Greenway-road length. When the level had been reduced to 14 inches, the men went in and thoroughly stirred up the 5 inches of sedi- ment at the bottom, converting the whole mass into a slurry, this being run off into the quarry above des- cribed. This work of cleansing was kept up continuously from Wednesday the 13th right through the night until Thursday at 5-30 p.m., then from Friday 15th at 6 a.m. through the night until Saturday at noon. Mr. Wilding added that he made a careful examination of the whole of the interior brickwork and found it in very good condition. During •operations the supply to the town had been continuou through the tank at the pumping station. The refilling of the reservoir commenced on the evening of Saturday, 16th inst. The water pumped in was raised from sft. 9in. on Mtraday the 18th, to 191 t. 3m. on Twos- , day, the 26th inst. The total length of time from the ' commencement of the work on the 13th, to the time when the town was supplied thr migh the reservoir again from the 19ft. 3in. height was 14 days. The tank was now shut off, and the supply was through the reser- voir. Mr. Pritchard in moving the confirmation of these minutes said it was very satisfactory to find that there was no deviation in the quality of the water of the town. The analyses had.demonstrated that it was pure and abeolutely free from any trace of contamine- tion, and it had been pronounced by the analyist as a first clase water. So they looked up on the water as free from suspicion. (Hear, hear.) Certainly it contained a few grains of cominn salt per gilloe and that Wagth rAaa , was hard for washing purposes. So far as dietary p ir- . poses were concerned each of them could form a 3.1 e- ment as to what a few grains of salt would do. It w ,s well known that a few grains of salt was necessary on human existence, and he thought they might have hat tomething which was still more objectionable than that. Taking everything together he thouent they had every reason to congratulate themselves that they had such a water. Many authorities in the country, he was certain would rejoice i they had a else water: It was a matter of great surprise to him that the question Pf the purity of the water Shoul3 have been raised, but the two analyses that had come in since, and the medical officer's report, and the latter's verbal answer to a question that there was nO infect;ous disease that could be traced to the water sup- ply, should set the public mind at rest. (Hear, hear.) Now, another very important matter was the finanoiti aspect. The water undertaking had been in the Couneil's hands q years. When they took it over Mr. Tunstall in annual ssiarlisko- his report statea' that there would not be a sufficieat ener ally ygnintga rate of jug fundrevenue t wo ipt rhaosopvuuhi tdbe aid indicatedal clo ra othhi neeinterestthe traaeetnspdpo probably otlt report, aaewnb aadl statedaltdi C.oPratot2eltdh.'ein the £ would be required annually in ad- dition to the revenise from the water to provide for the annual interest,and the sinking fund. The total amount they paid altogethee for the water works was £70.640 The annual interest on that was £2,240. In addition to that they had to provide for a sinking fund for 60 years of £530. That made a total of £2,770,and they re- quired that income from some source or other. The water works during these years had annuelly paid the amount of interest and also paid £2533 10s. 83. on the sinking fund. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Imisen rose to a point of order, He asked whether the statement of Mr. Pritchard was germane to the minutes of the Water Committee? Was it net a fact that the Secretary had been instructed to prepare a statement showing exactly the financial position of the water undertaking ? Would not the Water Committee and the Council, he asked, be in a better pesition to follow Mr. Pritchard if they were in possession of the n fTohr The Chairmanati en t he Ac rcectoarrdyi nwg a tso i nt the ter mu citneudt et so opfrtehpee re? Water Committee I think Mr. Pritchard is in order. Mr. Wilson asked whether criticism on the matter would be at some future time pr whether it was to be completed them ? The Chairman : It will not interfere with the report. Mr. Pritchard continuing said that with the exception of a ld in the £ in 1897 which realised £lBO 17s 81 the water works had provided all the money they required. They expected that they would have had to call on the rates to meet one half of the £2770. Another important thing Was that they were writing off £l2O for water empties which was at the rate of £5OO a year. In suite of these reverses the water undertaking had been able Co do what he had declared that morning. That sheaei they were in a s`roog financial poison. Their debenture bonds were quoted to-Jay at £lO6 and £lOB. Suppesing Runcorn gained its normal condition the water works would pay an interest on the £70,000, and the annual eeerram i 1,110 the sailthee fund te redeem itself of the £7lOOO Li a d.htien i. hav-a profit to go to the reductiun of the rates or some other useful pur- pose. He considered that the purchase of the water works by the late Commissioners was fully justified, see- ing that they had had only one rate of a 14 in the £ which breught in £lBO in five or six Years, and that they were having nothing out of the extra rate levied this year. Mr. unison asked if they were to understand that only £lBO had been taken out of the rates for the water works ? Mr. Pritchard : The exact figures are £lBO 17. Bd. It was in consequence of the coal strike that that sum had to be asked for. If it had not been for the extra cost of fuel the probability was that the ratepayers would not , leave been called upon for one 14. to wara's the water undertaking. The price they paid for the undertaking was moderate and not excessive. He thought it neces- I sary to point out that remarks bad been made by some irresponeible persons that the water undertaking was a financial failure. The figures quoted by him had de- monstrated its cons oicuous fieancial fineness. At the time of the transfer of the works to the Council the directore were in favour of it. He thought the change had happened at an opportune junction, and if the un- dertaking had passed with the management into younger bands the probability was that they would not have had have acquired ith9ywhasadbyanceimn,:),:iltsoriravl the opportunity at the present time, and the only way purchasec°gldwhich would have cost £20,000 or £30,000 they water, and even builders undertook to supply company absolutely ihrnnesagiodurnelai r refund Staffloo lcrisnututel pit little ivmn time while gettingea worksag o, t awki ntie tth hey e y lnoe that Consuming T w 0,000 wallo; per quarter, and as this was essential for their manufactures, they had to give them some assur- suptptelyo.orTthherere"muilltlioon g of that assurance assur- ance that they was that the industry had settled in fißuncorn,e i °d to the many improl was w per quarter. Mr: Pritchard, !continuing, ea veinentS affsctel by ear. Wildinse the surveyor, in coenection with the works, which had prevented consi- derable leakage, and caused the works to be carried on in a very systematic menner. He also referred to Wes- ton, and said the water supply to that village had been put. right at a cost of £2OO. In answer to Mr. Pritchard, the Surveyor said that so far as the tide and the construction el the Ship Canal were concerned they had ne influence on the water supply. Mr. Pritchard remarked that if it were otherwiee it would be a very serious matter. Mr. Collier asked Mr. Pritchard what he allowed for depreciation, and the wear and tear of machinery. Mr. Pritchard said those were kept up out of the revenue. Prejudiced minds were the most difficult to deal with, and it was absolutely impossible in some cases to remove that prejudice, but he thought so far as they could ascertain the individuals who expressed dis- satisfaction might be counted upon one's fingers. In conclusion he thought the works had a hopeful future and would for the present and future generations yield a wholesome Supply of water. Mr. Wilson seconded the minutes of the committee. He could not endorse all that Mr. Pritchard had said,, as he considered that he had introduced irrelevant matter. Mr. Imision thought Mr. Pritchard had ben allowed considerable latitude, although he didn't question the Chairman's ruling. He had taken them all by surprise, and be had never known anything more unfair. He (Mr. Imison) remembered a prominent member of that auth- ority,or of the authority at the time,stating at a meeting of ratepayers that the purchase of the water undertak- ing would result in a profit of £lBO, but he could not bring that statement there and pit it against that of Mr.Pritchard's,and for that reason be declined to discuss the question. Mr.Pritchard in his opening remarks told</p>
        <p>lhem that there had been no deviation in the quality of the water,but he (Mr.lmison) knew perfectly well that there bad. He therefore proposed to wait until the neat meeting assuming the members had a copy of the finan- cial position of the works. Other members spoke, arid the motion was ultimately carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>THE FINANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>_ It was recommended by this Committee that the seal of the Council be affixed to a general district rate 35. 3d. in the £ for the year ending 31st March, 1899, and that the sum of £534 119. 10d. for empties, redactions of assessments, Sec., in respect of the general district rate for the year ended 31st March, 11198. be written off.—Confirmed on the motion of Mr. Imison.</p>
        <p>LIPPER MERSEY NLITIOLTION COMMISSION. Mr. J. Pritchard was appointed the Council's repr2- sentative on the above commission.</p>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHT FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Collier moved the following resolution : "float a committee be appointed to consider the offer of the Municipal Eleetric Supply Co., to supply Electricity in Runcorn and to consider and report as to the advisabil- ity of establishing an .Electric Installation in Runcorn, and that such committee do consist of Mr. Bisbrown, Mr. Gatley, Mr. Kirkham, Mr. Lighthurn, Mr. Wilson and Mr. Collier."—Mr. Collier said the company would bear all costs.—Mr. lusison asked whether it was pro- posed t ) light the streets.—Mr. Wilson replied that it was and also to supply individual applicants. It was very cheap indeed.—The metion was carried it being understood that the committee would report to the Council.</p>
        <p>THE LOCAL OCVERNEENT BOARD AND THE BOUNDARIES.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Local Government Board stating that they had given careful consideration to their Inspector's reports on the inquiries held at Runcorn re the application of the Council for a Provisional Order to alter or amend the Runcorn Improvement Act,1852.50 as to make the limits of the urban district co-extensive with those of the township of Runcorn,by incorporating with the said district so much of the estuary of the river Mersey as was added to the township of Runcorn by the Poor Law Amendment Act,1863. They had come to the conclusion not to issue the Order asked for.—The letter was referred to the General Purposes Comm tee. This concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SEQUEL TO A BUILDT.NG TRANS.kOTION</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO A BUILDT.NG TRANS.kOTION</p>
        <p>AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Oa Friday, at the Widnes County Court, before his Honour Judge Collier, a claim was brought by Edwin Wood, builder and contractor, against J. C. Boyle,baker, Widnes, for £2B 5i 31, balance of account owing on the cost of building an oven. There was also a counter-claim to £l3 3s. Mr. A. A. Tobin, barrister-at-law, instruc- ted by Mr. J. W. Knowles, solicitor, Widnes, appeared for the plaintiff,and Mr. H. L. Riley, solicitor,St Helens, appeared for the defendant. Toe Case was one in which the plaintiff had constructed a baker's oven, kc.' for the defendant, and owing to some alleged imputed slander by the defendant, the plaintiff refused to complete the work. The counter-claim by defendant was made in consequene.e of the work not having been done in a proper ann efficient m inner. E lwin Wood, the plaiut ff. Atated that when the work was proceeding he met the defen -lant in toe street, and told him that they would soon be ready for the oven door and frame. Thee were fi-tiraga which were usually purchased by the baker, and he (witness) told the defendiaat that he did not kno.v where to procure them, unless it was at Manchester. The defendant told him to go to Lowe's, in Preseot-street, Liverpool, and he would supply him with what he required if witness told him the size of the oven. The defendants also told him that he might as well get the waggon and blowpipe at the same time. On witness asking the defendant what description of doors he would require, he said a single door would do. Plaintiff went to Levee's and ordered material for an Bor 12 feet oven. When the door and frame were pot into their respective positions, from information he received, plaintiff went to see the defendant, svho told him that he wanted the door mak- ing into two. Witness told him that he wanted one at firstaand that he would have to pay for the alteration. He (witness) sent th s door down to Linaker, the black- smith, for the necessary alterations. The defendant SAW the oven during the week, and he never found any fault with it. Witness was going towards the Bighous3 just before the end of the week,when he met the defend- ant with a gentleman. About three or four hours later he again inet the defendant, who asked him if he (wit- ness) would like to know who the gentleman was that was with him. It was,he aail,a MaStrange,of Warrine- tan,coho was in a large way of bueleess as a baker. He had told defendant that the oven was grand. Witness asked him if he had got Szinge to inspect ir,and he said 'Yes." Plaintiff asked the defendant as to the height of the chimney.and he had put it accarding, to his approval. The defendant said he would like the place whitewashed and he (plaintiff) eent mom to do it ; and the painting had been done to the defendant's satisfa Alen. Witness had been in business in Widnes for over 14 years, and hie charges were less in this case than usual. He charged nothing for his own time and supervinion.—Ceoss- examined by Mr. Riley :It was not true that the flue was blocked up with brick bats. The roof Was water, tight, and when witness left the work he did no observe any sacking, or that the roef was packed with bags. Re-examined by Mr. Tobin : He had not finished the contract because of the slander.—The hearing was then adjourned until three (..'aloch.—Upon the Judge renaming his seat, Mr. Tobin said that they could agree between themselves, but Mr. Riley's client said "I do not want to agree with anything," He asked leave to suggest that the defen ,ant should come forward, and that His Honour should interrogate him upon the enorm- ous expense that would be incurred in the event of him proceeding with the case. If that were done he would certainly agree. —Mr. Riley said that his client had authorised him to mike a specific offer, which he re- garded as an extremely gencroue offer.—His Honour,the counsel, and Mr. Riley then consulted in private for about half an hour, at the end of which time the follow- ing compromise was drawn up:—The defendant to take £2O out of the claim, arid the counter-claim to be with- drawn, each party paying their own costs. The defend- ant undertakes forthwith to return gas brackets, cheffer, and old material left on the premises. He ex- presses WS regret that he had used any words calculated to hurt the plaintiff's feelings in connection with the work done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>FAMINE IN IRELAND. A RUNCORN FUND OPENED.</head>
        <p>THE FAMINE IN IRELAND. A RUNCORN FUND OPENED.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the inhabitants ef Runcorn, was held on Thurseay evening in the Bethesda Lecture Hall,Runcorn for the purpose of starting a fund in the town to assist in the alleviation of the suffering people in the South West of Ireland. Mr. S. Taylor J.P. Chairman of the Urban District Council presided, and he was supported by Mr. F. J. Norman LP., Mr. J. W. Lightburn, Mr. J. D-nnellan, Mr. It. J. McCirtney,Mr. M. Nickson, and the Rev. Father Gore. Mr. Taylor said as Chairman of the Urban District Council he r,ad been asked to call a public meeting to see what could be done to render relief to the people who were suffsring from dire want in the South West of Ire- land. He at once acceded to the request. From the handbill he noticed that there were same 300,000 people suffering in Ireland through the failure of the potatoe crops. Last year the potatoe crops were not up to the average yield by one half. That being the case they must know that the masses were suffering very consider- ably. In fact it WAS well-known that people who tilled the land had difficulty in making both ends meet at the best of times. Tuerefore they could imagine what the Irish people were suffering from under the present con- ditions. Be was pleased to eay that the people of this country were taking active steps to raise funds towards this good object. So far as Runcorn was concerned he was sure that the people would come forward and help such a worthy cause, by giving these poor pellet° practical assistance. They had already had sympathy from various quarters, and Father Gore had told him that he had had respenses from many people. To-day he had received a letter from Mr. J. Okell,super- intendent of the RUTICOM Police, enclosed in which was a sovereign. (Applause.) Tnat was most encouraging. What it wee-proposed to do was to make a house to house visitation,and he felt from past experience and a knowledge of the Runcorn people that everyone would give their practical help and sympathy. (Applause). flue Rev. Father Gore then addressed the meeting. He first of all read two letters from the Rev. Father Ryan, of Middlewich, and Mr. Madden, barrister, of Birkenhead, apologising f(r their non-attendance.nfle. had received from Sir J. Ti Brunner, mp. :_9. Enis more Gardens, 28th April, 1898. Dear Father Gore, —I am very gild to hear that a public meeting is to he held in Runcorn to consider what steps shall be taken to assist the efforts which are being made to relieve the distresS in the West of Ireland. I need not assure youlhat the action of tee Urban Council in summcp,itig a meeting has my most cordial approval. It is a matter of great satisfaction to me that the people of the Northwich division are making so ready a response to the imperious claims of suffering and misfortune in Ireland, I am confident that the citizens of Runcorn who have won for themselves so proud a reputation for their public spirit will show themselves not less active in their charity and benevo- lence to a cause which must appeal irresistibly to every humane and generous heart. Yours faithfully, John T Brunner." The Rev. Father Gore, continuing, said the. question was not one of religion, pblitics or nationality, nut ores of common humanity.—Mr. Lightburn and Mr. Norman also spoke. Mr. Lightburn proposed that the town be divided into wards and that a chairman be appointed over a com- mittee to act in each ward. The Rev. Father Gore and Mr. Donnellan were appointed secretaries, and Mr. Haddock of Parr's Bank, and Mr. Chantler, of Williams, Deacon Bank were appointed treasurers. A vote of thanks passed to Mr. Taylor for presiding brought the meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>His Royal Highness the Duke of Cambridge, who has been the guest of the Duke and Duchess of Westminster at Eaton Hall during the past few days in order to at- tend the Chester race meeting, concluded his visit on Thursday morning.</p>
        <p>DEAT a AND FUNERAL OF ALDERM&amp;N GRE WINTALLI JP.</p>
        <p>Much regret was expressed in the town and dis- trict, when it became known that Alderman Greenall, .LP., had passed away on Monday at the age of 50 years. For some time Alderman Greenall has been in failing health, but it was not anticipa- ted that the end was so near, and when the sad news arse% ed in Warrington, it came in the nature of a shock to the deceased's gentlemen's Many friends. He resided at 1, Groby-place, Altrinchans,l where his death took place at 10-30 a.m. Alderman I Greens!! was one of the best-known men in War- I rington, principally from the fact that he had been I three years Mayor of the Borough, and always al prominent townsman. Until a very few years ago, no resided at Hill Top,Latchford, but on relinquish- ing the office of Mayor, he took up his abode at I Altrincham. He was head of the firm of G. and J. Greenall, wine and spirit merchants, and distillers, Bridge-street, Warrington, and attended to his business with as much regularity as his precarious health permitted. During his illness the deceased gentleman was attended by Dr. Golland, of Altrinchatn, who did his best to prolong his patient's life. He was born at Wilderspool in 1848, and was a son of the late Mr. Edward Greenall, J.P., C.C., of Grappenhall Hall. Alderman Greenall's early education was received at Beau- marls Grammar School, and from thence he went to Magdalen College, Oxford. In 1884 he married Miss A. M. Dunn, daughter of Mr. Nicholas .J. Dunn, J.P. and D.L. for County Pembroke, of Cumberland House, Tenby. Ile was first elected a member of the Warrington Town Council for the South West Ward, when he suc- ceeded Mr. John Lee. He only remained in office until the end of the year, when he left the Council, and was again elected ix November, 1885, since which time he has been centinuously in office. Honours fell quick upon Mr. Greenall, who was in 1891 not only appointed an alderman, but a justice of the peace for tile borough,and on the 9th Novem- ber be was unanimously elected Mayor. In 1892, the Council unanimously re-elected him for another twelve months,and again in 1893 a similar honour was conferred upon him. His Worship was, in November,lB92,placed on the Commission of the Peace for the county of Chester. He was one of the trustees of the Blue Coat School. Both Alderman and Mrs. Greenall manifested considerable interest in the praise- worthy efforts which were made in Warrington to alleviate the sufferings of those who were thrown out of employment by the colliers' strike in 1893. They not only attended at the soup kitehens, but visited the homes of the poor, and personally re- lieved much distress existing in the borough by the gifts of money and clothing. Deceased at the time of his death was the alderman for. the Town Hall Ward, and was a member of the following commit- tees :—Watch,Finance and Estate, Town Hall,Park aria Baths,Markets, and the General nemeses Com- mittee. He leaves a widow but no children. THE FUNERAL. The remains of the late Alderman Greenall were quietly laid to rest in Grappenhall Churchyard at noon on Thursday. At the request of the widow and relatives the funeral was of a private character and the number present was not very large. An impressive service was conducted in the church by the Rector, the Rev. Richard Greenall, who was assisted by the Rev G C Keine, vicar of St. Ketharine's, Gloucester. Whilst the congregation assembled in the church. Mr W Asquith Wood, the or- ganist,played Mendelssohn's " 0 Rest in the Lord." The choir was in attendance, and very efficiently led the singing. The 90:h Psalm was sung, and also the hymns " Now the labourer's task is e'er " and " Brief life is here our portion." Amengst those present were the widow (Mrs Edward Greenall), Mr Henry Greenall. Mr and Mrs Peter Greenall, Mr and Mrs ty C Jones, Mr Cyril Greenall, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Mr James H Dunn. the Rev H V and Mrs Pigo-, Mr Arthur Pieot, Mr Herbert Greenall Piece, Mrs Irving and Mrs Eckersley. There were also present in church with others Ur and Mrs John White, Mr Lister-Kaye the Mayor of Warrington (Alderman Wright) and the Town C:erk (Mr J Lyon Whittle)—the two last-named repre- senting the Corporation of Warrington • Alderman James Faircleugh (depnty mayor), Alderman 'Francomb, Alder- man Flutchmeon, Councillors 11 Roberts, Dr Cannel!, James Sanethurst, and Ellison; the Borough Treasurer (Mr John Fairhurst), the Borough Surveyor (Mr Thomas Long,din) ; Messrs R I Wynne Jones. James Tinsley, Vere B Davies, George Dakin, Leigh Layland,Henry Leyland, Ebenezer Dillow, W Price (Inland Revenue Department, Warrington), Mr E J Hall. Mr Edward Macaulay, and the following as representing the Distillery, Bridge-street M. sari Lee. James Ince, Jam's Richards, Joseph Hunter, and T Fitzpatrick. As the congregation left the church for the 'graveside, the organist played the Dead March in " S dol." The ieter- ment took place in a new grave—the sides of which were lined with ley leaves—in the new portion of the burial ground. A large number of beautiful wreaths covered the coffli lid. The inner ceffin was of oak and th uside of fumigated oak, heavily panelled. Oa the lid was a full length brass cross with shield attached,the engraving on the shield baring the simple inscription "E 1 wa,d Greenall,ho.n April 9th, 1848, died May 2nd, 1898." Mr. Ralph Johnson,of Rylands-street satisfactorily carried rut the funeral arrangements. RFFERENCE AT THE TOWN COUNCIL ADJOURNMENT OF THE MEETING. At the meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening reference was made to the death of Alderman Greenall, and ass mark of res- pect it was decided to adjourn the proceedings. The Mayor presided, and there were also present Alderman Fairclough (deputy Mayor). Aldermen Blackhurst, Francomb, and Bolton, Councillors IT. Roberts, J. Shaw Green, F. Monks, R. neaten, J. Smethurst, W. Pierpoint, G. Plumpton, A. Bennett, L. Ellison, A. Woods, DeS, Duncan, W. Furness, F. R. Roberts, D. Tinnion, Joseph Wilkinson, Dr. Can- nel!, F. W. Monks. A. Jolley, John Roberts, John Lee, J. Grace, A. Peacock, Thomas 'Burton, with J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), J. Deas (sanitary en- gineer), and W. S. Haddock (gas engineer). The Mayort,at the outset, said : As a mark of respect to the memory of the late Mr. Alderman Greenall, I pro- pose that this Council de adj tt-n after an expression of our condolence and sympathyl has been given to Mrs. Greens!! in her affliction. The late Alderman will ever be remembered for his uniform courtesy and urbanity. His genial kindness to all alike, rich and poor, was a strong feature of his life, and his high sense of honour and his contempt for anything which he thought was not straightforward and above board were no less; marked eharacteristics. Mr. Gr enall was first elected to the Council in November,lBBo,and with a short inter- regnum had served in the Council for upwards of 13 years. He was elected an Alderman in 1891. and in the same year was first elected Mayor of the Borough,. an office which he filled with great fairness and satisfaction during three years in succession. He also represented the Council as one of the Governers at the Grammar School,and he was a magistrate for the County of Ches- ter as well be of his native town. I beg to move : " That this Council receives with deco reeret the an- nouncement of the death of Edward Greenall, Esq., an Alderman and a Justice of the Peace of the Borough,anci desires to record the lose that the borough has sustained by his death,and to express its appreciation of his long and valuable public services • and that the Town Clerk be requested to convey to Mrs. Greenall the sincere sympathy of the Council with her in her bereave- ment*" COUECMOY HENRY ROBERTS said he seconded the reso- lution with very much regret. He had, he said, been in the Council the whole of the time the late Alderman was a member of it and he could say with absolute truth that be did not know anything during all that period in which the Alderman aid not act with absolute impar- tiality, fairness and honour. Representing, as he did to SOMA .'xtpnr, the temperance sentiment of the town he (Mr. Roberts), could say that it was a matter of great nticraetion to himself and others associated with him to know that in any question aff ,cting licensing matters the late Alderman Greene!' never allowed himself to take part. That circumstance al-me stamped the character of the man for integrity and honour. As the Mayor had so well said his enurtesy, urbanity, and general character were shown alike to every member of the Corporation, and to all with whom he came in contact. Councillor Roberts deeply regretted that Alderman Greenall had been taken away, especially at so early an age. They looked as a rule for some of the best years of a man's life between 50 and 80, in any public capacity, and it must be a matter for very great regret to all who knew Alderman Greenall that be should have been taken away from them at this comparatively early age. He begged to join His Worship in the regret he had expressed, and which the town generally felt at the moment in the death of Alderman Greened!. (Hear, hear). Alderman FAIRCLOUGEI supported the resolution with great sympathy. Re could sirak from authority as to the valuable time the late Mr. Greenall had given to the town. Couneillor Lee said that he had known Alderman Greenall 30 years sod he could say candidly that be en- dorsed every word the Mayor and Councillor Roberts had said. There wan not a better hearted man in War- rington,and he was one of the few men the speaker had known in his life who never used a hard word against ' anybody. (13ear,hear.) Councillor Emmett( said he had known the late alder- man all his life, and had been in close touch with him as a neighbour for a very long time, and it was with deep regret that he heard of his death. He had always been a very kind neighbour and one in whom people had</p>
        <p>every confidence. Councillor Ellison added that he felt sure that their expression of sympathy that night would be very well received.</p>
        <p>The resolution was then unanimously passed. The MAYOR moved that the Council meetiog be ad- journed until Tuesday next. Alderman BLACKHURST : I think Tuesday would be convenient. I second the motion. The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>_ I The MAYOR alluding to the funeral of the late Alder- man Greenall said he instructed the Town Clerk to write to Mr. Henry Greenall with reference to the Council at- tending, and he had sent a very kind letter from Mrs. Greenall saying how much she appreciated the honour they wished to pay her late husband, but it was her desire that the funeral should be strictly private. He (the Mayor) thought that if any gentleman wished to go in his private capacity be could do so. Councillor LEE : I suppose you would go in any case? The MAYOR: I should like to go, and the Town Clerk with me.</p>
        <p>Councillor Srrkw GREEN: I think it would be very nice if the Mayor and Town Clerk represented us.</p>
        <p>The Council then rose. REFERENCE AT THE POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>After the ordinary business at the Warrington pollee Court on Thursday, Lieutenant Colonel Ridgway, (the chairman of the bench), said he thought he ought to mention the loss the Bench has sustained in the death of his old friend AldermaVreenall,a man who had done a great deal for the town, and who did his duty on every occasion ho was required. Be was sure be only echoed the sentiments of thP Court when he said how sorry they were for his loss and how they sympathised with his widow.</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST endorsed what Colonel Ridg- way had said. Ho had known Mr. Greenell for s3me- thing like 20 years and had had a continuous connection with him in the carrying out ot many things connected with the town, such as the relief of the poor, during the years of his Alayorslty. He was sure the town had lost a man whose sympathy was with the 14Joor and with everything for the good of the town. Mr. G. F. F. WRITE (deputy clerk) supported the re- matka from the 13,..racti on ehalf of his father and the solicitors practising in the Court,.</p>
        <p>(Aa)erttsrirtrins nut LLtatur,O.</p>
