      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>INFLUENZA. EPIDEMIC.</head>
        <p>THE INFLUENZA. EPIDEMIC.</p>
        <p>At tbe present time, everywhere, almost, is the Infla- euza. Thousands of patients are down with this terrible scourge which as again visited our ceuntry,and is corn - misting frightful havoc among an classes of society. It is much more prevalent than is generally known, and , the cases are more severe than on former occasions. Having observed its ravages and its baneful effects on the conetitution on the occasion of its former visits, we cannot but regard its recurrence with special dread, as a great many have not yet,if they ever will, regain their former health. The present form of the disorder attacks the organs of digestion, as well as the lungs. Nor are the symptoms precisely the same in each individual. Many, too, feel the after effects of Influenza for months and months. To have the microbes or bacilli in the blood even for three days seems thoroughly to weaken and exhaust the constitution. Now, as a preventa- tive, we know of nothing equal to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic A few strong doses takes in time have often proved effectual in warding off attacks of this malady, and al- ways succeed in mitigating their severity when a patient is under their influence. Do you suffer from Chills, alternating with Sudden Flushes, Severe Headache, ac- companied by difficulty of breathing, and symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest, under and between the Shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness of the muscles, Loss of Appetite and Nervousness, or are you oppressed with gleomy forebodings and depressed spirits ? If you suffer from any of these symptoms, know that they ar3 the forerunners of Influenza, and it behoves you to resort at once to an effective and suitable remedy, and that is wilym Evans' Quinine Bitters,The Vegetable 'lonic. By common consent patients every where admit that they feel more depressed and miserable after an attack of Influenza than while under its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. There is a want of " Go," a lack of cheerfulness, de- pression of spirits, feeling of misery and helplessness, and such despondency as to be almost unbearable, the slightest exertien is fatiguing, what was considered a pleasure is a toil, the appetite had entirely gone, and to sum up, " Life is a burden to those who have not hitherto experienced for one day in their lives such feelings of misery and helplessness. The best way to banish these miserable feelings is to take nourishing food, and moderate exercise, to avoid solitude, and mix in cheerful companY, and to take Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. We can strongly and confidently ree.ommerd this preparation, as it has treated severe cases of Influenza,extreme Wer.kness and Languor with greater success than any Other known remedy. A few doses taken in time will drive away the sense of extreme helplessness and feeling of misery and weakness which Edicts patients after a severe attLetc. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is sold in bottles, 24 91. and 45.6 d. each. Beware of Imitations. See the narne "Gwilyrn Evans" on Label. Stamp,and Bottle. Sole proprietors Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>BEFITE THE M&amp;GISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFITE THE M&amp;GISTRATES.</p>
        <p>FOOTPATH OBSTRUCTION FOR BETTING PURPOSES. At the Warrineton Borough Police Court on Monday, William Green, 14 Norman-street, Patrick Fallon, Latchford, Bernard Flennigan, Town Hill, and James Barry, 5 Bakehoues- yard, Foundry-street, all young men, were summoned for wilfully obstructing the free passage of the footpath in Winwick-street by standing upon it. Mr. Browne represented Green. Hennigan and Barry were absent.— P.C. Lewis said the defendants at 12.25 p in. on the 25 h ult. were loitering about Winwick-street near the Cen- tral Station. They were with other men, and all were standing on the footpath. life had previously cautioned these men. There had been serious complaints made to the police of this class of offence.—Sergeant Evans cor- roborated. Ile said he had warned the defendants several times. The police knew what the defendants loitered about the foothpaths for.—Mr. Browne submit- ted that there was no case for him to answer. The real offence was no doubt betting in the street; that was what the police really alleged, but he (Mr. Browne) was , not going to let it out in evidence. That court had been in existence for a great number of years—ever since Warrington was a borough—and the clerks had always advised that no offence had been committed under simi- lar circumstances to the present. It was no effence under the Towns Police Clauses Act. Why bad other towns gone to the Home Secretary for bylaws if it was not because they could take no proceeding either under the Highway Act or the Towns Police Clauses Act ? He was not in favour of street bettine, but there had been no legal offence committed in the present cases. Until , they got a bylaw there was nothing to prevent the de- ' fendants actin e as they had done —Mr. Birchall : Would you argue that We should ignore obstructions of the foothpath P—Mr. Browne: You cannot do that. If you place a box or a cart on the foothpeth there is an obstruction within the meaning of the Act. The question has been raised over and over again at Wolverhampton and Birmingham. A mere tandine on the footpath is no nflenc .—Coun- culler Shaw Green said the magistrates were eatis- fied that an obstruction of the footpath had taken place, and they would no doubt take the Clerk's ruling en the matter (To the Clerk) : Harp we power to find these defendants 7—The Clerk : Yes if you think they wil- fully obstructed the footpath.—Mr. Browne : I must apply for you to state a case. You know what the re- sult will be.—C.ouncillor Shaw Green : As far as the facts are cencern-d I think they are clear.—Mr. Browne: As a matter of fact this offence took place on Monday, and there is no racing on Mrinday.—PS. Evans: War- wick races were held on this day. (Laughter).—Mr. Brewne : These defendants deny that they ever speke to each other.—Pattiek Fallon then spoke on his own behalf and denied that be stood on the footpath —The Bench decided to fine the defendants ss. and costs each, Mr. Browne : lam compelled to ask for a case. We Mayor : The Bench have de- r. Browne : Thank you cpiadye d underto g r apartot v eosut a-T ch :s I will make the usual application in writing. DISSENSION IN THE WARRINGTON MARIET SHED. On Monday before the Mayor (in the chair) and ether megietrates. at the Warrington Borough Police Court, Jane "Sewers, 9, Fr-Than-lane. was summoned by Frank Fox. 29, Froghall-lane,for assault. Mr. Arthur Browne, I solicitor, appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. Jenkins feel the defendant. Mr. Browne stated that both plaintiff and defendant were in the boot trade in the market. I Frank Fox was the manager for Mr. James Halliwell. It was alleged that Frank Fox stopped an errand be of Mrs. Bowers' and asked him if the venetian blinds he was carrying were from the pawn- shop. It was contended this was only in fun, as similar expressions often passed between stallholders. However Fox apologised later on for it. Mrs. Bowers hPard of the incident from the lad. The tellfng of it probably made it appear more serious than it was, fir she lost her temper, and a few days after when she saw Fox struck him under the Kt ear, Mr, 11411isvell interfered. The Bench after hearing several witnesses, and Mr. Jenkins , en behalf of the defendant, dismissed the cm,.</p>
        <p>Tag RESULT OF POVERTY. Oa Monday at the Borough Police Court, before the Mayen On the hair). and other magistrates, William and Emma Fiddler 2, Ann's-yard, were onmmoned by , Taspector McAteer on behalf of the /CS P.C.C. Inspec- tor McAteer said they were the parents of four children, William Fiddler the father, being a brick-setter. In consequence of a report a gentleman made to him he visited the defendant's home. It was in a dirty condi• tion, and what little clothing there was being worn by the children aad the mother, was ir. a filthy state. The wife Emma Fiddler admitted she had no change of cloth- ing for herself, as it was in the pawnshop. One of the children a hor, was covered with marks from vermin on the bedy. There were very few cooking utensils in the house, and very little furniture. The bed occupie4 by ths man and woman and two children was in a filthy state. The two other children occupied another bed.— P.S. Smith corroborated the Inscector's evidence.—Alice Molyneaux a neighbour, called on behalf of the defen- dants said the was at her door close te, when the In- spector came and there was a fair amount of furniture in the house at the time. The house and children were not so dirty as he said. Defendaate pleaded that their poor circumstances were tFe cause. The male defendant said his wages ware only Si. an hour, and he had been ill for six weeks and unable to work. The Bench gave them five weeks to produre a hotter state of things. and instructed the Inspector to visit them in the mean- time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. _ _</p>
        <p>MoNDAY.—Before the Mayor (In the ehale). CounclVer James Smethurst. Councillor Shaw-Green. Alderman Black- '</p>
        <p>_ hurst and Mr. d. W. Hayward A DEsaaTl,o FATHER.—Tbornas Fury, farm Int )urer, Friars'-green, was charged with running away from the borough, leaving his wife and two &amp;Waren charze tble in the Union.—Prisoner pleaded guilty.—Mr. B. Shearer, who proved the case. stated that the two children respectively seven years, and eve years of age were admitted into the Workhouse on March -24th, having been deserted by their father and had been there ever since. A. warrant was issued and prisoner was arrested in a house in Warrington by Detective-Inspector be committed top' ison for one month. Monagh.n.—The Bench ordered Fury to DRUNK AND DlSORngaLsr.—Rilen H.aly. JaCkson's Bow, for this offence in Buttericaritet-street on Saturday night was fined ss. or in default seven days.—P.C. Reddington proved the case.—George Tronton, carter' Jacieon's-rovr, Church- street, was fined ss. or In default seven daYs Evans proved the case.— Elizabeth Lythßoe, 26, pattern-lane, for this offence, proved by P.S. Smith, was fined the costs 3s 6d.</p>
