        <p>WEDNESDIY.—Before the Mayor (chairman), Councillor J</p>
        <p>Smetursi.</p>
        <p>ANOTHFR 1117• BAND SENT TO —LILL -Joseph Heat an was chargei with running away from the borough and leaving his wife and two children chargeable to the Union since August 2nd, 1897.—Mr. :shearer said that in January last year prisoner appeared before the Board, having rea•ted from his wife, and they sgreed to take his two children for 6s. a week. He paid two weeks and then t rew up a good situa- tion at Messrs. P. Walke^ and Sons. Twelve months ago he was apprehended and sent to gaol. They had not seen him since and in November another warrant was t .ken out against him and he was apprehmded in Manchester on Tues- day The two childr,-n had cost the Union £35. There was no reason why he could not keep the children. Mr. Shearer tre-sed the case as there were over 4) deserted women and children in the Workhouse now.—Pris mer said he had been nimble to obtain work and beggei for another chance.—He arse sent to gaol for six w.eks ha.d labour. n'atuitenty.—B,fore Lieutenant-Colonelf Ridgway (In the . . - .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>chair). and Councillor J tiroi:uhural.</head>
        <p>chair). and Councillor J tiroi:uhural.</p>
        <p>THEFT FROM • SCRAP HEAP.—Thomas 11 an was oharg-td with st sling froras the yard of Messrs. Monks, Hell and Co., a quantilm of scrap iron value sd.—Prisoner p eaded not guilty—The Chief Coleable said that ...bout 4 o'clock that morning P.C. Coup of the Lancashl,e Cosnty Police was on duty close to the Borough b mndaries whets he met prisoner curying a lot of scrap iron and questioned him. His answer noc being satisfactory, ths officer Iramght him into the Borovgle whore he met P.C. Johnson. Prisoner was taken to Monks's yard where the iron wee leentill.d by Henry Allander, watchman, as a port on of the scrap heap Pris- oner ssid the iron was given to Wm by a man in the works. Ile himself had absolutely no right on. the premises whatever When charged he said I hopo I shall get six months. I have been in gaol ve times s:nce February.".—Henry Allen- der, night watchman for Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co. cor- roborated, and stated that the Company suffered much from rooberles of this description.—The Chief Constable Bald that Mr. Monks bad told him they bought their own scrap two or three times over.—P.C.'s Coup and Johnson also ga•te evi- dence in support of the charge.—Prisoner steted to the magistrates that the iron was given to him and he was going to send it to his father in Irelend.—lfe was cornmittsd to gull for 14 days' with hard labour.—P.C. Coup was corn. mentecd for his vigilance oy the chairman of the bench and the Chief Coast eble.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WErmEsnsr.--Before Captain Vilithington (in the chair), Mr. J. J. Blackly. and Lieut.-I olonel Ri.igway. TEMPOA•Ry A.UTHoRITY —Mr. James Fletcher WI Maker was granted tsrnoorary au.hority to sell intoxiciting liquors on the 13th and 14th May at Newton Races. _</p>
        <p>UNLICENSED DOGi.—Adam Hindley, Poul Lon, was charged with ke ping a dog without a license. The defendant said to the officer when he cal ed that he was keeping the dog for a man named Thomas Ha.rop with whom he lodged. The dog would belong to him after the 9th April, the day the officer called, lie took out a license the same day in his own name. He produced the license and also one in the name Thomas Harrop. The case was dismissed.—Mary Norton, of Buxton. for this offence on the lath April was fined 78.6 d. DRUNK ON THE CANAL SIDE.—John Houghton. :;arikey, who was found drunk on the canal side on the 16th April, and might have been drowned but for the ready assistance afforded in pulling him out of the water, was titled 2s. 6d. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS'.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS'.</p>
        <p>Moarnmr.—Before Mr. F. J. Norman and Mr. B. Speakman. DRUNICICNNESS.—Thomas Johnson was fined 51. including Posts for this offence committed In Bridge-street on Saturday night. TEMPoILLEY AUTHORITY.—James Tatlock applied for per- mission to sell at the Oddfellows Arms. Wellington-street. in lieu of Alfred Griffiths ; Robert Ashley in plane of Martha Jones at the Prospect Inn, Halton ; Frederie'r Dutton at the Wilson's Hotel, Bridge-street, in lieu of his father Charles Dutton. All the applications were granted. LODGING IN AN EMPTY llollBE.—Seilna Gibbln, a well- known charat.ter, was charged with sleeping in an unoccu- pied house in Mersey-street. Runcorn, on Sunday.—P.o Bowyer proved the case.—On promising to return to the Workhouse prisoner was discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SUGGESTED NEGLECT OF A</head>
        <p>THE SUGGESTED NEGLECT OF A</p>
        <p>CHILD AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE ADJOURNED INQUEST,-VF.,RDICT.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held in the Parade Room at the Police Station by Mr. S. Brighouse on Tuesday afternoon, with reference to the death of a child, named Annie Forster, daughter of George Forster,of 27, Napier-street. The child died on April 25th,and the inquest was adjourned last week in order that a post-mortem examination might be made. The first witness called was Sarah Bird who said she was a qualified midwife. On the 18th of January she was sent forte attend Mrs. Selina Forster, the mother of the deceased. When she arrived she found that the deceased child was born. It had had the usual attention and was fully developed, and had every appearance of health. She attended the mother and infant for six days, when the latter appeared to be doing all riedst. She did n“t see it alive again. On Monday, the 25th of April, she was passing along Napier-street, when Mrs. Forstor stopped her and told her that her baby we dead. She went to the house and saw it. The body was very much wasted. Ellen Hunter deposed to seeing the child at 2.15 pin, OR April 25th. It was not dead only convulsed. The deceased and its clothing smelled badly, and the clothes were dirty. The body was very much wasted, and tha deceased seemed as if it had not had pr. per nourishment. IThe child died while she was in the house.—The Coroner: Don't von know that some children waste away with the b, st of care and nourishmehtp—Witnesa replied in the affirmative. _ _</p>
        <p>Mary Forster, a neighbour ef George and Selina Fors- ter, but no relative, deposed to seeing the child just be- fore it died. It was very wasted. Mrs. Forster (the 1 mother) appeared to be doing her best for the family generally. That was the first time she had seen the child. The other children were kept clean. The last baby but lone Mrs. Selina Forster had was a very puny one. Inspector McAteer, N.S.P.C.C., was the next witness called. Ile stated that he visited the houm on several occasions. On his first visit the deceased child was drinking sour milk out of a porter both-, lie gave the little one some fresh milk, which it dean': greedily, but vomited afterwards. The house was in a dirty con- dition, and the child's body was also dirty. When he visited the house again the child was again drinking sour milk nut of a porter bottle. The father of the deceesed child told him he earned from 30;. to 34i. a week.—The Coroner There would be no lack of tanners in Warrington if they were paid at that rate.—The fath- er said his wages were 28s. a week. That was the sum he told the Inspector.—ln answer to the Coroner, the In— spector added that Mrs. Forster was the mother of seven children. He could not say that the deceased had suffered from want of food. but she was emaciated. lie could not tell what had set it up. De. Reginald Smith, police surgeon, Warriegtoe, who had made a nost-mortem examina`ion a the body, said it was greatly emaciated and weighed 0013 5 lb. 3n. The primary cause of death was consumption of the bowels —a disease of the same nature as ordinary consumption of the lungs. There wag no evidence that death was brought on by starvation, but more probably by wrong ' diet.--The Coroner: Have you heard anything to-day that, combined with your post-mortem, would lead you to believe that some orniosion on the part of etthnt of the parents ha 1 hr,),l,llit About the death P—Dr. Smith: No. Any child Wag liable to that disease, even the children of wealthy parents, but with proper diet and care Cases might recover. He found food in the chill's stomach, —ln answer to a juryman, the doctor said the thild's life might have been saved for a month or co with early medical treatment. George Forster, father of the rine&amp; ied chi! I,t hen gave evidence. He stated that since the first of January, h., had only worked fir.Ve.n weeks, and his wages had aver- aged 23i. a week during that time. The Coroner said it would he his duty ta tell the jury that they nonld not on the evidence -Arhich had been given send the father and mother for trial. A verdict thet " The child died from natural causes. namely consumption uf the bowels, as would appear from the medical evidence " was returned.</p>
      </div>
