      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN INTER-WORKS CONIPETITION FINAL TIE. ---- PRE3ENTITION OF THE MEDALS TO WM.'S TEAM.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN INTER-WORKS CONIPETITION FINAL TIE. ---- PRE3ENTITION OF THE MEDALS TO WM.'S TEAM.</p>
        <p>Wigg's and Weston te-a—m—s—havirig qualified for the final met on the Runcorn ground on Saturday evon.inZ, before an excellent attendance of spectators. Come lov- able interest was centred in the event. Weston won the toss, and Wigses commenced the game, which proved from the start to be of an exeizing character. The enthusiasm was intense, and the delight of the Wigefi contingent unbounded, when Wainwright sect the ball Railing between the uprights from a penalty kick. This concluded the stinting, but the players on both sides worked hr land energetically un to the interval. wh.lla Nof V ihgogststilleitdiphsythaeppelnaacletrys goal ito R nil.ve Os n the res ni n fected with the excitement that was reigpnlanrgelettioph"emine. and they played for al they were worth. Their efforts however, were futle, and Wigses obtained the coveted. offered for the first season, by two points to nil, After the match the spectators gathered around the stand to witness the presentation of the medala.—Mr. Pownall siid he had to aPologz for the absence of Mr. Speakman, their president who had been invited to dis- tribute the medals. but unfortunately was unab!e to do so. They were all very sorry for this, hut the co nmittee had provide I for the cnntingency, hy inviting Mr. W. Carroll, to perform the function. In the position of chairman of the committee, Mr. Carroll had conducted the business of the club in a manner which ref% 'fed the high's: credit upon him. He felt sure that no one was deserving of the honour more than Mr. Carroll With re- card to the competition it had been a gr at success.both financially and from a playing point of view. There had only hems one match to which an exception I could be taken, and that was the one played on the previous Saturday, when there was some rough play, In future he hoped that they would have right down good football, like that played that eyeeing.—Mr. W. ' Cartoll than h inded the medals to the o'ctora, and re- marked that wnile he regretted the absence cf Mr. Speakman, he could hardly express the pleasure it gave him to take so prominent a part in that, the cloning scer.e of another season of football, a season that had , breucht them much regret, and something to be thank- ful for. It was a season that had seen the removal from their ranks of Mr. Langshaw, who had been secretary far a numh-r of years, and who had always taken a deep , interest in the club, and who was at all times jealous of its honour. It was a season that had seen an inaugura- tion of what had proved the most successful competition that had ever been run in Runcorn. It was especially gratifying to know that it had proved in the first place that f.intbill had received a fresh life and energy ; and that while others were ready to decry football, it was nleasisg that tiler had in Runcorn a gentleman like Mr. F. J. Norman on their side. Re hoped that Mr. Norman would long take an interact in the good old club. Re had great oleasnre in coegratulating Mr. Wainwright and his side in having won the medals,as the match had certainly not been a walk over for them.—Mr. Wain- wright suitably responded ,and the ceremony concluded.</p>
