      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>riIiICKBTERS I BUSSEY'S Demon Driver Bats are t grandest wade. BLTSEY'S New Metal; Balls retain their shipe, and last longer than any other. BUt3sEY'S Leg Guards are exceptionally good. BUssEY'S Batting Gloves are far superior to the usual class. Buehur's Bags are made to wear. BUSSEY'S Diary and Companion is a Gem for 6d Apply for BUSSEY'S Catalogue from Local Sports Outfitters, or 38 Queen Victoria-street, London. Factories, Yeckhatn, London; and Jiliruswcli, Suffolk.—Advt. 4</p>
        <p>RUNCORN,- v. BIRKENHEAD WANDERERS. Despite the wet wi.a.,her a great number of spectators assemuled at Upper-park, Ora Saturday afternoon, to witaese the final match of the home seasci•, the visitors bzing Ruricorn.A prompt start was rnade,the hoinesters kicking elf and iminetliacely rushing dawn the field. Runcorn returned, and when the game was at half-way Auel, of Runcorn, was injured, and had to leave ths field. On resuming the name three-quarters passed Well, and made ground, but good kickiug by Runcorn backs, assisied hy a puor kick on the part of Caton, the Wanderers' full back, took tile ball to the Wanderers' hue. It was werk,d away, however, to centre, where, fr,iin a tree kick, Jolley !andel a splendid goal for Run- corn. Pascal team in turn was awarded a couple of free kicks, tile net result being somewhat in favour of the Wands.erA, who, with anything but a representative t-aur, were playing up well against their powerful opponents. Hayes now secured die ball and, following up a snort punt, caught the ball again, thereby making cunsiderate ground fur the Wanaerers. A fine bout of passing fly the Wanderers' backs, in which Clayton was promiu ni,ahuod, resulted in a try, while just alter- wars Lii.ersedge, after a grand rue, went into touch just agaiaat tbe fine. Following up the pressure Dillon got, hold mad passed to Clayton, woo got in near touch, no goal resuleiug, although Crluae..'s attempt was a good one. Several tree kicks ensued, which led to the Wan. derers approaching their opponents' line, a dead ball being the outcome, followed shortly by a touch down, the result of a fruitless drop by Welabe. The Wanderers had the best of matters up to half-time, wLea the score way—Birkeuhead Wanderers one try, Runcorn one goal (tree kick). Final :—Birkenhead Wanderers three points, Runcorn two p AWL</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WARRING [ON CUP. BE•PLAYED FINAL TIE.</p>
        <p>WITTON ALBION V. NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS.—The pro- test .1 toe WiLton club having been upheld owing to an iatringement by the Newtun club, who gained the verdict on April 16th by tour gods to three, the tie was re-played at Da ton Bank on Saturday afternoon. Despite the unfavourable weather there was a capital attendance, and, under the adverse circumstances, a highly crr.ditable exposition was given by both teams. Flaying with the wind during the first half, the Witton men railed to make much headway against the sound detence of the Lancastrians, and Marsh, in goal, gave a flue exhibition. Occasional visits to the Witton goal, chiefly led it liker, also had futile endings, and at half- time Mere was no score. With the aid of the wind the chances pointed to those of the Newton team, but bad judgment lost favourable openings, and only on one occasion did they really threaten the Witten goal. Tides- well saved in fine style, and, together with Stanley and Ro-e, the Wittort goal was kept intact, as that at the cioso neither side had registered a point. An extra bait-hour was ordered, and during that period Deakin managed to take advantage of only a partial clearance by Marso, and suce.eded it. scoring the only goal of the match. Newton played up desperately hard in the concluding stage, but to no purpase, and .he final result read as Witton Albion, eno goal ; Newton-le-Willows, none. The cup was presented to the winners at the Marquis of Grauby Hotel by_Mr. H. Isherwood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night a meeting of the General Committee of the Northern Union was held at the George Hotel, Huddersfield, Mr. Warren, Warringtoa, presiding. Wath Brow, Siestall, Fudges , and Kinsley Clubs were ad- mitted into membership. The Committee awarded 10 guineas to the family of the late Mr. Cuss, playing member of the Ripon Club. Holbeck was fined 70s. for a late start at Stockport on April 9. Cumberland County was awarded a sett on the Union Q.mmittee. It was decided to inform the Wigan Club that if the sum of £4 6i., due te Halifax for division of gates, was not paid before the next meeting extreme measures would result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WARRINGTON WORKS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>FINAL. VICTORY OF PEARSON AND KNOWLES.</p>
        <p>The Works Competition promoted by the Warrington Rugby Football Club was brought to a conclusion on Saturday when the final tie betwem Pearson and Knowles and Crosfield's was played off before 2,000 spec- tators. Miserable weather prevailed, heavy showers be- ing ad ded to a perfect gale of wind every few minutes. The Mayor and Mayoress were present,having consented to distribute the medals, and with them on the stand were Council \ors Evaos (pr 9sident of the club), J. Lee, Peocick,Joho Roberts, Burton and Tinnion. A loud cheer arose fronzthe.throatsiof the partizans of both teams as they emerged from the pavilion. Mr. Priestley (Brough- ton), was the referee appointed, but he failed to put in an appearance, and the whistle was given in charge of Mr. A. Jones. The teams were as follows : Pearson and Knowles : R. Hockenhull. full back :W. Sampson, D. Gibson, T. Hockenhull, and T. Moores, thresquarters; A. Forster arid Allen, half backs ; Wood- ward, Fre.eth, J. Cunningham, Hughes, Sudlow, Allen, Hands, and Williams, forwards. . .</p>
        <p>_ Crosfields : L)n,cfmore, full back; Elarris,Winstanley, Berry,and Wright,throequarters ; G. Woods and Dutton, half backs ; Leah,W. Ray, Burrows,Ml Woods, Hamp- ton, Hammersley' Lawton, and G. Dutton, forwards, _</p>
        <p>The wind was blowing across the ground and afforded neither side much a,sistance. Crosfield's won the toss and the Forge started. Dutton returning. Allen and Forster got away and entered the &amp;Japery half,but were driven back by strong forward play. Crosfield's now retaliated only to be spoiled by off-side tactics. A bad mistake by Wright lost them further ground,tut a mark by Harris proved of good service. The Soapery men got well up and Dutton nearly got through from Woods'pass. A nice passing, movement by Crosfield's resulted in Her. ris crossing the line. but Wins' anley has passed forward and the ,clrer was recslled. Lonemore failed to return a ount and the foremen rushed to the opposite end, Harris being hurt, but he was able to resume Play. T. Elockenhull got away cleverly, and passing by Gibson and Moores resulted in the latter opening the scoring with a clever try. No goal resulted. This roused Cros- fields' up and they pressed the Foremen back. Dutton spoiled several opportunities by sticking to the ball, when he should havo passed. On one occasion be had hard lines with an abortive drop at goal. A rush by the s•apery forwards was checked pluckily by W. Sampson, and at half time the Forge led by a try to nil. Urger- funately for Crosfield's the wind now veered round and they had to face the storm on resuming. Tais lot them their chance of equalising. Early on in this half Forster had a shot at goal from • mark and failed, and a strong dash down touch by Sampson was finely stopped by Longmore. Several plucky dforts to break away were made by Harris and Berry, but Moores proved a if.urnleling block in every case. Play Was very poor and tiers was no regret when time arrived. Pearson and Knowles won by I try (3 pointJ) to Croafields' nil. After the conclusion of the match the spectators all crowded to the stand,where the medals were distributed by the Mayoress. For the winners there were gold medals and for the runners-up gold-centre silver medals. They Nad been supplied by Messrs. Eustance and Co., and b3th sets were very handsome. The MLYOII said he supposed he would be expected to say a word or two. He was sorry they had not had a finer day for the final contest, and though he was not an enthusiastic footballer he had eejoyed the game thoroughly. He understood that there had been 20 clubs in the competition and he was pleased to learn that throughout the whole of it the games had been conducted in a friendly spirit which reflected great cre lit upon all who had taken part in them. (Hear, hear.) Ile was pleased to be able to say that the com- mittee of the Football Club had decided to hand a sum of £65 over to the Warriugton charities. Previously there had been some £4(10 or £5OO given to Warrington charities and he was proud that there was an institution in the town with such a kindly feeling to the charities and he hoped it would prosper. (Applause.) The prizes were then presented by the Mayoress to the 30 players c maposing the two teams. Councillor TINNION said he was sure all agreed with I him that the Mayor and Mayoress deserved a hearty vote of thanks for having been present on such a day. (Applause). He was sorry the weather had not been finer, as the charities would then have benefited to a greater extent. He was in full sympathy with these Competitions, and endorsed the spirit which had been shown throughout them, and begged to tender his sin- csre thanks to the Football Club, for the way they had taken the Competition up. They had in doing so, not only helped junior clubs, but they had helped the chari- ties of Warrington. He thought they were specially in- debted to the Mayoress for coming out in such terrible weather to present the medals, and help forward the object in view. It was only one more proof that the Mayor and Mayoress had given, that they were in full sympathy with everything for the good and welfare of Warrington. Councillor Jong RosztaTs seconded the proposition, and remarked that last year the Workingmen's Infir- mary Committee was delighted with the £33 from the Football Club, and was very thankful to receive such a lift. He hoped the sum would not be less this year, but whatever it was they would be extremely obliged to the Football Committee. The motion was carried with cheers, and the Mayor having, responded, the crowd dispersed.</p>
        <p>FUR.—SKIN RUGS Dressed. Cleaned, and Lined Fur alterations of every description. B. COOK ganufactnring Furrier, 21, Renshaw-atreet, Liverpool. Rs- tabightd 24 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>There was a fairly large attendance at the meeting of this society on Sunday afternoon. Mr. Thomas Haw- thorn presided, and in the course of a brief address he referred to tho recently-publislied auto-biography of Joseph Arch which he recommended his hearers to road. He proceeded to speak of Mr. Arch's strong faith in God, that He would help him to accomplish the work which he had been called to do on behalf of the agricultural labourers ; and also of the work which lay in the future of the young men of to-day to take up. The speaker for the occasion was Mr. Leybouro. ef Evertoo, who chose as his text "The King bath brought ma into His cham- bers." His remarks took the form of personal experi- euce and were of a most interesting description. Ho re- ferred to his career as a publican in charge of " The Elephant and Castle," and as a professional boxer, and to his ultimate conversion to a fol- lower .)f Christ. Mr. W. H. Wallington very effec- tively sang two solos: "The Los': Chord," and"Honour and Arma." The aervic. was also taken part in by Mr. S. Rowlands, and Mr. Haley,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE ASSOCIATION. THE COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP. FIRST ROUND. WARRINGTON CEMETERY V. CHEETHAM TEMPLE —The opening match of the Champions has certainly indicated tat the Warringtonlans intend making a bold bid to retain the honour they as ably succeeded in gaining last season. The first raatch in this year's tourney was played on the green attached to the Angel hotel, Stret- ford, on Saturday, and although there were several I names absent from the combination which did duty last year, the substitutes worthily upheld the trust reposed , in them, and at the coneluslon of the m tech Warrineton had gaieed the victory by the substantial margin of 50 points. The green was in excellent condition, and al- though prior to the match rain fell, the good qualities of the turf were shown ten minutes after the match had started, the woods running in a fashion which the skil- ful bowler readily appreeiates. The first quartette ended with honour" easy,for although A. J. Jones gained a substantial victory over his opponent after a game extending over 26 ends, W. Povey, after giving every hepe of running out an easy winner, failed to maintain his good form until the close, and his plucky opponent, Healey, gained a deservedly popu- lar victory by two shots. T. Bridge was another warriogtonian who " flattered only to deceive," for he failed after the 23rd end, and Wilson won by two points. That promising player, T. Cooper bowled in his usual skilful manner, but en the present occasion he met his match in Murphy. The players peeled repeatedly until at the 22nd end Murphy had a substantial run, which placed the issue beyond doubt, and the Cheatham player gained the fiat by three points. Thus when the next quartette tried conclusions honours were easy, but the Warrington supporters had implicit faith in those who followed, and that they had justification was evidenced by the results. U. Williams showed that he still retained all the skill which marked his expositions last season, and gained one of the most decisive victories of the atternoon, defeating his opponent, Clough, by no fewer than thirteen points. J. Ashton accounted far Holliegsworth by six points after a game extending now 26 ends, but it was a dineedone battle between E. Taylor and Whyett, the Warrington secretary gaining a capital victory by a single point, after one of:the most exciting finishes rf the afternoon. The game between J. Allis en end A'nolstencroft was also a close affair, and the players repasied each other during the 31 ends played with a pertinacity which raised the enthusiasm up to concert pitch. The bowling was of a h igh class nature, and the end saw the old medallist beaten by a single point. The Warringtonians thus en- joyed a lead of 19 when the last quartette entered upon their task. Few were prepared for the summary fashion in which the Warringtonians accounted for their op- ponents. R. Anderton, the popular skipper of the cham- pions, gave one of his best displays in defeating Feather- stone, and the issue was never in doubt after ten ends had been played. From that point Anderton trundled in brilliant style, and finally won by nine points. One of the surprises of the afternoon was the remarkable way in which E. Butterworch defeated Burns. The War- ringtonian had only been drafted into the team at the last moment, but nevertheless justified the confidence • placed in him by defeating his opponent by eight prints. I Warrington's sucvsses did not end here, for R. Rothwell never gave hie opponent a chance after the fifteenth , end, and ran out a winner by a dozen points. T. Ellison was well rewarded for a rare display of pluck, for after being in arrears by seven points at one period—the game • being called 19-12—he eecured the jack, and Toothill failed distinctly to cope with the skilful play of thd ! Warringtonian, who was loudly cheered for well-earnes victory by two points. The victory of 50 points WA indeed well-earned, and throughout the afternoon the trundling of the victors was consistent with that which brought them honours last season. Mr. H. Broady,</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Stretford, was an efficient referee. Score : WARRINGTON NORTH. CHEETILL3I TEMPLE. HPo vey T Bridge A J Jones 2l John Smith l4 .41 T Cooper ............... ........ 18 W Murphy lt H Williams 2l W Clough 8 1 J Allison B Taylor T Ellison J Wood 2l 3 Burns He • J Ashton lit Anderton 2l P Featherstone 11 Bothwell 2l W Greg (eapt) 9</p>
        <p>Majority 50 for Warrington. WILM9LOW v. SOLITE Warnurrerrou.—Wilrnslow ap• geared for the first time in a county competition ou Saturday,at Withington Midland,anclinaaed through the ordeal most encouragingly and with every prospect of securing a prize before the season has passed. Tied against one of the most skilled teams in the sister county Wilmslow put in seven wins,against five by their oppen• ents, and returned but one singlc-figure score. De. tails :</p>
