      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>Key West,which has now become as familiar to the newspaper reader as New York itself,is the most westerly of the Pine Group of Islands, and is comprised within the State of Fiorida. Like nearly all the other islands in the West Indian Archipelago it is of coral construction, is six miles long and two wide,and rises nowhere more ban about fifteen feet above the level of the sea. The city of Key West is picturesquely situated, and contains several handsome houses ana churches, Almost all the plants,as ha the Bahamas, grow with their roots outside the soil, only holding to the earth by one or two strong fibres, " coraline " being sufficiently nourishing in com- bination with the dews,whica are very heavy,to replace the richest soil. A huge orange tree may frequently be seen growing athwart a wall,the roots completely bare, clinging on either side to the cement of whichthe wall is constructed. The islai d has a population of about 5,000.</p>
        <p>It is a somewhat curious fact that Dr. Nausea in his address at the recent meeting of the Russian Geographi- cal Society, when he was cordially received by an audi ence including several Grand Dukes and Duchesses made use of the English language. In asking permis- sion to speak in English Dr. Nansen explained that he knew no Russian and was not very sure of his German As for French—the language cf the Court and of society in Russia,—Dr. Nansen does not profess to speak it with any degree of ease, so that he was compelled to fall back on English, which is now almost a second mother tongue with him. Honours of all kinds have been heaped on the distinguished explorer. Be has been made a member of the Russian nobility, has had the Order of St. Stanislaus conferred on hint by the Tsar, and is the first foreigner to whom the Russian Geogra- phical Society has presented its large gold medal. On leaving Russia Dr. Neilson intends, I understand, to take a holiday tour in the South of Europe before returning to his scientific labours at his Norwegian home.</p>
        <p>The "Daily Chronicle"printa the following as the views , of Mr. Rhodes, expressed in conversation on the subject of an Anglo-American entente :—"ln my opinion fawning is so vital to the future ot the British and American peoples as a cordial understanding now and a deeply rooted conviction of mutual goodwill. From this, if future events should render it necessary, a formal alli- ance may t)e born. It is obvious that if America crushes Spain from the outset there will be an unthinking tend- ency in this country to sympathise with the little boy who has been thrashed by the big boy. But what we must not forget is the fact of the atrocities perpetrated by Spain in Cuba. America, has done, in view of these, precisely what Great Britain would have done if they had occurred in an island close to her own coasts. In fact, we should probably not have been so patient as America has been. At any rate, the two peoples would meet the same circumstances with the same action. It is not for us to run after America with offers of alliance. Such a result must, of course, be a meeting half way. But it seems to me perfectly obvious that in any division of world-interests those of the British Empire and , America will be found united. You can't get over the fact that the two peoples are of the same race, and that the trite saying that "blood is thicker than water" is a true raying. England and America ne.id each othrr,and this need will grow rapidly in the future."</p>
        <p>Tbe war has already produced a couple of tragedies in the world of art, one of which will hit the Americans and the other the French. M. Salezi, the brilliant tenor of the Paris Opera,who had signed articles for two years in America which were to bring him £12,000,wi1l decline to go out. On the other hand, this year's Paris Salon will be deprived of an important picture by Ileraud. It was an American commission and had to be hurried away before hostilities began.</p>
        <p>What is the origin of the phrase "Blood is thicker than water," to which Mr, Cecil Rhodes referred the ether day and which is a good deal on Anglo-Saxon lips just now ? One historic occasion on which it was used was at the taking of the Taku forts, when our gunboat flotilla was very roughly handled by the Chinese batteries, and would have suffered more severely still but for the in- tervention of Commodore Tatnall, commanding some American vessels, who could stand passively by no longer, and, exclaimMg that blood was thicker than water, sect some of his blue jackets to the assistance of some of our wounded, if indeed he did not also wink at the endeavour of his ardent men to render us more effec- tive service still.</p>
        <p>John Carr, the U.S. Mail Carrier who has just com- pleted on foot a remarkable journey of 2,600 miles to and from Dawson City,says that the Skagway trail is in an awful condition,with hordes of men frantically trying to get forward ;it is like the retreat of a badly disci- plined army with the enemy in hot pursuit; murders are of daily occurrence,and no man's life or property is safe. Carr says the conditions there are a disgrace to the 'American Government, the disorder being confined to I that side of the line.</p>
        <p>We are told there is coining a wonderful electric floor- scrubbing machine which will do its work thoroughly in one-quarter the time,or at a saving of 75 per cent, over the old method of hand scrubbing. It is worked by an electric motor, which receives its current from a lamp cord, attached to any incandescent lamp socket that is handy. The brushes are held against the floor by means of spring pressure, and are geared with the motor so as to revolve at about 400 revolutions per minute. The wheels on which the machine rolls are rubber tired, and the apparatus can be pushed like a lawn mower. The operator has only to guide it and throw water on the floor. In place of the scrubbing-brushes, sandpaper blocks or blocks of stone may be used to dress down wood floors, the decks of vessels, or mosaic tiling.</p>
        <p>The distinction of being the most northerly white resi- dents in the world belongs to the Rev. Mr. Stringer, Mrs. Stringer, and the RSV. Mr. Young, formerly resi- dents of Teronto, and now missionaries supported by the frienas of Wycliffe College. They dwell on Herschel Island, which is believed to be the most northerly inhabited spot in the Queen's dominions. It is a bleak, desolate, treeless spot, ice-bound for nine months of the year, and lies just above the seventieth parallel of North latitude.</p>
        <p>Evidently nothing is sacred, sartorially, to the Ameri- can young lady. Having annexed her brother's " bowler " hat, his starched shirt front, his collar and his necktie, his jacket, and, for bicycling purposes, his i knickerbockers. her latest whim sto adopt his sleeping suit. According to the American fashion papers, the American girl, who thinks nothing of making a few nights' journey by train, declares that the masculine garment is far more comfortable and has fewer objec- tions than the more orthodox garment associated with the sex.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE NEW CANADIAN STEAMSHIP LINE.</p>
        <p>On April 8 last, we says the " Daily Chronicle," ex- clusively announced that a new Canadian steamship service was projected, and the line is now au fait accompli. The millionaire shipowner, Sir Christopher Furness, is one of the prime movers in the scheme, and the company will be shortly registered with a capital of £1,000,000, divided as to £500,000 in five per cent. cumulative shares,preferential as to capital and dividend and also £500,000 in ordinary shares. The scheme has received stront support, and from the lateet particulars, most, if not all, the capital has been found. Sir Chris- topher Furness has not only consented to be the manag- ing director, but has also agreed to take £150,000 ordin- ary shares for himself and friends or as much of the or- dinary shares as may not be applied for at the closing of the list. Of the shares only £175,000 preference and £175,000 ordinary will be offered to the public. Sir Christopher's offer to take all the ordinary shares, if the public do not, was conditional on other Manchester people taking an interest to the extent of £200,000. This amount has been found. The steamers for the service will be about 8,500 tons dead weight, capable of naviga- ting the canal, and of doitig twelve knots an hour. The ports to touch will be Quebec, Montreal and St. Johns, but in winter it is just possible that some Arne' ican cot- ton ports will be covered. Two directors of the Ship Canal Company, and mem- bers of the Manchester Corporation (Sir B. T. Leech and Mr. J. W. Southern), have recently been in Canada, and it is reported that these gentlemen have met with much succeAs, both from be Canadian Government and the Canadian Pacific Railway. The establishment of this cargo service is expected to help considerably in devel- oping the dry goods business of the Midlands, and a good deal of the West Riding trade it is hoped will b.i drawn to the new steamers.</p>
        <p>In consequence of an Urgent telegram from tie Admiralty, received late on Friday, the who'e of te stiff employed on the naval side of Portsmouth Gun Wharf were engaged throughout the , night (says the "Times ") in preparing and packing arm% These con- sisted of 1,000 Let—Metford rifles, with accessories, which were ordered to be despatched ty the first train Cu Saturday morning to Stirling for distribution amot.g the coastguard.</p>
        <p>The j,iners and carpenterslof Leigh, Tyldenley, Atherton, and surrounding districts have requested an increase in wages from 41. to 9. per hour, and a re- duction in working hours from 51 to 49it per week. The men, who number 166, were, failing these cencessions, to have struck work on Saturday, but the employer, sent a communication requesting the men's delegates to again meet them in a few days. They have agreed to do so.</p>
        <p>Sarah Rogers, the wife of a sawyer,was noticed stand- ing on the bank of tti: Regent's canal, near the Iron Bridge, Hackney, London, on Monday night. She was nursing her twelve menths old child, and it is alleged that she deliberately jumped into the water. The woman was rescued, but the child was drowned.</p>
      </div>
