      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>EAIILYSTOWN SOCIAL CYCLING CLUB •</head>
        <p>EAIILYSTOWN SOCIAL CYCLING CLUB •</p>
        <p>At the meeting of this club held at the headquar- ters Wellington Hotel, on Tuesday night, Mr. J. L. Evans presiding, the question of a cycle parade was broached, and after some discussion it was decided to proceed with the arrangements for a fancy dress parade some time in August.</p>
        <p>POLICE CAEIBS. , At the Police Station, Earlestown on Saturday morning before Mr. J.W. Emmett, the following , cases were dealt with.—Thomas Kelly of Burton- ' wo3d for being drunk in Market-street, Earlestown was fined 2. 6d. including caste.—Thomas Emer- son of Manchester for being drunk in High-st., Newton was fined 2i.6d. and costs—Mary. Clarkson of St. nelens for begging in Haydock-street was discharged on promising to leave the town.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN MISSION SCHOOL. On Sunday special services in aid of the above school were held in the Co-operative Hall, Earles- town. Sermons were preached mbrning and even- ing, to large congreqitions by Councillor Tanfield of Bradwell, and specials hymns and solos were I sung by the children and choir under the conductor- ship of Mr. S. Dring. Mrs. G. Bunting presided at the piano and Mr. E. Kenyon at the organ. The collections amounted to .E 6 Is 101. HIGH HONOUR FOR THE REV. JAMES LENNON. We learn from a Rome correspondent the, the Rev.Ja-nes Lennon,late Catholic rector of Newton-le Willows, and well-known throughout the diocese of , Liverpool, has been raised to the dignity of a do- mestic prelate to his Holiness, at the special request of the Rector of the Scottish College at Rome, and as a recognition of the worthy father's great gen- erosity to that institution. Father Lennon's many Liverpool and Newton friends will heartily con- gratulate the Right Rev. Monsignor on this marked expression of the Holy Father's appreciation of his life-long work and services. ' THE VISIT OF THE PRINCE OF WALES. For carrying out the arrangements in connection with the forthcoming visit of the Prince of Wales on the 17th inst. a Committee of the District Coun- cil has been formed consisting of Councillors Gil- mour, Patterson, Evans, Beatty, Simpson and Borron. It is proposed to decorate Church-street and High-street, Newton, and the decorations will be carried out by a Manchester firm. On the arri- val of His Royal Highness at Newton Bridge an address will be presented by the Council, but it has been distinctly laid down that the proceedings are to be purely formal, and that there will be no speaking. MERIT FOR A LOCAL CONSTABLE. • •</p>
        <p>_ We are very pleased to be able to announce that [ P.C. Bullen, who is stationed at Lowton has been awarded the merit badge, in recognition of his ser- vices in connection with a recent disturbance on his beat. Constable Bullen was very badly mauled in the melee, but although the odds were against him, he was successful in securing his man. The merit badge of course carries with it increased pay, and this will be some solatiurn to P.C.Bullen for the injuries he received. Before being removed to Low- ton he was stationed at Earlestown, where he was well thought of by the inhabitants. ANOTHER CHURCH CHOIR IN DIFFICULTY. Following on the dissension in the Wargrave Church choir, several of the male members of St. John's Church choir, have resigned their positions at the request of the Vicar. The difference has arisen through the members ef the choir being re- quested to stand in their places during the entry of the clergy, and turning to the East at the creed. Several of the members objected to the innovation, and refused to carry out the expressed wishes of the clergy. Consequently the Vicar has called upon them either to fall in with the arrangement or re- sign their positions, and they have decided to adopt the latter course.</p>
        <p>- - PRESENTATION TO A CHORISTER AT NEWTON. On Wednesday evening at the Congregational Schoolroom, Newton, occasion was taken of a social evening by the church choir to present Mr. Robert Owen, on the occasion of his recent marriage, with an enlarged photograph of the Congregational Church, a time piece,and a book of sacred song. as a token of their eeateem, and in recognition of his 16 years connection with the choir. The presenta- tion was made by Mr. A. Knight, the organist at the church, and congratulatory addresses were made by Mr. S. Duncalf, Mr. Pennington and others. Mr. Owen suitably responded and expressed his sorrow at having severed his connection with the choir.An excellent tea was partaken of by those present, and daring the evening, songs &amp;c., were given by mem- bers of the choir.</p>
        <p>- HOPE OF EARLIFESTOWN LODGES, 1.0.G.T. The above society held a successful meeting in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening under the presidency of C.T. Bro. T. Cutler. Two candidates were admitted to the Order and two were re- obligated and discussions arose dealing with the picnic. The evening being the quarter ending all the reports were read, the secretary stating that there were 118 members in good standing, and tak- ing the average of every meeting there were about 60 members present. Bro. J. Taylor then read the financial statement which showed a good balance in hand. The S.J.T., Bro. E. Newman,ga .e the report of the junior lodge which showed that there were 61 members. The election of officPr.. then took place and resulted as follows :—C.T. Bro. J. raylor; V.T. Sister Batton ; lodge secretary, Bro Barker; F.S. Bro. J. Wakefield ; treasurer, Bro. J. Warburton ; chaplain,Bro. J. Woods; marshal!. Bro. J. Whittington ; guard,Bro. Dutton ; eentinel,Bro.J. , Hatton. The meeting then closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY; AT EABLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY; AT EABLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>[By AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.] At one or two of the churches in this district the vestry meetings have been productive of dissensions between the parson and congregation. The most serious and lamentable dispute is at Haydock, where a certain section of the worshippers are at loggerheads with the vicar upon a question of ritual. Whether there is anything in their objection or not, is not my province to dwell upon, but it is a lamentable fact that the proceedings at the vestry, and at the church ser- vices since, were not conducive to devotion, and it is sincerely to be hoped that a way agreeable to all may very soon be found out of the difficulty. At St, John's Church, Earlestown, the dispute was of an entirely different character, and although ssme strong language was used, still there was not a scene anything approaching that at Haydock. At Wargrave the dissension is among the choir, the adult members of which decline to take part in:a certain portion of the service, owing to an innova- tion which they allege savours too muc'n of ritual- ism. In this case, the dissenting members have re- signed their positions, and now there are several vacant seats for males possessing Vocal abilities.</p>
        <p>--- The education question would appear to be at a complete deadlock so far as the outside public are concerned. There has been a meeting between one or two representatives of the largest ratepayers upon the subject, but what the outcome of the con- ference was f am not in a position at present to say. The strict sectarian basis proposed may be con- sidered abolished for it is quite on the cards that some of the largest ratepayers 'will refuse to con- tribute to the voluntary rate unless the suggested scheme of management is entirely remodelled.</p>
        <p>The re-opening of St. Peter's Church last week was attended with success gratifying to minister and congregation alike. The interior of the build- ing pieents a modernised appearance. and the elegant structure must certainly be a source of gratification to the family of the lo.to Colonel ' McCorquoiale,in whose memory the rebuilding has been carried out. All it rt quires now to make it one of the finest churches in We neighbourhood is the demolition of the old-fashioned belfry and the erection of a tower with a fine peal of bells. This can be accomplished by a handsome donation from some of our wealthy and generous residents.</p>
        <p>Many of our Catholic readers will rejoice to hear of ti e honour cqnferre.l upon the Rev. Jamr.a Lennon, who for many sears laboured zealously an the cause with which he was connscted in r'S ewton- le-Willows. As will be seen by a paragraph in another column,the Rev. Father has been elevated to the position of a domestic prelate to his Holiness the Fops at Rome. The Rev. Father Lennon retired from his position at St. Mary's and Sr. John's, New- ton, some few months ago, and it was thought had retired into private life, but it is evident his many years in harness have proved too attractive. aul be has been unable to completely sever himself from the cause he loved so much. He takes up his new position with the good wishes of all his Earlestown and Newton friends.</p>
        <p>A magnificent twin-screw-yacht of 1,560 tone ape on Thursday launched by the Fairfield Shipbuilding Com- pany of Govan, to the order of Baron Itothschild.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>lAAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 6 1-,98</head>
        <p>THE lAAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 6 1-,98</p>
        <p>NOTES ON €IEA AND</p>
        <p>Mr. R. G. HENSHALL afterwards moved that the best thanks of all preBetit be given to Mr. Monks and to the Mayor for the very valuable services they had been able to render the cause that afternoon. They might con- sider themselves vary fortunate in having secured the services of two such distinguished gentlemen, and he personally felt very much indebted to them, es did also all the Wealeyans or Warrington. (Applause.) Mr. Joni; TAYLOR said it afforded him very much pleasure to second the vote of Planks and be could fully endorse all that Mr. Ilenshall had said with regard to the Mayor and Mr. 'Monks. They were both public men. Mr. Monks was a gentlemen who was greatly beloved, while with regard to the Mayor, he had done a very graceful act that afternoon in coming amangt t them to take the chair. (Elear, hear.) • The motion having been heartily agreed to, The MAYOR briefly replied, and expressed the hope that the bazaar would be a great success. The Rev. W. Pores announced that the movement to hold a hazier was ' inaugurated some months ago by the late Dr. Starkey Smith, and there were two or three aubscriptions which ought to be annoenced /rem the platform. The late Dr. Smith gave them £4o—(applause)--knd they had sadly missed him, not Only on that occasion, but throughout the enterprise. Mr. Hightleld, of Aleore,had given them £lO, Mr. John Flartley £l.O, Mr. T. B. Carter £5, Mr. James D adds £5, Mr. E. I. Guest £2o,ansi in connection with various stalls, Mr. John Taylor £2O. Mrs.Willliam Silcock £2O,Mr. William Owen £5. (Applause.) The sale was then proceeded with. THE ORCHESTRA.</p>
        <p>Etch evening an orchestral band under the conductor- ship of Mr. Fred Pendlebury, gave a performance of classiest and popular music. Csasidering that the per- formers had few opportunities of rehearsing together, the music was given in a highly creditable instiller. and Mr. Pendlebury and the members of the orchestra are Jo be congratulated upon the success of their efforts. The mush; was greatly appreciated by all pres mt. At Tues- day night's proceedings the following programme was given :—Overture, "La Dladme " ; gavotte, "Cor- nelia " ; waltz, " Seasons" ; selection, " Darkey's Dream " ; waltz, " Ivy " ; selection, " Mikado " ; and following an interval—overture, " Bohemian Girl schottische, " Marie Stuart" ; waltz, " Dream Faces ; gavotte, " Immer Weider " ; and march, " Cornelius." 1 The gavotte" Cornelia." played after the first overture, is the composition of Mr. C. H. Pigot. , There was a slight variation of the proeramme on Wednesday and Thursday evenings. The members of the band were as follows : First violins, Mr. Clare. Mr. G. J. Allen, Mr. W. Brown, Mr. L. Knowles, Miss Winnie Pigot ; second violins, Mr. W. Brough, junior. Mr. Gorst, Mr. Osborne, Mr. A. Parkinson, Mr. H. Parkinson; flutes. Mr. W. Podmore, Mr. Osborne ; cornet, Mr. Ford ; bass•o, Mr. R. Whitfield ; violincello, Mr. W. Parkinson and Mr. J. Jackson ; oboe, Mr. Fred Davies; vista, Mr. G. John- son ; clarionet, Mr. Adcroft ; b..ritone, Mr. G. Lowe ; harp, Mrs. Fendlebury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ATHLETICS,</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS,</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The heading still remains, but there is very little to cemment upon in/ the domain of Rugby Football this week. There was no first-team match at Warrington, but a large amount of interest was shown in the final of the Works Competition between teams representing Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, and Messrs. Crosfield and Sons. A gale of wind and rain spoiled what would other- wise have been a fast game. Trio two teams infused plenty of energy into their play, but with the elements combined against them they were only able to give a disjointed exhibition of football'. They were exceedingly well matched and the final score of a cry to nil in favour of Pearson and Knowles just about represents the play. The only division in which I could see • hat the forgemen possessed any clear advautse e over their opponents was at half back, where they were admirably served by Allen and Forster. It is a coincidence worth noting that last year the final tie was also between ircn and soap, iron not unnaturally proving the stronger then, as on Saturday last. Crosfield's are to t e sympathised with in having twice reached the final and then been beaten on the poet. They should console themselves with the reflection that generally " the third time pays for all."</p>
        <p>The most interesting Rugby fixture in the north on Saturday was that betwem Oldham and a team picked from the Rest of the Competition. Some very fast play was witnessed on both sides,the scoring being alternate, and at the close each side rested with 8 points to their credit. This result is satisfactory. The Rest of the Com- petition would not have relished being beaten by Old- ham,and the Watershcdding crowd are so unaccustomed to defeat that to acknowledge inferiority even to tbe picked men of Lancashire and Cheshire would have been a bitter pill. Navlor,of Warrington, had a place among the forwards and displayed the dash and energy for which he is noted at Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>Oldham's record, as champion., is a fine one and will bear the scrutiny ot footballers everywhere. Jack Hurst is again top scorer with 23 tries to his credit,and the full statistical table is as fellows :—Matches played 40; won. 33.; lost 5 ; drawn, 2. Scores : Oldham, 50 goals 109 tries (427 points) ; opponents, 23 goals 37 tri3s (157 points). The 109 tries scorecf.were obtained by the fol lowing :—Hurst 23, Lawton 18, Marti,' 18, Taylor 10. Davies 9, E 'wards 7, Walsh 5, Willi arns 4, A. Lees 4, S. Lees 3, Frater 2, Telfer 2, Thomas, Hall, Merrell, and ifroome one each.</p>
        <p>It may be fully expected that the WarringtPn Com- mittee will have to find a few new players for the next season, and it is very imperative that they should not be too late in the field. There is by no mans an unlimited supply of crack players, and with the huge demand for talent delay on Warrington's part would he fatal to the best interests of the club. Of course I don't suppose anything will be done till after the annual meeting,when it is expected that the committee will be empowered to seek new talent. Other clubs are on the war path even now and last week the enterprising Oldham executive signed on two prominent Welshmen in T. Davies (three quarter back) and Illlor2.an Rees (half-back), Both these. players hail from Treherbert, and have kzond reputations in South Wales. In fact Davies is a centre for whom there has been much angling, and I fancy that therelwas knashing of teeth at Broughton when he capture leaked out. Rumour has it that one or two of the Rangers' officials are showing signs of gning pr-rea.turely grey with anxiety to net Tom Davies.</p>
        <p>The only League match on Saturday was at Leigh where the Broughton Rangers were defeated by 7 points to nil. Leigh have had a fairly successful season, as a glance at the table will show, and have improved, if only slightly, upon their last year's record. Uadoubtedly much of their success has been due to their exceptional strength behind the scrummage. Molyneaux has long been associated with their doings, and has done well to score ten tries, drop no fewer than seven goals, acd place a further seventeen. Even more remarkable, haw. ever, has been the success of his partner Dunbavin, who heads the list of try-getters with fourteen to his credit, and has dropped no fewer than a dozen goals, in addi- tion to having placed eleven. The record for the pair will take a lot of matching look where you will.</p>
        <p>Hunslet, at the finish, have had to fight hard for championship of the Yorkshire section, and the deciding contest between them and Bradford came off on Satur- day. kt half-time the Park Avenue men led by 2 points to nil, and until near the close maintained this lead, but a try by Ramage and a goal from the sure foot of Albert Goldthorpe gave Hunslet possession of the Shield. Brad- ford have certainly a claim to sympathy, for they have been in three finals within a fortnight,and failed in each —the Junior Shield, the Cup, and the Senior Competi- tion Shield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTB&amp;LL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTB&amp;LL.</p>
        <p>The chief event in local football under the Association rules was the re-played final to between Newton-le- Willows and Witton Albion.for the Warrington and Dis- trict Cup, A week before Newton secured the trophy with the assistance of an ineligible man,and on Saturday Witton reversed this decision by a goal to nil. It is dii- tinctly hard luck on Newton,after having fairly won the cup as they thought,to be beaten in a re-played match. A similar thing happened a fortnight ago, when Manches- ter City won the Cup they were fighting for, and they were ordered to re-play Stockport Countr, with the re- sult that the Cup has since gone to Stockport.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>BOWLINCi.</head>
        <p>BOWLINCi.</p>
        <p>In bowling, Warrington ar-p-p-sa-rs likely o carry off as many honours as last year. Wa.rrington Cemetery, the champions of the Lancashire and Cheshire Associatio,t, opened this season's competition on Saturday At Stret- ford with a handsome win of 50 points over Cheetham Temple. Same really fins play was witnessed, and the way the Warrington men pil.d on the agony at the close should cause a shiver of apprehension among the other contestants in the cornretition.</p>
        <p>South Warrington have also opened the season auspici- o9sly. their opponents being new aspirants for the cham- pionf.hip in Wilmslow. The latter gave the Warrinoton men an unexpectedly good game, and got within 12 of their nononeats total. But for Hackett's run-sway vic tory of 21-7, there might have been a different tale to tell.</p>
        <p>Orcurro. None but lunatics were to be found seeking pleasure awheel last Saturday, and as the Warrington Cycling Club doss not poses s any, there was no club run.</p>
        <p>A cyclist, at. Glavow, knocked a boy nver. Tho lad's mother saw the Itccid,•nt, and,te the rider's surprise, he euff:.ti the he: for estting: in the gentleman's WAr. The cuffing i 9 generally bestowed in the other direction.</p>
        <p>one of the best parers .tt down under." whom Platt-Betts has brought bark with him from Anstrslia the little Catfordian has a very high opinion of• This rider ha been engaged in the Palace string, and will,after s. little practice, takm the helm of one of the big machlors. fie Is a magnificently-built fellow, standinz gfr. 2m., and should b., a valuable acquiaition t.o the team. fie may also be trying his hand on a single later on.</p>
        <p>CRICKET. Roth the tirq and second elevens of IVarrineton were unable to play on Saturday, owing to the rain of Fri- day and Saturday. This ia to he regretted as the matchea between the Bolton and Warrington teams are amongst the most enjoyable of the soacon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>LtNCISHIRE FIXTURE% IE9I</head>
        <p>LtNCISHIRE FIXTURE% IE9I</p>
        <p>MitY 9. 10. and 11, Lancashire y M.D.O. and Ground.at Lord's May 12. 13, and 14, Lancashire v Hampshlre, at Southamp- ton. May 16 and 17. Lancashire v Sixteen of Bast Lancashire League, at Burnley. May 19. 20, awl 21, Lancashire v Leicestershire, at Manches- ter. May 23, 24, and 25. Lancashire v Derbyshire, at Derby. May 26, 27. and 28, Lancashire v Warwickshire. at Liverpool. May 30 and 31 and June 1 (Whit-Monday). Lancashire v Kent. at Manchester. May rand 31, Lancashire (Second Team), v Surrey (Second Team), at the Oval. June 2.3, and 4, Lancashire v Hampshire, at Manchester. June 6 and?, Lancashire (Second Team), v Staffordshire at Stoke June 10, 11, Lancashire (Second Team) v Durham. at Man- chester. June 13. 14, and 15. Lancashire v Warwickshlre. at Birming- ham. June 16, 17, and 18, Lancashire" Surrey, at Manchester. June 20, 21, and 22. Lancashire v Somersetehlre, at Taunton. June 23. 24, and 25. Lancashire v Gloucestershire, at Bristol. June 27, 28, and 29. Lancashire v Middlesex. at M,nchester. June 30 and July 1 and 2. Lmeashire V Notts, at Manchester. July 4. 5, and 6. Lancashire v Notts. at Nottingham. July 7. 8. and 9. Lancashire v Susses. at Manchester. July 11, 12 and 13, Lancashire v Yorkshire, at Sheffield. July 11 end 12. Lancashire (Second Team) v Staffordshire, at Manchester. July 14. 15, and 18, Lancashire v E3SOX. at Manchester. July. 21, 22, end 23, Lancashire v Leicestershire, at Leicester. July 26. 26. and 27. Lancashire v Somersetshire et Liverpool. July 23. 29, D. Lancashire v Gloucestershire. at Manchester. August 1,2. and 3. Lancashire v Kent, at Canterbury. August 1 and 2 Llncashire (Second Team) v Surrey (lecand Team) at Manchester. August 4. 5. and 8. Lancashire v Usetex. at D'Y'.ll. August. 11, 12, and 13, Lancashire v Yorkshire (Baker's benefit), at ,AanChester. August 15. IS. and 17. Lancashire v Sussex. at Brighton. August 18, 19, and 20. Lancashire v Surrey, at the Oval. August 22, 23, and 24. Lancashire v Middlesex. 