      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WESLEYkN BAZA.ALR AT</head>
        <p>WESLEYkN BAZA.ALR AT</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>A GREAT SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>Oue of the chief events in the town this week has been the Wesleyan Bazaar, held in the Parr Hall. Whatever may be said against bazaars they are un- doubtedly an excellent means for raising funds for religious objects, and they are projects in which ladies take a special interest. This was especi- ally the case in the bazaar under notice. For months east the ladies connected with Bold-street Wesleyan Chapel have been busily engaged in making and- collecting the materials which decked the stalls at the Parr Hall this week,and that the promoters had some confidence in the *access of their efforts may be judged by the expectation that £l,OOO would be realised. The objects in view were the removing of a debt of £7OO on Bold-street Church and Sunday School, and the raising of a further sum of £3OO tor the renovation of the vestries and the improvement of the premises behind the church. At the time of writing the total pre3eedB are not ascertainable but there sef3nis little doubt but that the whole of the money askisd for will be obtained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>DECORATIONS.</head>
        <p>THE DECORATIONS.</p>
        <p>The decorstion of the bazaar, as we stated last week, was entrusted to Mr. George M. Bridges,ef Kings Lynn, the well-known artist and b.ztar decorator. The artist has endeavoured Co represent old English abbeys and castlee.and in this he has succeeded in a remarkable de- gree. The pictures show a rich and harmonious b end- ing of col ,urs, and the effect is both unique and pleas- ing. No fewer than eight castles and abneys are de- picted on the scenery, and one of these was speciallF painted by Mr. Biadges for the bazaar. This was a scene renresenting the Vale of Tiotern and Tintern Abbey,and it formed a background to the orchestral platform.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>STALLS AND STALLHOLDERS.</head>
        <p>THE STALLS AND STALLHOLDERS.</p>
        <p>Within the abbeys and castles laeies waited upon the visitor intent on soiling all the beauteous wares with which their stalls worm la le a. The articles on sale were those amens- met with at bez tars, and they were ad- mirably arranged. The Sunday sehool and Weeley Guild stall was in the basement of Dryburgh Ahhey,whieh occupied a position to the left of the platform. It contained a great variety or fancy and ornamental articlea, and was in charge of Mesdames Barlow and Collin and Miss Bradbury, Assistants : Mesdames J. Parkinson,and C. H. Pigot ; Misses Ashdown, Barlow, Collin,and Lingard. At Raglan Castle there was an excellent assortment of goods offered for sale. Especially noticeable were a large quantity of handsome sideboard cloths, table centres, tea cloths and cushions. There was also an excellent specimen of Madeira wickerwork. Fancy linen work was to be seen in abundance, and a large quantity of small ornamental articles were tastefully arranged for sale. The ladies responsible for this stall were :—Mesdames Triggs, Prince, Williams, Fairhurst, Davey, Weaver, and Hickman. Assist- ants: Mesdames J. Bayley, Kent. Bennett (Clap. gates), Lunt. Clegg, J. Weaver, and Jones, and Miss Prince. Bran-pies : Marian and George Weaver, Maud and Reggie Davey, and Elsie Walton. The Chinn stall, at Chepstow Castle contained an admirable dieplay of Royal Worcester sod Dresden, while Mettlach ware, Bretby and numerous other exhibits also came under netice. The arrangement of this stall reflected credit upon Mrs. W. C. Nicholson who was assisted by Mrs. Allen and Mrs. IVhitehouse. Proceeding next to the Netley Abbey stall, a beautiful old oak chair with crocodile skin top was noticeable. A handsome rosewood music-stool was also on sale. For the rest there was a large assortment of fancy goods. A couple of paintings by Miss Gornell were also displayed. The ladies in charge were Mesdames R. G. Hensball, John Taylor, J. B. Isaac, cod W. J. Dunn and the Misses Ben- son, assisted by Mine,: aenshall, Taylor and Isaac. Greceries were offered for sale in the basement of Ear- lech Cattle, and three very capable saleswomen were found in Mrs. John Gandy, Mrs. J. T.Pattison,and Mrs. W. Isaac. Fountains Abbey was one of the moat im- portant stalls,and the articles offered for sale included two large etchings, handsomely mounted and framed. There were also some oil paintings by Mr. Pendlebury, of Lendon,and other paintings by Miss Bessie Dutton. A quantity of beautiful art needlework was also notice- able. The ladies in charge were : Mesdames Potts,S. B. Stringer, F. Pendlebury, Bodle, Chorley, and Robinson, Misses B. Dutton, Blackhurst, Laverock, and Potts. The refregernent stale Conway Castle,in charge of Mrs Toft was very charmingly decorated, and on it were tainty edibles in great variety, tastefully arranged. A well-managed tea room supplemented the refreshment stall, and there was, in addition, a commodious smoke room, where a cigar and cup of coffee might be enjoyed by gentlemen visitors. The tea room was under the Personal superintendence and management of Mesdames R. H. Silcock, and Harrison, and Miss Weaver; assisted by the Misses Lily Shaw Green, Emily and Morrie Ben- nett (Westland), Madge Silcock and Ethel Roberts. The flower stall was capitally arranged. It contained well-developed specimens of roses, geraniums and lilies, and a fine collection of orchids. In the beckgronad were arranged a number of handsome hot-house plants and ferns. This department was in charge of Misses Hick- man, Cox, Ciesie Pigot, Mollie Collin, Hartley, F. U. Laverock, and L. Pigot. THE OFFICERS OF THE BAZAAR. Much of the success of tha Bazaar is due to the cap- able officers who have been at the head of affairs. Special mention should be made of the exertions of the Rev. W. G, Nees and Mr. R. H. Sileeck, the two hon. secretaries, upon whom much of the work fell. It is to be regretted that Mr. Silcock should be incapacitated through il'-h ankh, from ateeeling the bazaar after hav- ing se largely shared in the preparatory work. The officers were : President, Rev. William Potts ; hon. treasurer. Mr. J. B. Isaac ; bon secretaries : Rev. W. G. Triggs, Mr. R. H. Silcock. Committees : Printing and Advertising Committee : Messrs. S. G. Davey, John Fairhurat, R. G. Henshall, W. Hickman, J. B. Isaac, F. Peudlebury, R. fl. Silcock, W. Silcock, S. 13. Stringer, John Taylor, George Weaver. Cloak-room Committee: Messrs. It Dale, A. Horrocks, A. G. Juub, W. C. Nichol- son, J. T. Pettison, George Perkins, 8. B. Stringer, Parcels Committee : Messrs. Bodle, S. G. Davey, W. J. , Dunn, W. Laac, T. Prince. Entertainment Committee: Messrs. W. Breugh, P. Harrison, F. Hickman, J. Park- inson, W. Parkinson, F. Pendlebury, C. H. Pigot, Alfred Silcock, S. B. Stringer.</p>
        <p>THE Ot'ENING CEREMONY. About two o'clock on Tuesday afternoon the bazaar was formally opened in the presence of a large company. Amongst tho' e present were the Mayor and Mayoress (Mr. and Mrs. E. Wright), the Rev. W. Potts (super- intendent of the circuit), the Revs. G. Harbottle, W. G. trigs, and J. Gunnell (ministers of the circuit), the Revs. W. H. Drummond, Benjamin Weaver, T. F. Ntattbews, J. Warnock, C. Finch, J. T. Schofield ; Coun- cillors Shaw Green, F. R. Roberts and Arthur Bennett ; and Messrs. F. Monks. J.P., C.C., S. G. Davey. Elwin Richardson, R. G. Henshall, J. B. Isaac, E. Brown (Lymm), W. H. J. Jenkins,John Fairhurst, James Sum- tnersgill. George Weaver, John Hartley, C. E. Parker, Pattison, W. C. Nicholaon, T. B. Carter. R. W. Collin, Elijah Place, F. Pendlebury, S. B. Stringer, George 1101- lord, W. Benson, S. Billings (Haydock), John Gandy, Isaac Mills, P. S. Smith, and others. The opening proceedings were conducted from the platform on which the Mayor and Mr. Monks were accompanied by the ministers present, and several gentlemen prominently connected with Wesleyaniem. After the singing of a hymn The Rev. W. Pons said he gave the Mayor a hearty welcome in their name, and asked him to preside over the meeting.</p>
        <p>His WORSHIP then called upon Vac Rev. John Gunnell to offer prayer. The MAYOa is calling upon Mr. Monks to op,m the biztar, said he would not detain the audience for more than a moment or two, but he wished to say that he appeared among them with very great pleasure. When the inembe.s of the committee asked him if he would accept the position of chairman he had no hesitation in consenting to do so, because he believed they were en. gaged in a noble work. He was in perfect sympathy with all they bad met together to do. For many months past loving hands and hearts had been hard at work in preparation for that day, and his sincere wish was that they might obtain the sum they required—which, he believed, was £l,OOO —before the close of the bazaar. He was very pleased indeed to hear from two of his friends preserrt that at the anniversary services held on Sunday the sum of £43 14s. was raised, and that, he thought, was very creditable to all concerned. In con- clusion, he wished them all every p )ssible prosperity and no v ilAli much pleasuse in asking Mr. Monks to open the 11ziar. (Applause.) Mr. Meants, who was cordially received, remarked that wh,:i the deputation asked him to perform that ceremony he confessed he felt a great deal of hesitation, but he could not help the reflection that, after all, they were progressing, for he remembered the time when he doubter] very much whether any Wasleyan would have the courage to ask a Unitarian to take part in any fuuc- Son whatever (Laughter,) But, happily, those times had passed. They were broadening out into wider liberties, which they appreciated, and they were under- stailding mire arid mere what liberty really meant. He hoped they would all be able to work together in that eligiour in 11.,1111'1,V: without introducing a•iy tgeolozic al Opinions. They would all vary, and he did not know that he walk! even like his wife to think altogethcr as he did. A little 'variety at any rate helped things on, and in the seeking after trath, which he was very clad to see was the vocation of many of their very best minda, not the least of whom was still living at the ripe ago of 93, and was still giving to the weed the inspiration which came through him—all these movements were tending in the right direction. putting them on a firmer foundation of religious truth. (Applause.) Lie knew that it W.4,1-liOt the wish of the young ladies particularly that the time should be Spent in speechmaking, and having regard to them and to their %reciting he thought he could net do better than briefly say that no declared the bazaar open. He hoped it would receive all the support which they all expected and had been working for. (Ap- t this stage of the proceedings. Mr. James Parkinson, of ankey-street, took a photograph of the hatereating nom</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>CONCERTS.</head>
        <p>THE CONCERTS.</p>
        <p>It is unnecessary to say that music took no secondary I Dart in the various attractions of the bazaar. Concerts in a room upstairs were held,under the able direction of Mr.T. Hickman,and Mr. P. Harrison. The first took place at 7.30 p.m. on Tuesday evening. 4 pianoforte „lo by Miss F, Toft, was highly creditable. A song by Miss L. Mason was exceedingly well received, and deserved all the praise it received. Two dances from " Henry Vlll."were rendered on the violin by Miss Winifred Pigot. shewing both skill and executive ability. Another song by Miss L. Mason was equally well received. Mr. F. Hickman made a very efficient and sympathetic accom- panist. The Cereza Guitar and Mandoline and under the leadership oi Miss Milne,of Fro Isham, contributed their efforts to the acknowledged success of the concerts this evening. The members of this excllent orchestra viz :—• Mis G. Allm, Miss L. Allen, Miss A. Atkinson, Miss I. 13ailev, Miss F. Carlisle, and Miss E. Carlisle, were tricioubtedly clever per- formers. They rendered se!ections and were dressed in gipsy appropriate costumes. It would be invidious to mention any names in particular. The different Selec- tions given displayed both skill and finish. The concert at 9.0 p.m. was rather better attended. A song contri- buted by Mr. W. H. Walling,ton,and a humorous reading by Mr. H M. Ashton were well received. A violin sole by Miss Winnie Pigot showed a high degree of training and was well appreciated. Mr. W. H. Payton as accompanist displayed his well-known ability. The concerts were also well patronised on Wednesday and Thursday evenings. An entire change of programme was submitted each evening, and on the whole the con- certs were very successful. The vocalists on Wednes- day evening were Mrs. D. S. Stone, Miss Brough, Mr. George Gough, and Mr. A. Fieldhouse, and they secured hearty receptions. Mr. George Whitfield contributed humorous pieces, and the instrumental selections in- cluded a vicalin solo by Mr. Allen, and a pianoforte solo by Miss Pigot, The accompanists were Mr. B. Wads- worth.and Miss Pigot. Thursday evening's concerts in- cluded solos by Miss Woodward, Mr. W. Woods, Mr. Oilier, Mr. W. Critchley and Mr. D. Fanshaw,and a duet by Mr. George Perkins and Miss Perkins. Mr. H. M. Ashton was again very successful with his humorous readings. The other items on the programme included pianoforte solos by Miss E. Bennett,and Mr.G. Hoffer&amp; The accompaniments were played by Miss Edith Bennett, and Mr. Frank Hickman. The receipts from the concerts were very satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ENTERTAINMENTS.</head>
        <p>THE ENTERTAINMENTS.</p>
        <p>Quite a number of—ast-r-a-Cii-o-Ws—in—ilWform of side shows were provided, and with one or two exceptions they were a great success. Oen of tho most amusing was the hat trimming competition far gentlemen only, which caused roars of laughter. The competitors were each supplied with a shape and trimmings. also needle and thread. At the word of command they at once proceeded to business, and 15 minutes were allowed for the completion of the task. A prize was awarded the most successful result. This department was under the management of the Misses Bilebrough, Sherlock, and Waring. An autograph quilt was also an unique exhibit. It contained the signatures of about a thonsand people*. The idea was first suggested by an old scholar and teacher, and was carried into effect by several ladies of the conoregation. The payments for the signatures realized VS. Eight signatures appeared on each square, and there were 120 squares, The quilt attracted considerable attention.and was much admired. It was under the care of Mrs. Fairhurst. In the whistling competition, soma of the competitors did not put in an appearance, and it was therefore not as succeasful as it might otherwise have been. A spelling be competition in charge of the Rev. W. G. Triggs, was on the other band, a success. The same remark applies to an impromptu streaking con- test. Here the competitors chose their subject, from a number in a hat, and had to commence speak-. log at once. As may be readily supposed the auditors were greatly entertained. From the opening to the close of the bazaar Miss Gertrude Shaw Green, an amateur palmist, held con- sultations in a gipsy tent. Very diverting entertain- ments were also given by Signor Bertoasconi, Mr. G. M. Midges' manager. These attracted large audiences.who seemed to greatly appreciate the clever artiste's perform- ances. Large numbers flocked to see what the pro- gramme described as the " only living representation of an Egyptian mummy, fresh from the catacombs of Egypt, and brought over at considerable tremble and expense." Signor L. Romana exhibited portraits in natural colours, and an art gallery was a revelation to all who attended. WEDNESDAY'S OPENING PROCEEDINGS. SPEECHES BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD AND COUNCILLOR H. ROBERTS. There was again a good attendance when the second day's proceedings commenced on Wednesday aft( rnoon. Councillor H. Roberts praeidedan the abeence of Mr. E. J. Guest ; and the Rev. John YongP, who had been an- nounced to take part in the opening ceremony, was un- able to be present. The Rev. George Harbottle gave out the hymn and offered prayer, after which The Rev. Was. Parrs explained that Mr. E. J. Guest was too unwell to be present.and he had received a letter from him explaining his absence which he would pre- sently rem,. Councillor Roberts had kindly consented to take his place. (Applause.) Mr. Potts added that I he made an omission the previous day in the announce- I merits, namely, that Mrs. and the Misses Taylor, of Horsernarket-street, had contributed £5 in connection with the Raglan stall. He had also to apologise for the absence of Mr. R. H. Sitcock, one of the secretaries, who had rendered yeoman service in connection with the bazaar. Na one had worked harder among the gentle- men than he had done, except his (the speaker's) col- loague,Mr. Triggs. Mr. Guest had forwarded them £2O (Applause.) They were pleased to receive the money. but they missed him,and were sorry at the cause of his Ab9etlef.. (Hear, hear.) At the same time, that would not. diminish the heartiness of the welcome they were prepared to give to Councillor H. Roberts. There was no one they could ask who would more 1 worthily fill the position of chairman, while with regard to Mr. A. H. Crosfield big great ability and generosity were well-known. (Anplause-) Mr. Potts then read the following letter from Mr. Guest: —" Oak Mount. Helsby, Warrington, 2nd May, 1898. Rev. W. Potts, Dear sir,—The effects of the influenza I had a fortnight since do not leave me, and I must have a change for a few clamor my work will fall hopelessly into arrears. I must ask you kindly to excuse me from takin the chair on Wednesday. It is a disappointment to me, as I should have liked to introduce Mr.A.H. Crop eeld,although, indeed, he needs no introduction to a Warrington audience. His great ability and generosity are well known. Warrington is not fortunate in the noble deeds and gifts of rich men. With some few noble excep- tions the town has not had rich benefactors. Amongst those'exceptions I rank the Crosfield family asperhaps the most genernus. The Town Hall and Park are monuments of George Cromfield's kindness in great part. I esteem John Croslield as one of the most generous-hearted men we have, The Mission School at Bank Quay and the People's College proclaim his unselfish support of re- ligion and elementary education. But these are only indications of his readiness to help any good cause. No man in Warrington is more approachable than he when help is needed. We trust his son Arthur will carry nn the traditions of his family to even wider and nobler issues. Personally I should have liked to be with you because the honour you have conferred 013 me is</p>
        <p>due to the fact that my fattier was associated with George Dickenson, Dr. Smith, Mr. Garnett, Mr. Dutton, Mr. Hadfield, Mr. Sanderson, Mr. Carter, and many others in the erection of Bold-street Church. My early religious convictions were e'egiun and fostered in this church, and Me sympathies with "he church are indis- soluble. Circiamstances which I could hot control obliged me to leave the cireuit, but I hoped when I left to be Able to keep up my connection with the church. But every Methodist knows that if he continues faithful to his early convictions he will be celled upon to do his ut- moat in the circuit in which he hies. And I have found this to be the case in the Frodsham circuit. But,it is the bounden duty of all old friends to come up to the help of the choral of his earlier days. The need of 'Bald-' street church is caesed by the fact that so many of those who did help in former days hive either been called over to the great majority or have left the circuit. lam glad In this hour of need to do what little I can, and should have liked to be more closely identified with you. With kind regards, yours faithfully, E. I. Guest" Councillor H. Roberta, who was cordially received, said that he, along with themselves. was very sorry at the cause of Mr. Guest's absence. Mr. Guest was to him. as he wee to most of them, a personal friend, and a man whose thoroughness to the convictions of his early days had made him generally and universally respected. He was sorry on their account he was not present that day, but he (the Chairman) was very glad indeed to have an opportunity , as a member of a neighbouring and sympathetic church,of expressing the warm interest they had at Wycliffe Church in the success of the present undertakings They were pleased to know that yester- day's efforts got together the very large amount of £5OO. It seemed to him at aay rate that that was a pretty safe augury that with the other day' e salee the objects for which the bazaar was being held would be satisfactorily accomplished, if not exceeded, lee was pleased to be present for another' reason. He, as they knew, was a Congregationalist or, as he preerrel to be called, an Independent. He was one of those who thought that if one's convictions ssrengtherred one's early training, no circumstances, whatever their nature might be, should be allowed to induce him to stray away from them. (Hear, hear.) But he happened to live in a district where they had not a branch of their own church, and where he did the next best thing—he went to the Wesleyan chnrch, when he was either too Ism- or circumstances were not favourable for a two-mile walk into town. (Laughter.) And he must say that he felt indebted for the religious hospitality which he and his family bed received on numerous occasions in the little Chapel at Latchford. They got all mess of preachers there—he did not mean anything in- vidious ; he simply meant that some of the preachers were better than others. (Laughter.) They could not expect all men to be equal, but at the same time the variety and the different character of the , interpretation of the Scriptures were interesting in themselves ; he was going to say that sometimes the in- terpretation was entertaining, if not universally profi- table. And in taking the chair that day, which he did at short notice, he felt that he was in some degree repay ine the debt which he owed, and which he was not ashamed to owe, to Wesleyan Methedism. (Applause). He was ale. glad to be present for another reason. He was proud to be associated with any movement which had for its object the strengthening and furthering of Nonconformity in Warrington. and of Wesleyan Metho- dism, if they liked, in particular. The other part of his duty was an exceedingly pleasant one, and that was to formally introduce to the meeting Mr. Arthur H. Cros- field. As Mr. Guest had said in his letter,nn introduc- tion was really necessary in the case of Mr. Crosfield, I whose personal friendship he was proud to claim the honour of. He esteemed it a great privilege to perform that function on his behalf that afternoon. The name of Crosfield had become in many sense.s, and certainly in the domestic sense, a household word in Warrineton —(laughter)—and essociated as it had been for many years with the material, social, and moral progress of the town many of them had come to believe that it had very nearly by this time approached perfection. (Re- newed laughter.) Mr. Crosfield came of a good stock, and if it were not out of place in a Wesleyan bazaar, he would say in the words of the play " he is one of the best of 'em." (Applause.) Some of them were perhaps vain enough to hope that in years to come he would occupy a more prominent and a more impertant posi tion than he did now. slicer, hear). He would now ask Mr. Crosfield to declare the bazaar open. Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, who was very heartily reeeived, said he wished to thank the committee for their kindness in inviting him to open the bazaar that day. He got the invitation from his friend Mr.. Henshall when he was enjoying the sunny climate of Cannes, in the South of France, and he wrote to Mr. Henshall telling him that he thought the invitation was a very kind one indeed. At the same time, he only wished he had obtained the services of some one who was more au fait with this kind ot work, because this was the first bazaar he had ever had to open. He could not say that this was the first bazaar he had ever had any connection with, in fact he should very much doubt if there was any one in the hall who had had more to do with bazaars than hes had had. (Laughter.) There was a sort of prevalent misconception abroad whieh led people to believe that every soap man was a gold man. He thought the very first thing that was done by the authorities who inau- gurated these bazaars throughout the country was to send a round robin to every soapmaker in the coun- try, at any rate he could' assure the audience that their manufactures from Bank Quay were con- stantly travelling from John O'Groat's to Land's End and considering the objects for which bazaars were generally started—for the support of the churches and chapels—if there was anything in the old saying that cleanliness was next to godliness, he did not know that their contributions were altogether out of place. (Laugh- ter.) He certainly must confess on coming into the room that he was gratified and struck with the pleasing effect of the whole exhibition. There were some sages of the century who were accustomed to inveigh against the ugliness of modern industry ; it was only the literal truth to call this an industrial undertaking, and it was a pleasing example of the tendency in these days to make the best of their circumstances and oppertunities, and to do things in good taste and style. He should like, on behalf of the gentlemen around him, and the others in the halt, to express their acknowledgements to the ladies who had, he know, had so murh to do with the pleasing effect which had been produced, Their efforts deserved every success, and from what Mr. Roberts had just said as to the result of the first day's sale,he thought they might lock forward to beating the record. He certainly hoped they would do an, and in order that they might achieve those results be had better not waste too much time. He had therefore much plea- sure in declaring the bazaar open for the second day. (Applause.) The Rev. W. G. TRIGGS in proposing a vote of thanks to the Chairman, and Mr. A. H. Crosfield, said he would like in the first place to state that he had received a letter from Mr. J. Charlton Parr, stating that Mrs. Parr was sending a few flowers, and he took that oppm tunitv of sending £5 with his best wishes for the success of the bazaar. (Applause.) He had also to acknowledge an anonymous donation of £5 from a gentleman who heard , him preach a sermon a little while ago on not allowing your right hand know what the left hand doeth. (Laughter)* Personally he regretted the absence of Mr. R. Silcock, who had worked energetically for the ibazaar, and he felt he had lost a most efficient colleague. In proposing that their heartiest thanks he tendered to Mr. Roberts and Mr. Croefield, he stated that Mr. Roberts was known to them better than he was to him. He (Mr. Triggs), was only a soujourner amongst them but he had not been living in Warrington so long without knowing that Mr. Roberts was a great moral , force in this Borough, and he should think frem what he could gather that he was going to he a greater one. (Hear, hear.) So much had been said concernine Mr. Crosfield in Mr. Guest's note, and from the Chairman and Mr. Pots that he need not add anything in com- mending that vote of thanks for their hearty approval. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Joum FAIRHURST said it WEIS hie pleasinz duty to second that vote of thank. Ho hoped they would be still more successful that day than on the previous day. (Hear, hear.) The resolution wag c .ried unanimously, and the Chairman re;ponded ea behalf of Mr. Crosfield and him- self. The sales then commenced. THE RECEIPTS.</p>
        <p>The proceeds at the bazaar nn Tuesday were £504 11a 8:1 ; on Wednesday £265 51 101 ; Thursday £221 15s 9d. This brings the total for the three days to £991 13s 3i. When the annnuncement was made on Thursda, evening it was received with loud cheering.</p>
